Abarbaree,f,Ἀβαρβαρέη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/a.bar.ba.ré.ɛː/(Attic Greek)|/a.βar.βa.ˈre.i/(Koine Greek)|/a.var.va.ˈre.i/(Byzantine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀβάρβαρος (abarbaros), ἀβόρβορος (aborboros) meaning ""without filth"", itself from α (a), a negative prefix, and βόρβορος (borboros) meaning ""mud, mire, filth"". This was the name of two naiads (nymphs) in Greek mythology."
