Acindynus,m,Ἀκίνδυνος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Akindynos. St. Acyndinus was a Persian Christian and ordained priest. He was arrested and slain for the faith during the reign of King Shapur II.
Acintya,m,,Indonesian Mythology,,,,,,,,"From Sanskrit अचिन्त्य (acintya) meaning ""unthinkable, inconceivable, incomprehensible"". This is the name of the supreme deity in Balinese Hinduism, also known as Sang Hyang Widhi Wasa (""the Divine Order"") and Sang Hyang Tunggal (""the Divine Oneness"") in Balinese."
Acis,m,Ἄκις(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˈeɪ.sɪs/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Ἄκις (Akis), from the name of the Jaci or Aci river on the east coast of Sicily, now destroyed by lava flow from Mount Etna. In Greek mythology, Acis was the lover of the Nereid Galatea, who turned him into a river after he was killed by the jealous cyclops Polyphemus."
Aciscla,f,,"Spanish (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Acisclo.
Acisclo,m,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Hispanicized)|Spanish (Rare)",/a.ˈθis.klo/(European Spanish)|/a.ˈsis.klo/(Latin American Spanish)|/a.ˈsik.klo/(Cordoban Spanish),,,,,,,Spanish form of Acisclus.
Acisclus,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Possibly from Latin asciculus meaning ""adze, stone hammer"", itself a diminutive of ascia (""axe""). This is the name of a Hispano-Roman saint of the 3rd century BC in Cordoba (Spain) who was martyred in the Diocletianic Persecution along with his sister Victoria. He is the patron saint of the Spanish city of Cordoba."
Ack,m,,Scots,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Alexander.
