Aeghte,f,,East Frisian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Aegiale,f,Αἰγιάλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αἰγιάλη (Aigiale), derived from Greek αἰγιαλός (aigialos) meaning ""beach, shore"" (also see the masculine form Aigialos). In Greek mythology Aegiale was the daughter of Adrastus and Amphithea. She married Diomedes but was unfaithful to him during his absence."
Aegialeus,m,Αἰγιαλεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αἰγιαλεύς (Aigialeus), which is derived from Greek αἰγιαλός (aigialos) meaning ""beach, sea-shore"". Also compare Greek αἴξ (aix) meaning ""a tall wave"". In Greek mythology, this is the name of a son of the river god Inachus, as well as the name of a son of king Adrastus of Argos."
Aegidia,f,,Late Roman,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Aegidius.
Ægileif,f,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"The first element Ægi- may be related to Old Icelandic Ægir, ""the sea"" or ""the god of the sea"", found in compounds as ægisandr ""sea-sand"" or the Icelandic place-name Ægisiða. Alternately, it may be related to Old Icelandic ægja, ""to frighten, make terrible"", found in compounds such as ægilikr, ""terrible, awful"". The second element -leif, -læif or -lof is a feminine name element derived from Primitive Scandinavian *-laibó a formation from the stem in OW.Norse leif ""inheritance, legacy"", but as an element in personal names ""one who comes after, heir"" and thus to -læifR/-lafR."
Ægill,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Old Norse variant of Egill.
Aeginetes,m,Αἰγινήτης(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ai̯.ɡiː.nɛ̌ː.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/ɛ.ʝi.ˈni.tis/(Koine Greek)|/e.ʝi.ˈni.tis/(Byzantine Greek)|/ae̯.ɡiː.ˈneː.teːs/(Classical Latin)|/e.d͡ʒi.ˈne.tes/(Ecclesiastical Latin),,,,,,,"Means ""inhabitant of Aegina""."
Aegir,m,,Astronomy,/ˈaɪ.jɪər/|/ˈæ.ɡɪər/|/ˈiː.dʒər/|/ˈeɪ.jɪər/,,,,,,,"Anglicized form of Old Norse Ægir. This is the name of one of Saturn’s moons, as well as an exoplanet (also known as Epsilon Eridani b) orbiting the star Epsilon Eridani, both named for the figure in Norse mythology."
Aegis,m,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"From the mythological device called the Aegis. In the Iliad, the Aegis is a device worn by Greek gods Athena and Zeus, resembling an animal skin or a shield and sometimes bearing the head of a Gorgon."
Aegje,f,,East Frisian,,,,,,,,Variation of Aeghte.
Aeglanor,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aiglanor.
Aegleis,f,Αἰγληίς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αἰγληίς (Aigleis), a derivative of αἴγλη (aigle) meaning ""radiance, splendour, light of the sun or moon"" (compare Aegle). In Greek mythology this name belonged to one of the Hyacinthides, the four daughters of Hyacinthus the Lacedaemonian, who was said to have moved to Athens and, in compliance with an oracle, to have caused his daughters to be sacrificed by the Athenians on the tomb of the Cyclops Geraestus for the purpose of delivering the city from famine and the plague, under which it was suffering during the war with Minos."
Ægli,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Old Norse variant of Egill.
Aegnor,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Sindarin form of Aikanáro. In the works of J. R. R. Tolkien, Aegnor is an Elf, the brother of Finrod, Galadriel, Angrod and Orodreth. He fell in love with the mortal woman Andreth."
Aegocerus,m,Αἰγόκερως(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Meaning ""goat-horned"".An epithet of Pan descriptive of his figure with the horns of a goat."
Aegon,m,,Literature|Popular Culture,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Germanic element agjō ""edge of a sword"" and the Greek word αγώνας (agónas) ""struggle"". This is the name of multiple characters in George R. R. Martin's 'A Song of Ice and Fire' series, as well as the TV show based on the books 'Game of Thrones'."
Aegyptius,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/ae̯.ˈɡyp.ti.us/(Latin)|/äe̯.ˈɡʏp.t̪i.ʊs̠/(Latin),,,,,,,Latinized form of Aigyptios.
