Ahiqar,m,,Ancient Aramaic,ah-HEE-kar(Aramaic),,,,,,,"From the Aramaic name hyqr meaning ""My brother is precious"".The Story of Ahiqar, also known as the Words of Ahikar, is a story first attested in Aramaic from the fifth century BCE that circulated widely in the Middle and Near East. It has been characterised as one of the earliest 'international books' of world literature.This name appears in the deuterocanonical Book of Tobit, where he is a relative of Tobit who becomes a prominent administrator in the court of king Sennacherib."
Ahira,m,אֲחִירַע(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,ah-ee-rah(Biblical English)|ə-HIE-rə(English),,,,,,,"Means ""my brother is evil"" in Hebrew. In the Bible, Ahira was a son of Enan. He was the chief of the tribe of Naphthali at the time of the census in the wilderness of Sinai."
