Aiulf,m,,Lombardic|Medieval English|Anglo-Norman,,,,,,,,"A Germanic name formed from the name elements agin ""edge (of a sword)"" and wolf ""wolf"" (see Aginulf).As a medieval English name, it could either be a Scandinavian Norman name derived from Eyiólfr (or Eyjólfr) or a contraction of Old English Æðelwulf."
Aiuri,,,Basque (Modern),/a.ˈju.ɾi/,,,,,,,"From Basque ainuria or aiuria meaning ""howl""."
Aius,m,,Roman Mythology|Ancient Roman (Rare),[ˈäː.i.ʊs̠](Latin),,,,,,,"From Latin āio meaning ""to say, affirm"", cognate to Etruscan 𐌀𐌉𐌖𐌔 (aius) meaning ""omen, tell, response"". Aius Locutius (meaning ""spoken affirmation"") was a Roman deity or numen associated with the Gallic invasions of Rome during the early 4th century BC."
