Akkübey,f,𐰔𐰝𐰅𐰎(Orkhon Inscriptions)|Ак Күбэй(Yakut),Turkic Mythology,/ak.ky.beɪ/,,,,,,,"Ak Kübey is traditionally interpreted as a celestial feminine entity within Turkic mythology, symbolizing the protector of innocence and the nurturer of souls. It is derived from Ak (""white,"" ""pure,"" ""sacred"") and kübe/kübey (a term linked to kübe, meaning ""vessel"" or ""womb,"" and küde-, ""to guard/protect""). She represents the divine vitality of the World Tree, Ulu Kayın. Unlike mere deities, she is the silent pulse within the trunk, nourishing the primordial man Er Sogotoh with sacred life-force#. While some folklore connects her to Kayra Han, she operates as a distinct manifestation of primordial maternal creation, symbolizing the bridge between celestial purity and earthly life."
