Alal,f,,Kurdish,,,,,,,,"Possibly from the Kurdish al meaning ""banner, flag""."
Al-'ala',m,,Arabic,,,,,,,,"Means ""the exalted"". Laqab of Ala 1. This was the personal name of Abu Sa'd al-'Ala' ibn Sahl (c. 940–1000), a Muslim mathematician, physicist and optics engineer. Ibn Sahl is credited with first discovering the law of refraction, and used it to create anaclastic lenses."
Alala,f,Ἀλαλά(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀλαλή (alalē) meaning ""war-cry, battle-cry"". This onomatopoeic name belonged to the female personification of the war-cry in Greek mythology. She was an attendant of the war god Ares, whose war-cry was her name: Alale alala."
Alalcomeneïs,f,Ἀλαλκομενηίς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Probably means ""the Protectress, the Defender"" from the Greek verb ἀλαλκεῖν (alalkein) ""to ward, to keep off"". This was an epithet of the goddess Athena as guardian of Boeotia. It is connected with the name of Ἀλαλκομεναί (Alalkomenai), a city in Boeotia; also compare Alalcomenes."
Alalcomenes,m,Ἀλαλκομένης(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Means ""guardian"", derived from Ancient Greek ἄλαλκον (alalkon) meaning ""to ward, keep off; to defend, succour, protect"", a variant of (or related to) ἀλέξω (alexo) ""to defend, help"" (compare Alexamenos). Alalcomenes was an autochthon in Greek mythology, and the eponymous founder of the ancient town Alalcomenae. In some accounts, he raised or tutored the goddess Athena."
Alale,m,ალალე(Georgian)|آلاله(Persian),Georgian (Archaic)|Persian,/ʔɒː.lɒː.ˈle/,,,,,,,"Derived from the Georgian adjective ალალი (alali) meaning ""honest, truthful, upright"". It ultimately comes from Arabic حلال (halal) meaning ""allowed, permitted"" as well as ""lawful, legal, legitimate""."
al-Aleem,,‎اَلْعَلِيْمُ(Arabic),"Arabic (Anglicized, Rare)",al-Al-Leem,,,,,,,"""The All-Knowing One"", ""The Knowledgable One""God, Allah, KnowledgeMost commonly known as one of the 99 names given to Allah in Islam. When used in the context of God, it means the all-knowing one. When used to name a human, it means the knowledgable one."
