Alexagoras,m,Ἀλεξαγόρας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Greek verb ἀλέξω (alexo) meaning ""to defend"" as well as ""to help"". The second element is derived from either the Greek verb ἀγορεύω (agoreuo) meaning ""to orate, to speak publicly"" or the Greek noun ἀγορά (agora), which can mean ""assembly"" as well as ""market, marketplace""."
Alexah,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",ə-LEHKS-ə,,,,,,,Alternate spelling of Alexa.
Alexakis,m,Αλεξάκης(Greek),Greek,,,,,,,,"Modern Greek diminutive of Alexios and Alexis, as it contains the modern Greek diminutive suffix -άκης (-akis). This name is typically only used informally, meaning: it does not appear on birth certificates."
Alexamenos,m,"Ἀλεξαμενός, Ἀλεξάμενος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀλεξάμενος (alexamenos), which is the participle of the Greek verb ἀλέξω (alexo) meaning ""to defend"" as well as ""to help"". Also compare Aleximenes.This name appears in a famous graffito of the early Christian era, which mocks the Christian beliefs of a Roman soldier called ""Alexamenos the Faithful""."
Alexamenus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Alexamenos. This name was borne by an Aetolian general from the 2nd century BC as well as by Alexamenus of Teos, a Greek writer of Socratic dialogues who lived in the 4th century BC."
