Al-muntasir,m,المنتصر(Arabic),Arabic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic منتصر (muntasir) meaning ""victor"". This is part of the title المنتصر بالله (al-Muntasir bi-llah) meaning ""He who triumphs in God"", which was the regional title of Abu Ja'far Muhammad, an Abbasid Caliph in Baghdad."
Almus,m,Ἄλμος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˈæl.məs/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἄλμος (Almos), of uncertain etymology. Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἄλμα (alma) meaning ""grove"", which in turn is apparently related to ἄλσος (alsos) meaning ""sacred grove, hallowed place"". Another possibility might be that the name is derived from ἅλμα (halma) meaning ""spring, leap, jump"", or perhaps ἅλμη (halmē) meaning ""sea water, brine"" (see hals). In Greek mythology, Almus was a son of Sisyphus and the Pleiad Merope."
Al-Musta'in,m,ٱلْمُسْتَعِين(Arabic),Arabic,[al.mus.ta.ʕiːn],,,,,,,"Means ""seeker of aid"" in Arabic, derived from the verb استعان (istaʿāna) meaning ""to seek help"". This is the name of two Abbasid caliphs."
