Aloe,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Aloe is a genus containing over 500 species of flowering succulent plants. The most widely known species is Aloe vera, or ""true aloe"". It is called this because it is cultivated as the standard source for assorted pharmaceutical purposes."
Aloé,,,French (Rare),,,,,,,,"French version of Aloe, The name is often used in fiction for the joke ""Aloé Véra"". Also a other version of Aloés"
Aloeus,m,Ἀλωεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Greek ἀλοάω (aloaō), which can mean ""to thresh, to tread"" as well as ""to crush, to smash"". In Greek mythology, Aloeus was the son of Poseidon and Canace, and husband to Iphimedeia. The Aloadae, two giants, were named after him."
Alof,m,,Medieval Dutch|Medieval German|Dutch (Rare)|West Frisian (Rare),A-lof(Dutch),,,,,,,"Medieval Dutch and German variant of Adolf as well as the West Frisian form of the name. Also compare Aalf (its medieval form is Aelf) and Alef.A known bearer of this name was Alof de Wignacourt (1547-1622), a French nobleman of Flemish descent who was the 54th Grand Master of the Order of St. John of Jerusalem."
Alofisula,,,Samoan (Rare),ah-low-fee-sula,,,,,,,"From alofi meaning ""to sit in a circle"", and sula meaning ""to praise one's riches"" in Samoan."
Aloha,,,Hawaiian (Rare)|American,/a.ˈlo.ha/(Hawaiian),,,,,,,"Short form of Kealoha and other Hawaiian names containing aloha meaning ""love"" or otherwise derived directly from the word."
Alohalani,f,,Hawaiian,/ah-loh-hah-LAH-nee/,,,,,,,"Alohalani is derived from Hawaiian elements Aloha meaning ""love"" and Lani meaning ""heaven"" or ""royalty"". Which, means ""beautiful love"" or ""heavenly love""."
ʻAlohi,,,Hawaiian,ah-LO-hee,,,,,,,"Means ""shine, shining"" in Hawaiian."
ʻAlohilani,f,,Hawaiian (Rare),/ʔa.lo.hi.ˈla.ni/,,,,,,,"Means ""heavenly brightness"" from Hawaiian alohi ""shine"" and lani ""heaven, sky"". In Hawaiian myth this was the name of a heavenly land."
