Amyclas,m,Ἀμύκλας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),ə-MIE-kləs(Greek Mythology),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Amyklas. In Greek mythology this was the name of the founder of the ancient town of Ἀμύκλαι (Amyklai), latinized form Amyclae, in Laconia."
Amycus,m,Ἄμυκος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology, possibly related to Ancient Greek ἀμύσσω (amysso) meaning ""to scratch, tear, mangle, wound"". Sometimes associated with the unrelated Latin word amicus ""friend"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a king of the Bebryces tribe in eastern Bithynia (northwestern Anatolia) and son of Poseidon, known for his prowess at boxing, and a centaur who fought against the Lapiths."
Amye,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Amy.
Amyen,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,Archaic French name of uncertain origin and meaning which was recorded up to the 1600s in the French Bourgogne-Franche-Comté region. Current theories include a local derivation of Latin Amantius (which would make Amyen a cognate of Amant) and Ammien.
Amygdalia,f,Αμυγδαλιά(Greek),Greek,,,,,,,,"The name is derived from the Greek word amygdale, meaning “almond tree”."
