Amymone,f,Ἀμυμώνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/æm.ɪ.ˈmoʊ.niː/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμύμων (amymon) meaning ""blameless, noble, excellent"", itself from ἀ (a), a negative prefix, and μῶμος (momos) ""blame, reproach"". In Greek mythology Amymone was a daughter of Danaus, and grandmother of Palamedes."
Amyn,m,,Arabic,a-MIN,,,,,,,"Possibly a variation of Aameen, or Amin"
Amyna,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Amina.
Amynander,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Amynandros. A notable bearer of this name was king Amynander of Athamania, who lived in the 3rd century BC."
Amynandros,m,Ἀμύνανδρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀμύνανδρος (amynandros) meaning ""warding off enemies"", which consists of the Greek verb ἀμύνω (amyno) meaning ""to ward off, to defend"" combined with Greek ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man""."
Amynomene,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"The name of an Amazon warrior, said to mean ""defender"", or sometimes ""blameless defender"". It is probably derived from Greek ἀμυνομένη (amynomene), participle of the verb ἀμύνω (amyno) meaning ""to keep off, to ward off, to defend, to assist; to avenge, to repay"". The translation of ""blameless"" may stem from its similarity to the name Amymone."
Amynta,f,,"Literature|English (Australian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Amyntas. It was used in 18th-century pastoral poetry.
Amynthe,,,French (Archaic)|Louisiana Creole,A-MINTH(French)|a-MINTH(Louisiana Creole),,,,,,,French feminine and masculine form of Amyntas.
Amyntor,m,Ἀμύντωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"From Greek ἀμύντωρ (amyntôr) meaning ""defender, protector"", which is a derivative of ἀμύνω (amynô) ""to keep off, defend oneself"". (Compare Amyntas.) This name belonged to several characters in Greek mythology. It was also borne by a 4th-century BC Macedonian aristocrat who was the father of Alexander's general and close companion Hephaestion Amyntoros."
Amyoli,f,,South African,amio-li,,,,,,,"Name of South African origin, meaning ""sweet and calm""."
Amyr,,Амыр(Altai),Altai,,,,,,,,"Means ""peace"" in Altai."
Amyra,f,,Muslim|Indian (Muslim),,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Arabic Amira 1.
Amyracle,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Amiracle
Amyrah,f,,Malaysian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Amyria,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Not available.
Amyris,m,Ἄμυρις(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,A-MUY-RIS(Classical Greek)|ə-MEER-is(English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἄμυρος (amyros) meaning ""watery, humid"" or ""not perfumed"", or perhaps from ἄμυρον (amyron) meaning ""saffron thistle"" (Carthamus lanatus)."
Amyrtaeus,m,Ἀμυρταῖος(Greek),Ancient Egyptian (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀμυρταῖος (Amyrtaios), a Hellenization of the original Egyptian form Amenirdisu. This was the name of the only pharaoh of the Twenty-eighth Dynasty of Egypt."
Amyruth,f,,English,,,,,,,,Combination of Amy and Ruth 1.
Amythaon,m,Ἀμυθάων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˌæ.mə.ˈθeɪ.ɒn/(English),,,,,,,"Possibly means ""co-fighter, fighting together"", derived from the Proto-Greek name Hammuthawon, composed of elements related to Ancient Greek ἅμα (hama) meaning ""at the same time, together"" and ὑσμίνη (usmine) meaning ""battle, conflict, combat"". In Greek mythology, Amythaon was a prince of Iolcus as a son of Cretheus and Tyro."
Amytis,f,Αμυτις(Ancient Greek),Old Persian (Hellenized),,,,,,,,"Greek form of an uncertain Persian name, perhaps *Umati which is equivalent to Avestan humaiti meaning ""having good thought"" (derived from hu ""good, well, beautiful"" and maiti ""thought, opinion"", from man ""to know, to think""). This was borne by two famous Babylonian women: the wife of King Nebuchadnezzar II of Babylon, for whom the Hanging Gardens of Babylon were said to have been built; and the daughter of the Persian king Xerxes I."
Amza,m,Амза(Abkhaz),Romanian|Abkhaz (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Amzad,m,আমজাদ(Bengali),Bengali,/ˈa.mɔ.d͡ʒad̪/|/ˈa.mɔ.dʑad̪/,,,,,,,Bengali form of Amjad.
