Anavamadarshin,m,अनवमदर्शिन्(Sanskrit),Buddhism|Sanskrit,/ɐ.n̪ɐ.ˈʋɐ.mɐ.d̪ɐɾ.ɕin̪/(Sanskrit),,,,,,,"Means ""exalted knowledge"" from Sanskrit अनवम (anavama) meaning ""high, exalted, illustrious"" and दर्शिन् (darśin) meaning ""knowing, understanding"". In Buddhist tradition this is the name of the tenth of the 27 buddhas preceding Siddhartha Gautama, known in Pali as Anomadassī. It was also borne by a 13th-century Buddhist monk from Sri Lanka."
Anavat,m,อนวัช(Thai),Thai,/ʔa˨˩.na˦˥.ˈwat̚˦˥/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อนวัช (see Anawat).
Anavea,f,,Polynesian|Tongan|Samoan,ah-na-veh-ah,,,,,,,"From ana meaning ""cave"" and vea meaning ""chief"" in Tongan, or from Samoan anafea meaning ""when""."
Anaveah,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˌɑn.ə.ˈveɪ.ə/|/ˌæn.ə.ˈveɪ.ə/,,,,,,,Contraction of Ana and Nevaeh.
Anavey,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",AN-ə-vay(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Anevay.
Anavi,f,,Indian (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Anavia,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Combination of Ana and Olivia, or a variant of Anabia."
Anavieve,f,,Filipino (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Annavieve
Anavrin,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Derived from the word nirvana spelled backwards.
Anawach,m,อนวัช(Thai),Thai,/ʔa˨˩.na˦˥.ˈwat̚˦˥/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อนวัช (see Anawat).
Anawat,m,อนวัช(Thai),Thai,/ʔa˨˩.na˦˥.ˈwat̚˦˥/,,,,,,,"Means ""blameless, flawless, faultless"" in Thai, ultimately from Sanskrit अनवद्य (anavadya)."
Anawati,f,,Indonesian,/a.na.ˈwa.ti/,,,,,,,Combination of the name Ana and the feminine suffix -wati.
Anawmadatthi,m,အနောမဒဿီ(Burmese),Buddhism,/ʔə.nɔ́.mə.dət.θì/(Burmese),,,,,,,Burmese form of Anomadassī (see Anavamadarshin).
Anawynn,f,,"English (American, Rare)",/ˈæn.ə.wɪn/(American English),,,,,,,Combination of Ana and Wynn.
Anax,m,"Ἀναξ, Ἄναξ(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, chief"". In ancient Greece, it was a title denoting a tribal king or military leader.In Greek mythology, Anax was a giant. He was the son of Uranus and Gaia, and father of Asterius. He was king of the town and territory Anactoria (""place of Anax""), which was later seized by a Cretan who killed Asterius and renamed the city Miletus after himself.Anax was also an epithet of the Greek gods in general, characterizing them as rulers of the world."
Anaxágoras,m,,Galician|Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,"Galician, Spanish and Portuguese form of Anaxagoras."
Anaxagore,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Anaxagoras.
Anaxàgores,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Anaxagoras.
Anaxander,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Anaxandros. This name was borne by a king of Sparta (Greece) from the 7th century BC.
Anaxandra,f,Ὰναξάνδρα(Ancient Greek),"Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology|French (Quebec, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Anaxandros. In Greek legend this name was borne by the wife of King Procles of Sparta. It was also the name of a 3rd-century BC Greek painter, who is mentioned in Clement of Alexandria's essay 'Women as Well as Men Capable of Perfection'."
Anaxandre,m,,Catalan|French,,,,,,,,Catalan and French form of Anaxander.
Anaxandridas,m,Ἀναξανδρίδας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|History,,,,,,,,"Aeolic and Doric Greek form of Anaxandrides, because it contains ίδας (idas), which is the Aeolic and Doric Greek form of the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This name was borne by two kings of Sparta: the first lived in the 7th century BC and the second in the 6th century BC."
Anaxandrides,m,Ἀναξανδρίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Anaxandros"" in Greek, derived from the name Anaxandros combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This was the name of an Athenian Middle Comic poet from the 4th century BC."
Anaxandro,m,,Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,Spanish and Portuguese form of Anaxander.
Anaxandros,m,Ἀνάξανδρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""master of a man"", derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, chief"" and ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man"" (genitive of ἀνήρ (aner) ""man"")."
Anaxanım,f,,Azerbaijani,,,,,,,,"From Azerbaijani ana meaning ""mother"" and xanım meaning ""madam, wife""."
Anaxanor,m,Ἀναξάνωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, chief"" and ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"".Also compare the Greek name Anaxandros, which is closely related."
Anaxarchos,m,Ἀνάξαρχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/ˌæ.nəɡ.ˈzɑːr.kəs/(English)|/a.nák.sar.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/a.ˈnak.sar.xos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek, Constantinopolitan Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king"" and ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""master, ruler"". Alternatively, the second element could derive from ἀρχή (arche) meaning ""origin, source""."
Anaxarchus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Anaxarchos. This name was borne by a Greek philosopher from the 4th century BC.
Anaxarco,m,,Galician|Spanish,,,,,,,,Galician and Spanish form of Anaxarchus.
Anaxáreta,f,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Spanish form of Anaxarete.
Anaxarete,f,Ἀναξαρέτη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ə.ˌnæk.sə.ˈɹɛ.ti/(English)|/a.nak.ˈsa.re.teː/(Classical Latin),,,,,,,"Means ""master of virtue"", derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""lord, master"" and ἀρετή (arete) meaning ""virtue"". In Greek myth this name belonged to a Cypriot maiden whose rejection of Iphis incurred the wrath of Aphrodite. Anaxarete reacted so coolly to the shepherd's passionate love for her that he killed himself; when even the sight of Iphis' dead body did not move her, the goddess of love turned her into stone."
Anaxarque,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Anaxarchus.
Anaxenor,m,Ἀναξήνωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Variant of Anaxanor. This name was borne by a Greek citharode from Magnesia on the Maeander, who was a favourite of the Roman politician Mark Antony (1st century BC)."
Anaxibia,f,Άναξίβια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Anaxibios. This name is carried by several characters in Greek mythology.
Anaxibios,m,Ἀναξίβιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/a.nak.sí.bi.os/(Attic Greek)|/a.nak.ˈsi.βi.os/(Koine Greek)|/a.nak.ˈsi.vi.os/(Byzantine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""master of life"", derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, chief"" and βίος (bios) meaning ""life""."
Anaxibius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Anaxibios, used by a 5th century bc Spartan admiral."
Anaxicrates,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Anaxikrates. This name was borne by two eponymous archons of Athens, the earliest of which lived in the 4th century BC."
Anaxidamo,m,,Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,Spanish and Portuguese form of Anaxidamus.
Anaxidamos,m,Ἀναξίδαμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Doric Greek form of Anaxidemos, because it contains δᾶμος (damos), which is the Doric Greek form of δῆμος (demos) meaning ""people"" as well as ""country, land"".This name was borne by a Eurypontid king of Sparta (7th century BC). He is better known under the latinized form of his name, which is Anaxidamus."
Anaxidamus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Anaxidamos. This was the name of a Eurypontid king of Sparta, who lived in the 7th century BC."
Anaxidemos,m,Ἀναξίδημος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, chief"" combined with the Greek noun δῆμος (demos) meaning ""people"" as well as ""country, land""."
Anaxikrates,m,Ἀναξικράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/a.nak.si.krá.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/a.nak.si.ˈkra.tis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Means ""powerful master"", derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king"" and κράτος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength""."
Anaxilaos,m,"Ἀναξίλαος, Ἀναξίλας(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""master of the people"", derived from Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, chief"" and λαός (laos) meaning ""people, folk""."
Anaxilas,m,"Ἀναξίλας, Ἀναξίλαος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant of Anaxilaos.
Anaxilaus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Anaxilaos. This name was borne by a Greek philosopher from the 1st century BC.
Anaximandre,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Anaximander.
Anaximène,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Anaximenes.
Anaxímenes,m,,Catalan|Galician|Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,"Catalan, Galician, Spanish and Portuguese form of Anaximenes."
Anaxippos,m,Ἀνάξιππος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king"" and ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse""."
Anaxippus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Anaxippos. This was the name of an Athenian comic poet from the 4th century BC.
Anaxo,f,Ἀναξώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king""."
Anay,m,,Tuvan|Marathi|Spanish (Mexican),,,,,,,,"Means ""goat"" in Tuvan."
Anaya,f,अनया(Sanskrit),Indian (Rare)|Pakistani (Rare)|Spanish (Modern)|Asturian (Modern)|Medieval Spanish,a-NAH-ya,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Anay, which is said to be derived from Sanskrit anay ""misfortune, adversity; without a superior"". In Hinduism, the masculine form Anay is an epithet of Vishnu, generally interpreted to mean ""not conducted by anyone; he is one whom none can lead""."
Anayah,f,عَناية(Arabic),Arabic,A-Nih-Ah,,,,,,,"Means ""care, protection, diligence"" in Arabic."
Anayancy,f,,Spanish (Latin American),/a.na.ˈʝan.si/(Latin American Spanish),,,,,,,Variant of Anayansi.
Anayansi,f,,Literature|Spanish (Latin American),/a.na.ˈʝan.si/(Spanish),,,,,,,"Invented by Panamanian author Octavio Méndez Pereira for his historical novel El tesoro del Dabaibe (1934), where it belongs to an indigenous princess, daughter of the cacique Careta, who falls in love with the Spanish conquistador Vasco Núñez de Balboa - a character based on a historical indigenous Panamanian woman. According to 16th-century Spanish chronicles, Careta gave Balboa his daughter as a peace offering, and she became Balboa's interpreter, guide, and companion. Her original indigenous name is unrecorded and lost. After her conversion to Christianity, she was baptized María.The derivation is uncertain; it is popularly claimed to be of indigenous American origin, possibly inspired by the phonetics of Ngäbe (Guaymí) and other Chibchan languages. In Panamanian national consciousness, Anayansi is often portrayed as a princess, a peacemaker, and a founding mother figure."
Anayat,,عنایت(Urdu),Urdu,,,,,,,,Variant of Inayat.
Anayatzin,f,,Nahuatl|Mexican,,,,,,,,"Etymology uncertain, probably uses the reverential or honorific suffix -tzin."
Anaybe,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Not available.
Anayel,m,,Arabic,Ana-yel,,,,,,,"Arabic name, meaning ""gift of God""."
Anayibe,f,,Indigenous American|American (Hispanic),a-nay-yayb(Indigenous American)|ah-nah-YEE-bay(Hispanic American),,,,,,,"Derived from the native South American tribe, the Kogui people. It means ""white flower"" or ""snow flower"" in the Kogui language."
Anayis,f,Անայիս(Armenian),Armenian|Walloon,,,,,,,,Variant form of Anahid.
Anayise,f,,Walloon,,,,,,,,Variant of Anayis.
Anay-kis,f,,Tuvan,,,,,,,,"Means ""goat girl"" in Tuvan."
Anayköz,f,Анайкёз(Karachay-Balkar),Karachay-Balkar,,,,,,,,"From the Karachay-Balkar анай (anay) meaning ""mother"" and кёз (köz) meaning ""eye""."
Anayla,f,,English (American),/ˈæn.ə.la/(American English)|/a.na.ˈjla/(American English),,,,,,,A modern blend of familiar name elements — especially from names like Ana + Layla — that are widely used across many cultures.
Anayo,m,,"Asturian (Modern, Rare)",a-NAH-yo,,,,,,,Masculine form of Anaya.
Anaysa,f,,Arabic|Hebrew,,,,,,,,Not available.
Anaysi,f,,Spanish (Latin American),,,,,,,,Not available.
Anazia,f,,African American (Modern)|Nigerian,an-ay-szee-ah(African American),,,,,,,Transferred use of the surname Anazia.
Anaztázia,f,,Hungarian (Rare),,,,,,,,Cognate of Anastasia.
Anazuita,f,,Obscure,an-a-zoo-ee-ta,,,,,,,New creation from Annunziata influenced by Ana.
An Bài,m,,Vietnamese (Rare),/ʔaːn˧˧ ʔɓaːj˨˩/|/ʔaːŋ˧˧ ʔɓaːj˦˩/|/ʔaːŋ˧˧ ʔɓaːj˨˩/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Vietnamese 安排 (an bài) meaning ""to preordain, prearrange""."
Anbao,m,安宝(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 安 (ān) meaning ""quiet, calm, peaceful, tranquil"" and 宝 (bǎo) meaning ""treasure; precious, rare""."
Anbar,f,عنبر(Arabic),Arabic,,,,,,,,"Means ""amber"" in Arabic (see Amber)."
Anbariz,m,,Medieval Arabic (Moorish),,,,,,,,Not available.
Anben,m,安本(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 安 (ān) meaning ""quiet, calm, peaceful, tranquil"" and 本 (běn) meaning ""root, origin""."
Anberlyn,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Amberlyn.
