Anthylla,f,Ἄνθυλλα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄνθος (anthos) meaning ""blossom, flower, brightness"". Anthylla was a sacrificed victim of the Minotaur in Greek mythology."
Anti,m,,Lule Sami|Hungarian,,,,,,,,"Lule Sámi form of Ánte, a diminutive of Andreas."
Antia,f,,Basque|Romanian|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Basque form of Anthea.
Antialcidas,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Antialkidas. This was the name of an Indo-Greek king from the 2nd century BC.
Antialkes,m,Ἀντιάλκης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, compared to, like"" combined with the Greek noun ἀλκή (alke) meaning ""strength""."
Antialkidas,m,Ἀντιαλκίδας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Antialkes"" in Greek, derived from the name Antialkes combined with ίδας (idas), which is the Aeolic and Doric Greek form of the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Antianeira,f,Ἀντιάνειρα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντιάνειρα (antianeira) meaning ""a match for men"", itself from ἀντί (anti) ""against, compared to, like"" and ἀνήρ (aner) ""man"". In Homer's Iliad ἀντιάνειραι (antianeirai) was an epithet of the Amazons. This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology."
Antianira,f,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Antianeira, as well as the Italian, Portuguese and Spanish form of this name."
Antibrote,f,Ἀντιβρότη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""equal to man"", from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, compared to, like"" combined with βροτός (brotos), a poetic term meaning ""mortal man"". This was the name of an Amazon warrior in Greek mythology."
Anticlée,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Anticlea.
Anticles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Antikles. A known bearer of this name was Anticles of Messenia, an ancient Greek athlete from the 8th century BC."
Anticlides,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Antikleides. A known bearer of this name was the Greek writer Anticlides of Athens (3rd or 4th century BC).
Anticrates,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Antikrates.
Antide,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,French form of Antidius.
Antidolus,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,The seneschal of King Brandon the Saxon. He participated in the Saxon invasion of Britain in the early days of Arthur’s reign. He was killed at the siege of Clarence by Arthur’s Sir Eliezer (Eliezier).
Antidora,f,Ἀντιδώρα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antidoros.
Antidoros,m,Ἀντίδωρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""return-gift, recompense; present offered in exchange"", derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, opposite"" or ""instead of, in exchange for"" combined with δῶρον (doron) meaning ""gift""."
Antidorus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Antidoros.
Antie,f,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Antien,f,,Medieval Dutch|Dutch (Rare),ahn-TEEN(Dutch),,,,,,,"This given name originated in medieval times as a variant spelling (or even a misspelling) of Antjen, as the letter 'j' was regularly written as an 'i' in medieval records. The name Antien has since gone on to become a name of its own, in that this particular spelling took hold and also became pronounced just as it was written (i.e. the 'j' was no longer an 'i' in just writing, but also in pronunciation), thus truly making it independent from Antjen.The name is still in use in the Netherlands to this day, but it is considered rare, as in 2014 there were a little over 60 living bearers (of all ages) in the entire country."
Antieni,m,,Romansh,,,,,,,,"Variant of Antoni, traditionally found in the Surselva region and in central Grisons."
Antifón,m,,Galician,,,,,,,,Galician form of Antiphon.
Antifon,m,,Croatian,AHN-tee-fawn,,,,,,,Croatian form of Antiphon.
Antifont,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Antiphon.
Antifonte,m,,Italian|Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,"Italian, Spanish and Portuguese form of Antiphon."
Antiga,f,Антига(Russian),Azerbaijani (Expatriate),,,,,,,,Form of Əntiqə used outside of Azerbaijan.
Antigenes,m,Ἀντιγένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/an.ti.ɡé.nɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/an.ti.ˈʝe.nis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/an.di.ˈʝe.nis/(Constantinopolitan Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, opposed, compared to, like"" and γενής (genes) meaning ""born"". This name was borne by a general of Alexander the Great, who lived in the 4th century BC."
Antígon,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Antigonos via it's Latinized form Antigonus.
Antigon,m,,History (Magyarized),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Antigonus.
Antígona,f,,Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,Spanish and Portuguese form of Antigone.
Antigona,f,,Albanian|Croatian (Rare),,,,,,,,Albanian and Croatian form of Antigone.
Antigone,m,,"French|History (Ecclesiastical, Gallicized)",,,,,,,,French form of Antigonos via its Latinized form Antigonus.
Antigoné,f,,Greek Mythology (Magyarized),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Antigone.
Antígono,m,,Portuguese|Spanish|Galician,,,,,,,,"Portuguese, Spanish, and Galician form of Antigonos via it's Latinized form Antigonus."
Antigono,m,,Italian,an-tee-GO-no,,,,,,,Italian form of Antigonos (via it's Latinized form Antigonus).
Antigua,f,,Spanish (Rare),/an.ˈti.ɣwa/,,,,,,,"From a title of Virgin Mary, which came to be in Seville Cathedral's Virgen de la Antigua chapel. The word antigua ""old, ancient"" referred to a fresco depicting Virgin Mary, which was sculpted on the wall of an ancient mosque which served as the basis for the new cathedral. The veneration of the aforementioned depiction of Mary (or other similar images) came to be associated with the Virgen de la Antigua title and later spread all over Spain and Latin America.Another theory posits that Pope Sylvester I created the Antigua title in the 4th Century because Mary represented the fulfillment of the prophecies within the Old Testament."
Antika,f,อันธิกา(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.tʰi˦˥.ˈkaː˥˩/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อันธิกา (see Anthika).
Antikleides,m,Ἀντικλείδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Antikles"" in Greek, derived from the name Antikles combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Antikonie,f,,Arthurian Cycle,ahn-TEE-ko-nee(German)|an-TIK-ə-nee(English),,,,,,,"Medieval German variant of Antigone. In Arthurian legend, she is the sister of King Vergulaht of Ascalun who became infatuated with Gawain during one of his visits. This caused her brother to attack Gawain. She later married King Dulcemar of Tandernas and gave birth to Tandareis, who became one of Arthur’s knights. She is mentioned in both Der Pleier's Tandareis and Flordibel and Wolfram von Eschenbach's Parzival."
Antikrates,m,Ἀντικράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/an.ti.krá.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/an.ti.ˈkra.tis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/an.di.ˈkra.tis/(Constantinopolitan Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against"" and κράτος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength""."
Antileon,m,Ἀντιλέων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""lion-like, leonine"" in Ancient Greek, from ἀντί (anti) meaning ""like, equal to"" and λέων (leon) meaning ""lion"". This was the name of one of the fifty (or fifty-one) grandsons of Thespius fathered by Heracles in Greek mythology, his mother being Procris and his twin brother Hippeus. It was also the name of an Ancient Greek author."
Antillia,f,,Medieval French,,,,,,,,Recorded in 15th-century French-speaking Switzerland.
Antilochos,m,Ἀντίλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, compared to, like"" combined with the Greek noun λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Antilochus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Antilochos. This was the name of an ancient Greek historian.In Greek mythology, Antilochus was the son of king Nestor of Pylos, who fought in the Trojan War as one of the Achaeans."
Antim,m,Антим(Bulgarian),Bulgarian|Romanian,,,,,,,,Bulgarian and Romanian form of Anthimos.
Antima,f,,Italian (Rare)|Indian (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antimo.
Antimache,f,Ἀντιμάχη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antimachos.
Antimachea,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Antimache.
Antimachos,m,Ἀντίμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀντίμαχος (antimachos) meaning ""capable of meeting in war"", which consists of Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, compared to, like"" combined with the Greek noun μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"".Also compare the Greek verbs ἀντιμαχέω (antimacheo) meaning ""to resist by force of arms"" and ἀντιμάχομαι (antimachomai) meaning ""to fight against""."
Antimachus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Antimachos. Bearers of this name include two Greek poets and two Graeco-Bactrian kings.Antimachus is also the name of several characters from Greek mythology, one of which is a suitor of Penelope."
Antímaco,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Antimachus.
Antimaque,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized)|History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Antimachus.
Antimina,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Diminutive of Antima.
Antimony,f,,Popular Culture,AN-tə-mo-nee,,,,,,,"Antimony was named after the Greek words anti and monos to mean “a metal not found alone.” The chemical symbol, Sb, comes from the element's historical name, stibium."
Antimoz,m,ანთიმოზ(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,Georgian form of Anthimos.
Antimu,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Antimo.
Antimus,m,,Romansh (Rare),,,,,,,,Romansh form of Anthimus.
Antin,m,Антін(Ukrainian),Ukrainian,un-TEEN,,,,,,,"Ukrainian variant or folk/older form of Anton. A notable bearer was Antin Holovatyi, Zaporozhian Cossack otaman who was one of the creators and a commander of the Black Sea cossack army."
Antina,f,,Italian,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antinoo.
Antine,m,,Sardinian|Dutch (Rare),ahn-TEEN|ahn-TEE-nə,,,,,,,"Short form of Gosantine. Antine Nivola (1911-1988) was a Sardinian sculptor, architectural sculptor, muralist, designer and teacher."
Antinéa,f,,Literature|French,,,,,,,,"Created by Pierre Benoit in his novel L'Atlantide (1919) for Queen Antinéa. The name is inspired by Tinhinan, a 4th-century Tuareg queen with a legendary story. Tinhinan means ""woman of the tents"" but may be metaphorically translated as ""mother of us all""."
Antinea,f,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish and Italian form of Antinéa.
Antinella,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Antines,m,,Faroese,,,,,,,,Faroese form of Antonius.
Antinis,m,,Faroese,,,,,,,,Faroese form of Antonius.
Antino,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Variant of Antinoo.
Antinoe,f,Ἀντινόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Antinoos. This was the name of several princesses in Greek mythology, including one of the Peliades (the daughters of King Pelias of Iolcus and sisters to Alcestis, who were tricked by the sorceress Medea into killing their own father)."
Antinoo,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Antinoos.
Antinoos,m,Ἀντίνοος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Roman Mythology,"/an.tí.no.os/(Attic Greek)|/an.ˈti.no.os/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/æn.ˈtɪ.noʊ.ʌs/(English)",,,,,,,"Means ""against the mind"", derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against"" and νόος (noos) meaning ""mind, thought""."
Antinous,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),‘an-TIN-oh-us‘(English)|‘AHN-TEE-NO-UUS‘(Classical Greek),,,,,,,Latinized form of Antinoos. This name was borne by the favourite and lover of the Roman Emperor Hadrian (2nd century AD).
Antiņš,m,,Latvian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Latvian form of Antonius.
Antinu,m,,Sardinian,,,,,,,,Short form of Custantinu.
Antinuri,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Antioch,m,,Literature|History,AN-tee-ahk,,,,,,,"English form of Antiochus. The capital city of Syria bore this name, an important centre in early Christianity (founded c.300 BC by Seleucus I Nictor and named for his father, Antiochus). The name was used by J. K. Rowling in her 'Harry Potter' series of books, where it belongs a minor character."
Antioche,,Ἀντιόχη(Ancient Greek),French (Archaic)|Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/ɑ̃.tjɔʃ/(French)|an-TIE-ə-kee(Greek Mythology),,,,,,,"French form of Antiochus (masculine), as well as an ancient Greek feminine form of Antiochos (note, the usual feminine form was Antiochis). In Greek mythology Antioche, also called Antiope, was a daughter of Pylon and wife of Eurytus, by whom she became the mother of the Argonauts Iphitus and Clytius."
Antiochides,m,Ἀντιοχίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Antiochos"" in Greek, derived from the name Antiochos combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 5th century BC."
Antiochis,f,Ἀντιοχίς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antiochos. This name was borne by multiple Hellenistic princesses from the Seleucid dynasty.
Antíoco,m,,Spanish (Rare)|Portuguese (Rare)|Galician (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Spanish, Galician and Portuguese form of Antiochus."
Antiocu,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Antioco.
Antioga,f,,Sardinian (Rare)|Theatre,anˈtiːoga(Sardinian),,,,,,,Feminine form of Antiogu. It was used for a character in the Sardinian-language play Ziu Paddori (1918) by Efisio Vincenzo Melis.
Antioĥo,m,,Esperanto,,,,,,,,Esperanto form of Antiochus.
Antiokh,m,Антиох(Russian),Russian (Rare),/ɐnʲ.tʲɪ.ˈox/,,,,,,,Russian form of Antiochus.
Antiokos,m,Անտիոքոս(Armenian),Armenian,[ɑn.ti.jɔ.ˈkʰɔs](Eastern Armenian),,,,,,,Armenian form of Antiochus.
Antiomno,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,Lamanite king of land of Middoni.
Antion,m,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Perhaps an erroneous spelling of Antoine.
Antionah,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,a chief ruler in Ammonihah
Antione,m,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Antoine.
Antionette,f,,English,awn-twaw-NET,,,,,,,Variant of Antoinette.
Antionum,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,Nephite commander (c. AD 385)
Antip,m,Антип(Russian),Medieval Russian,UN-TEEP(Old Russian),,,,,,,Meaning unknown.
Antipa,m,Антипа(Russian),Russian (Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Russian and Romanian form of Antipas. It was borne by the early saint Antipas of Pergamum. The name is sometimes adopted by monks.
Antipas,m,,Biblical|English (Puritan),,,,,,,,Form of Antipatros (see Antipater).
Antipatra,f,"Ἀντιπάτρα, Ἀντίπατρα(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antipatros.
Antípatro,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Antipater.
Antipatro,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Antipater.
Antiphanes,m,Ἀντιφάνης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) ""against, in return"" and φανής (phanes) ""appearing"". This was the name of a 4th-century BCE comic playwright of Middle Comedy."
Antiphilos,m,Ἀντίφιλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/an.ˈti.ɸi.los/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, opposite"" or ""compared to, like"" combined with φίλος (philos) meaning ""friend, lover""."
Antiphilus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Antiphilos. This name was borne by a Greek painter from the 4th century BC.
Antipholus,m,,Theatre,an-TIF-ə-ləs,,,,,,,"Possibly a variant of Antiphilus. The first element of this name is Greek αντι (anti) ""against, compared to, like""; the second element is less certain, perhaps derived from Greek φωλεός (phôleos) ""den, lair"" (the source of the mythological name Pholus) or from Latin folium ""a leaf; a sheet of paper; trifle, thing of no consequence"". This name belongs to two characters in William Shakespeare's play 'The Comedy of Errors' (1592): the twin brothers Antipholus of Ephesus and Antipholus of Syracuse."
Antiphon,m,Ἀντιφῶν(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/an.ti.pʰɔ̂ːn/(Attic Greek)|/an.ti.ˈɸon/(Koine Greek)|/ˈæn.tɪ.fɒn/(English)|/ˈæn.tɪ.fən/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, opposite, in return"" combined with φωνή (phone) meaning ""voice"". This was the name of several historical Ancient Greek figures, including Antiphon the Sophist, who lived in the 5th century BC. It was also the name of a comrade of Odysseus in the Odyssey. In English, an antiphon is a kind of music sung responsively, usually a devotional chant, ultimately derived from the same Greek elements as the given name."
Antiphonus,m,Ἀντίφονος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀντίφονόν (Antiphonon), derived from ἀντίφονος (antiphonos) meaning ""in return for slaughter"" or ""in revenge for blood"", from ἀντί (anti) ""in return for; for the sake of, for"" and φόνος (phonos) ""murder, slaughter; blood shed in murder, gore"". In Greek legend Antiphonus was one of the sons of the Trojan king Priam."
Antiphos,m,Ἄντιφος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈan.ti.ɸos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""bright one, one who sheds light before him"", derived from Ancient Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against"" or ""compared to, like"" combined with φῶς (phos) meaning ""light"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty sons of King Priam, and one of the fifty (or fifty-one) grandsons of Thespius fathered by Heracles in Greek mythology, his mother being Laothoe."
Antiphus,m,Ἄντιφος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Antiphos.
Antipolo,,,Filipino (Rare),/ʔɐn.tɪ.ˈpo.lo/,,,,,,,"Taken from the title of the Virgin Mary Our Lady of Antipolo, also known as Our Lady of Peace and Good Voyage."
Antipus,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,Nephite commander in city of Judea.(c. 65 BC)
Əntiqə,f,,Azerbaijani,,,,,,,,Azerbaijani form of Antiqa.
Antiqa,f,,Uzbek,,,,,,,,"Means ""ancient"" in Uzbek but also figuratively ""amazing, wonderful""."
Antis,m,,Greek (Cypriot)|Latvian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Antissa,f,,English,,,,,,,,"From the place name Antissa, a city on the island of Lesvos."
Antistenes,m,Անտիսթենես(Armenian),Armenian,[ɑn.tis.ˈtʰɛ.nɛs](Eastern Armenian),,,,,,,Armenian form of Antisthenes.
Antisthène,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Antisthenes.
Antisthenes,m,Ἀντισθένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/an.tis.tʰé.nɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/an.tis.ˈθe.nis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/æn.ˈtɪs.θɪ.niːz/(English)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against"" and σθένος (sthenos) meaning ""vigour, strength."" This name was borne by a Greek philosopher from the 4th century BC."
Antjen,f,,Medieval Dutch|Dutch (Rare),AHN-tyən,,,,,,,"This name is a variant of Anna, where the diminutive suffix -tjen has been added to the name. Since -tjen is a diminutive suffix that was primarily used in the Middle Ages and has since been replaced by the more modern -tje, we can actually say that Antjen is the medieval Dutch form of Antje.The name Antjen has survived to modern times, but barely so, as it is extremely rare in the Netherlands these days: in 2014, there were less than 50 living bearers (of all ages) in the entire country. Most living bearers are elderly as well, so the name is set to become even rarer in the future."
Antjuan,m,,African American (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Antoine influenced by Juan 1.
Antlia,f,,Astronomy,ANT-lee-ə,,,,,,,"From Greek ἀντλία (antlia) meaning ""hold of a ship"". Antlia is one of the constellations created by the French astronomer Nicolas Louis de Lacaille in the 18th century. He originally named it Antlia Pneumatica, or Machine Pneumatique in French, in honour of French physicist Denis Papin’s invention, the air pump."
Antnin,m,,Maltese,,,,,,,,Maltese form of Anthony.
Anto,m,,Estonian,,,,,,,,Variant of Ando.
Antoan,m,,Croatian|Serbian|English,,,,,,,,Variant of Anton.
Antoanete,f,,Bosnian (Archaic),ahn-to-a-NEH-te,,,,,,,Variant of Antoaneta.
Antoanett,f,,Hungarian,,,,,,,,Variant of Antoniett.
Antobam,f,,Akan,ahn-to-BAHM,,,,,,,"Means ""posthumous child"" in Fante. It is typically given to a child whose father died before they were born."
Antoéne,m,,Picard,,,,,,,,Picard form of Antoine.
Antoenne,m,,Walloon,/ɑ̃.ˈtwɛn/,,,,,,,Walloon form of Antoine.
Antoennete,f,,Walloon,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antoenne and cognate of Antoinette.
Antogno,m,,Ligurian,,,,,,,,Ligurian form of Anthony.
Antohny,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Anthony.
Antoi,m,,Sardinian,,,,,,,,Contracted form of Antoni.
Antóin,m,,Irish,/an̪ˠ.t̪ˠoːnʲ/,,,,,,,Irish form of Anthony.
Antoinella,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Variant of Antonella and feminine form of Antoine.
Antoineo,m,,Obscure,,,,,,,,A variant of Antonio likely based on Antoine.
Antoinett,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Antoinette.
Antoinetta,f,,English (Rare)|Dutch (Rare),,,,,,,,Quasi-Latinization of Antoinette.
Antoiniece,f,,"African American (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Antoine using the fashionable suffix -iece.
Antoinique,f,,African American (Rare),/æn.twɔn.ˈnik/,,,,,,,Feminization of Antoine by using the fashionable suffix -ique
Antoíño,m,,Galician,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Antón and Antonio.
Antolia,f,,"English (American, Rare)|Spanish (Latin American, Rare)|Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Anatolia.
