Aoha,f,"葵葉, 葵波, 葵羽, 蒼羽, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-HAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 葵 (ao) meaning ""hollyhock"" combined with 葉 (ha) meaning ""leaf"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aohei,m,青兵(Japanese Kanji),Japanese,OW-HAY,,,,,,,"Aohei comes from Japanese 青兵 which consists of 青 (ao) meaning “blue, green” and兵 (hei) meaning “soldier, warrior.”"
Aohime,f,"青姫, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,A-O-KHEE-ME,,,,,,,"From Japanese 青 (ao) meaning ""blue"" combined with 姫 (hime) meaning ""princess"". Other kanji combinations are also possible."
Aohito,m,"青人, 蒼一, 葵仁, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),A-O-KHEE-TO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 青 or 蒼 (ao) meaning ""blue"" combined with 人 (hito) meaning ""person"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aohuan,m,奥欢(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 奥 (ào) meaning ""mysterious, obscure, profound"" and 欢 (huān) meaning ""joyous, merry, happy""."
Aohui,m,翱珲(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 翱 (áo) meaning ""soar, roam"" and 珲 (huī) meaning ""bright, glorious, splendid""."
Aoibh,f,,Irish (Rare),Eve,,,,,,,Aoibh - an old Irish girls name of Celtic origin meaning “Beautiful” “radiant” “Pleasant”
Aoibha,f,,Irish,EE-və|AY-və,,,,,,,"Derived from the Irish word ""aoibh"", meaning beauty. Numerous bearers can be seen on social media."
Aoibhann,f,,Irish Mythology,EE-VIN|EE-VAN,,,,,,,"Variant of Aoibheann.Irish meaning ""Fairy Queen"". She was queen of the fairies in Irish Gaelic Mythology."
Aoibheall,f,,Irish Mythology|Folklore,ee-val,,,,,,,"Probably from Old Irish óibell ""spark, fire"". In Irish legend this is the name of a banshee or goddess who appeared to the Irish king Brian Boru on the eve of the Battle of Clontarf (1014). She is still said to dwell in the fairy mound of Craig Liath in County Clare."
Aoibhgréine,f,,Irish Mythology,eev-GREN-ya,,,,,,,"Derived from Irish aoibh ""smile, pleasant expression"" and grian ""sun"". This name belonged to the daughter of Deirdre and Naoise in Longas Mac nUislenn (The Exile of the Sons of Uisnech), a story of the Ulster Cycle of Irish mythology. She was thought to marry Rinn, king of the Otherworld."
Aoibhí,f,,Irish (Modern),/ˈiː.vi/|/ˈiː.vʲi/,,,,,,,Irish form of Evie or a diminutive of Aoibheann.
Aoichi,m,"青一, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-EE-CHEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 青 (ao) meaning ""blue"" combined with 一 (ichi) meaning ""one"". Other combinations of kanji characters can also form this name."
Aoidh,m,,Scottish Gaelic,,,,,,,,Scottish Gaelic form of Aodh.
Aoika,f,"碧伽, 碧夏, 碧架, 碧花, 碧華, 碧香, 葵加, 葵可, 葵架, 葵香, 蒼佳, 蒼加, 蒼可, 蒼夏, 蒼果, 蒼華, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-EE-KAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 碧 (aoi) meaning ""blue, green"", 葵 (aoi) meaning ""hollyhock"" or 蒼 (aoi) meaning ""blue"" combined with 伽 (ka) meaning ""nursing, attending, entertainer"", 夏 (ka) meaning ""summer"", 架 (ka) meaning ""erect, frame, mount, support, shelf, construct"", 花 (ka) or 華 (ka) both meaning ""flower"", 香 (ka) meaning ""fragrance"", 加 (ka) meaning ""add, addition, increase, join, include, Canada"", 可 (ka) meaning ""can, passable, mustn't, should not, do not"", 佳 (ka) meaning ""excellent, beautiful, good, pleasing, skilled"" or 果 (ka) meaning ""fruit, reward, carry out, achieve, complete, end, finish, succeed"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aoiko,f,葵子(Japanese Kanji)|あおいこ(Japanese Hiragana)|アオイコ(Japanese Katakana),Japanese (Rare),[à.ó̞.í.kò̞],,,,,,,"From Aoi combined with 子 (ko) meaning ""child.""This name is very rarely used."
Aoileann,f,,Irish,ee-linn,,,,,,,"From Irish faoileann meaning ""fair maiden"" or ""seagull""."
Aoine,f,,Irish (Modern),EE-nə,,,,,,,"From Irish aoine meaning ""Friday"", derived from Latin ieiunum. Aoine has only been used as a given name in Ireland in recent times."
Aoise,f,,Irish (Rare),/ˈiː.ʃə/,,,,,,,"Derived from aois meaning ""age, era"" in Irish."
Aojie,m,"奥捷, 傲杰(Chinese)",Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 奥 (ào) meaning ""mysterious, obscure, profound"" or 傲 (ào) meaning ""proud, haughty"" and 捷 (jié) meaning ""win, victory, triumph"" or 杰 (jié) meaning ""hero; heroic, outstanding""."
Aoka,f,"葵花, 葵華, 葵香, 蒼花, 碧花, 藍華, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-KAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 葵 (ao) meaning ""hollyhock"" combined with 花 (ka) or 華 (ka) which both mean ""flower"" or 香 (ka) meaning ""fragrance"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aoki,,青木(Japanese Kanji),Japanese,/äo.ki/,,,,,,,"From Japanese 青 (ao), meaning “blue”, and 木 (ki), meaning “tree”. 青木 (although commonly アオキ) is the Japanese word for the Aucuba japonica, or the spotted laurel plant. In the language of flowers, the Aucuba japonica symbolizes the idiom 初志貫徹, meaning to carry out your first plan, or to follow through on your original intentions."
Aoko,f,"アオコ(Japanese Katakana)|青子, 蒼子, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-KO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 青 (ao) ""blue, green"" combined with 子 (ko) ""child"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aoku,m,"碧玖, 藍久, 青空, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-KUU,,,,,,,"From Japanese 碧 (ao) meaning ""blue, green"", 藍 (ao) meaning ""indigo"" or 青 (ao) meaning ""blue"" combined with 玖 (ku) meaning ""beautiful black jewel, nine"", 久 (ku) meaning ""long time"" or 空 (ku) meaning ""sky"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aolani,f,,Hawaiian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Hawaiian ao ""cloud"" and lani ""sky, heaven""."
Aolanis,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Aolani.
Aolei,,"傲磊, 傲雷, 傲蕾, 奥磊, 奥雷, etc.(Chinese)|奧磊, 奧雷, etc.(Traditional Chinese)",Chinese,/ˀɑʊ̯.leɪ̯/,,,,,,,"From Chinese 奥 (aò) meaning ""difficult to understand"" or 傲 (ào) meaning ""to defy, to refuse to yield to"" combined with 雷 (léi) meaning ""thunder"", 磊 (lěi) meaning ""pile of stones, open, upright"", or 蕾 (lěi) meaning ""bud"". Other hanzi combinations are possible."
Aolibama,f,,Biblical Italian,a-o-LEE-ba-ma,,,,,,,Italian form of Aholibamah.
Aoling,f,奥翎(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 奥 (ào) meaning ""mysterious, obscure, profound"" and 翎 (líng) meaning ""feather, wing""."
Aolú,m,,Irish (Rare),AY-loo,,,,,,,"A combination honoring Irish deities Aodh and Lugh. Aodh is often referred to as a ""god of the underworld,"" although this is likely influenced by Christian interpretation. He and his siblings were turned into swans by their stepmother, Aoife. Lugh was a deity famed for being skilled in many disciplines, including being a poet, smith, swordsman, harpist, historian, craftsman, historian, and warrior. He is known by the epithets Lámfada (""of the long arm""), Ildánach (""skilled in many arts""), Macnia (""youthful hero""), and Lonnbéimnech ""fierce striker""), among others."
Aom,f,ออม(Thai),"Thai (Rare, ?)",[ʔɔ̃ːm˧],,,,,,,Not available.
Aome,f,"青芽, 青女, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-O-ME,,,,,,,"From Japanese 青 (ao) meaning ""blue"" combined with 芽 (me) meaning ""bud, sprout, shoot"" or 女 (me) meaning ""female, woman, wife"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aomei,f,"傲梅, etc.(Chinese)",Chinese,OW-MAY,,,,,,,Combination of Ao & Mei 1.
Aomi,,"あおみ(Japanese Hiragana)|葵海, 葵巳, 翠美, 青海, 青実, 青水, 青美, 蒼海, 蒼実, 蒼深, 蒼美, 蒼望, 蒼未, 蒼魅, 蒼巳, 蒼弥, 碧海, 碧実, 碧水, 碧美, 碧魅, 碧巳, 碧洋, 明歩, 藍海, 藍美, 碧深, 明緒実, 明百美, 粟生実, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,A-O-MEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 葵 (ao) meaning ""hollyhock, althea"", 翠 (ao) meaning ""green, kingfisher"", 青 or 蒼 (ao) meaning ""blue"", 碧 (ao) meaning ""blue, green"", 明 (ao, a) meaning ""bright"", 藍 (ao) meaning ""indigo"", 粟 (a) meaning ""millet"", 緒 (o) meaning ""cord, strap"", 百 (o) meaning ""hundred"" or 生 (o) meaning ""live"" combined with 海 (mi) meaning ""sea, ocean"", 巳 (mi), referring to the Snake, the sixth of the twelve Earthly Branches, 美 (mi) meaning ""beautiful"", 水 (mi) meaning ""water"", 実 (mi) meaning ""fruit, good result, truth"", 深 (mi) meaning ""deep, heighten, intensify, strengthen"", 望 (mi) meaning ""ambition, full moon, hope, desire, aspire to, expect"", 未 (mi) referring to the Sheep, the eighth of the twelve Earthly Branches, 魅 (mi) meaning ""fascination, charm"", 弥 (mi) meaning ""all the more, increasingly"", 洋 (mi) meaning ""ocean"" or 歩 (mi) meaning ""walk"". Other kanji combinations are also possible.This name is most of the time given for females, but rarely given to males, if given at all."
