Aora,f,Ἀώρα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek ἄωρος (aoros) meaning ""unripe, premature, untimely"", composed of the negative prefix ἀ (a) and ὥρα (hora) meaning ""season, time period"". This was the name of a nymph in Greek mythology, namesake of the ancient town Aoros in Crete, later renamed Eleutherna. Local aristocrats claimed to be descended from Aora and her husband Eleuther, one of the Kouretes."
Aoraki,,,Māori (Rare),ow-ra-kee,,,,,,,"Means ""cloud in the sky"" in Māori. Aoraki/Mt Cook is New Zealand's highest mountain. Variant of Aorangi referring to the clouds surrounding the mountain."
Aoran,m,"奥然, 傲然(Chinese)",Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 奥 (ào) meaning ""mysterious, obscure, profound"" or 傲 (ào) meaning ""proud, haughty"" and 然 (rán) meaning ""yes, certainly; pledge, promise""."
Aorangi,,,Māori,ow-rang-ee,,,,,,,"Means ""bright sky"" or ""cloud in the sky"" in Māori from ao ""world, bright, cloud"" and rangi ""sky"". Aorangi is also the Māori word for ""planet""."
