Arche,f,Ἀρχή(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek|Late Greek,/ˈɑːr.kiː/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""beginning, origin"" as well as ""power, authority"" in Ancient Greek. Arche was the muse of origins and beginnings in Greek mythology. A moon of Jupiter was named after her."
Archeanassa,f,"Ἀρχεάνασσα, Ἀρχαιάνασσα, Ἀρχιάνασσα(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Archeanax, derived from Ancient Greek ἄρχω (arkho) meaning ""to rule, lead, command"" (see archos) and ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord"". Archeanassa was a hetaera living in Athens in the late 5th century BC, with whom Plato the philosopher was deeply in love with as a young man."
Archeantus,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,"Nephite soldier, one of three ""choice men"" (Moroni 9) slain in battle (c. AD 375)."
Archebald,m,,Scots (Archaic),,,,,,,,Archaic Scots form of Archibald
Archebios,m,Ἀρχέβιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""ruler of life, master of life"", derived from Ancient Greek ἄρχω (archo) meaning ""to begin, be first"" or ""to lead, rule, govern"" (compare ἀρχός (archos) ""master"") and βίος (bios) meaning ""life"". Alternatively, the second element could instead be βία (bia) meaning ""force, bodily strength, power""."
Archebius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Archebios. This was the name of an Indo-Greek king from the 1st century BC.
