Archelao,m,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Archelaus.
Archélaos,m,,Ancient Greek (Gallicized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Archelaos.
Archelas,m,,French (Quebec)|French (Acadian),,,,,,,,Variant of Archélaos.
Archelau,m,,Sardinian (Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Sardinian form of Archelaus.
Archélaüs,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized)|Biblical French,/aʁ.ʃe.la.ys/(Greek Mythology),,,,,,,French form of Archelaus.
Archeliht,m,,American (Hispanic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Archelochos,m,Ἀρχέλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Either a variant of Archilochos or an independent name in its own right. If the latter is the case, then the first element of this name is different from that of the aforementioned name, but the second element is exactly the same: Greek λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"". Here, the first element is in all likelihood the Greek noun ἀρχή (arche) meaning ""beginning, origin, source"", which you can also find in the modern English word archetype. As such, the meaning of this name is then basically ""the original ambush"", as in: the first ambush of its kind."
Archelochus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Archelochos. In Greek mythology, Archelochus was one of the leaders of the Dardanians, who aided the Trojans in their fight against the Achaeans."
Archelous,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Archelaus.
Archemachos,m,Ἀρχέμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄρχω (arkho) meaning ""to begin, lead, rule, command"" (see archos) and μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle""."
Archemachus,m,Ἀρχέμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄρχω (arkho) meaning ""to begin, lead, rule, command"" (see archos) and μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"". This was the name of one of the fifty (or fifty-one) grandsons of Thespius fathered by Heracles in Greek mythology, his mother being Patro. It was also the name of a son of King Priam of Troy."
Archemais,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"In the Prophecies de Merlin, the son of Angusel, King of Scotland. He took part in the expedition to save King Richard of Jerusalem from the King of Baghdad. He succeeded his father to the throne of Scotland."
Archeman,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A cousin and enemy of Tristan. King Mark of Cornwall was his uncle, and the evil Andret (Andred) was his brother.Tristan killed Archeman at the Fountain of the Lion."
Archemire,m,,Greek,Arki-mir,,,,,,,Not available.
Archemoros,m,Ἀρχέμορος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""the forerunner of death"" or ""the beginning of destiny"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἀρχή (arche) meaning ""beginning, origin"" as well as ""authority, dominion"" (see archos) and μόρος (moros) meaning ""fate, destiny; doom, death"". In Greek mythology, Archemoros was the posthumous name of Opheltes, the infant son of King Lycurgus who was prophesied to die if he touched the ground before learning to walk. He was eventually killed by a snake when his nurse left him unattended in a patch of celery."
Archemorus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Archemoros.
Archena,f,,Indian (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Archenior,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A duke and vassal of Feirefiz, Perceval’s half-brother."
Archenor,m,Ἀρχήνωρ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""master, leader"" combined with the Greek noun ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"".In Greek mythology, Archenor is the name of one of the Niobids."
Archeptolemos,m,Ἀρχεπτόλεμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from either Ancient Greek ἀρχός (archos) ""master, ruler, leader"" or ἀρχή (arche) ""origin, source"". combined with πόλεμος (polemos) meaning ""war, battle""."
Archeptolemus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Archeptolemos.
Archetimos,m,Ἀρχέτιμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/ar.kʰé.tiː.mos/(Attic Greek)|/ar.ˈçe.ti.mos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from either Greek ἀρχή (arche) meaning ""beginning, origin; authority"" or ἄρχω (arkho) meaning ""to rule, lead, command"" and τιμή (time) meaning ""honour, esteem"" (see timao)."
Archetimus,m,Ἀρχέτιμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinised form of Archetimos.
Archey,m,,English (Rare),/ˈɑɹ.t͡ʃi/(American English)|/ˈɑː.t͡ʃi/(British English),,,,,,,Diminutive of Archibald.
Archi,,अर्ची(Sanskrit),English (Rare)|Hinduism,,,,,,,,Variant and feminine form of Archie.
Archia,f,,"English (American, Rare)|African American (Rare)",,,,,,,,"Transferred use of the surname Archia, which is by far the most prevalent in the United States and as such might possibly be a relatively new surname. Its meaning is currently unknown, but it is possible that it is derived from the masculine given name Archie.Bearers of Archia as a given name (especially those in the African-American community) are likely to have been named after Tom Archia (1919-1977), an African-American jazz tenor saxophonist. However, it is also possible that at least in some cases, Archia is a direct feminization of Archie (i.e. people with a male relative named Archie named their daughter after said relative, but tweaked the name slightly so that it fits the gender better)."
Archias,m,Ἀρχίας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ar.kʰí.aːs/(Classical Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀρχή (arche) meaning ""power, rulership"" (compare the related word ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""master"")‎ combined with the masculine name suffix -ίας (-ias). Bearer Aulus Licinius Archias was a 1st-century BC Greek poet defended by Cicero."
Archibaldina,f,,"Scottish (Rare, Archaic)|English (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Archibald.
Archibaldo,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Archibald.
Archibaldos,m,Αρχιβαλδος(Ancient Greek),Germanic (Hellenized),,,,,,,,Hellenized form of Archibaldus (see Archibald).
Archibold,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Variant spelling of Archibald, usually used as a surname."
Archibong,m,,Efik,,,,,,,,Anglicised version of the Name Asibong.First Answered by King Archibong the first (Asibong Edem Ekpo Efiom Okoho)
Archidamo,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Archidamus.
Archidamos,m,Ἀρχίδαμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is either derived from Greek ἀρχός (archos) ""master"" or from Greek ἀρχή (arche) ""origin, source"". The second element is derived from δᾶμος (damos) ""the people"", which is a Doric Greek variant of δῆμος (demos)."
Archidamus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Archidamos. This name was borne by five kings of Sparta (Greece).
Archidike,f,Ἀρχιδίκη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant form of Archedike. This was the name of a famous hetaera from Naucratis in Egypt.
Archiebald,m,,English (African)|Filipino,,,,,,,,Variant of Archibald.
Archiee,,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Possibly a form of Archie.
Archiina,f,Арчыына(Yakut),Yakut,,,,,,,,Not available.
Archili,m,არჩილი(Georgian),Georgian,,,,,,,,Form of Archil with the Georgian nominative suffix -ი (-i). It is only used in Georgian when the name is written stand-alone.
Archilochos,m,Ἀρχίλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""master, leader"" combined with the Greek noun λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Archilochus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Archilochos. This was the name of a Greek lyric poet from the 7th century BC.In Greek mythology, Archilochus was a Trojan who was killed by Menelaus."
Archilus,m,,English (American),,,,,,,,Not available.
Archimago,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"An evil magician in The Faerie Queene who represents, at various places in Spenser’s elaborate allegory, the pope and Satan.Archimago hinders the successes of the heroes Guyon, the Red Cross Knight, and Arthur through sorcery and trickery. The Red Cross Knight throws him into a dungeon, but he manages to escape."
Archimède,m,,"French (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,French form of Archimedes.
Archimede,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Archimedes.
Archimédes,m,,Czech,/ˈar.xɪ.mɛː.dɛs/,,,,,,,Czech form of Archimedes.
Archimidis,m,Αρχιμήδης(Greek),Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,Modern Greek form of Archimedes.
Archimiro,m,,"Aragonese (Rare, Archaic)",ar-chi-MEE-ro,,,,,,,Aragonese form of Argimiro.
Archina,f,,English,,,,,,,,Feminization of Archibald.
Archip,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Archippos via its latinized form Archippus.
Archippe,m,,Biblical French,,,,,,,,French form of Archippos via its latinized form Archippus.
Archippo,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Archippos via its latinized form Archippus.
Archisa,f,अर्चिसा(Hindi),"Indian (Modern, Rare)|Hindi (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Derived from Sanskrit archis (अर्चिस्) ""ray of light"" or ""luster""."
Archisha,f,,Hindi (Rare),Ar-chi-shah,,,,,,,"Means ""ray of light"" in Hindi."
Archishman,m,অর্চিষ্মান(Bengali),Bengali,/ˈɔɾ.t͡ʃiʃː.ãn/|/ˈɔɹ.tɕiʃː.an/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Bengali অর্চিষ্মান (see Archisman).
Archisio,m,,"Italian (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Archisman,m,অর্চিষ্মান(Bengali),Bengali,/ˈɔɾ.t͡ʃiʃː.ãn/|/ˈɔɹ.tɕiʃː.an/,,,,,,,"From Sanskrit अर्चिष्मत् (arciṣmat) meaning ""sun, fire, flaming, brilliant, bright""."
Archita,f,,Indian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Architeles,m,Ἀρχιτέλης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄρχω (arkho) meaning ""to begin, lead, rule, command"" (see archos) and τέλος (telos) meaning ""end, result, purpose""."
Architimus,m,Ἀρχέτιμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Variant of Archetimus (See Archetimos). This was the name of a 1st-century Ancient Greek writer.
Architrenius,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"The eponymous protagonist of 'Architrenius', a medieval allegorical and satirical poem in hexameters by Johannes de Hauvilla.According to C.S. Lewis, this poem involves ""the story of the 'Arch-mourner', the youthful Architrenius, who, in mere despair at the wickedness within him and about him, sets out to seek his mother Nature and be healed. His journeys lead him through many places, including the university of Paris and the island of Thule, thus offering the author the opportunity for lavish satiric description, before the wanderer finally meets Nature."
Archival,m,,Spanish,AWR-CHEE-VAWL,,,,,,,"Short version of ""Archivaldo""."
Archivaldo,m,,Spanish,/aɾ.tʃi.ˈβal.do/,,,,,,,Variant of Archibaldo.
Archna,f,,Hindi,,,,,,,,"Derived from Sanskrit arcana ""honouring, praising."""
Archon,m,Ἄρχων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἄρχων (archon) meaning ""ruler, commander"".Notable bearers of this name include the Achaean statesman Archon of Aegeira (2nd century BC) and Archon of Pella, a satrap of Babylonia (4th century BC)."
Archonides,m,Ἀρχωνίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Archon"" in Greek, derived from the name Archon combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This name was borne by a tyrant of Herbita from the 5th century BC."
Archontia,f,Αρχοντία(Greek),Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Archontion.
Archontion,m,Αρχοντίων(Greek),Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Archontissa,f,Αρχόντισσα(Greek),Greek|Judeo-Greek (?),,,,,,,,"Means ""noblewoman, lady, female ruler; rich woman"" in Greek, ultimately from Ancient Greek ἄρχων (archon) meaning ""leader, ruler, commander"" (see Archon). This is the name of a 1939 rebetiko song by Vassilis Tsitsanis."
Archulus,m,,"English (American, Anglicized, Modern)",,,,,,,,Related to Archelaus
Archy,m,,English,AHR-chee(American English)|AH-chee(British English),,,,,,,"Variant of Archie.A notable bearer is the musician Archy Ivan Marshall (1994-), otherwise known as King Krule."
Archye,,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form and variant of Archie.
Archyn,m,Арчын(Yakut),Yakut,,,,,,,,"Means ""deliverer, savior, redeemer"" in Yakut."
Archytas,m,Ἀρχύτας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ˈɑːr.kɪ.təs/(English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from either Ancient Greek ἄρχω (archo) meaning ""to lead, rule; to begin"" or ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""leader, ruler, master"". Archytas was an Ancient Greek philosopher, mathematician, astronomer, statesman, and strategist of 5th-century BC."
Arcibaldo,m,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Archibald.
Arciel,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A Knight of the Round Table who killed his brother, Sanades, in combat for the love of a maiden. Arciel received a mortal wound in the fight.As soon as he was aware of it, he tried to slay the maiden to keep any other man from having her, but she fled. Galahad found Arciel in his death throes and had him buried."
Arcieor,m,,Italian,are-see-or,,,,,,,"It is an altered version of the Italian word ""Arciro"", meaning archer."
Arcília,f,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Cognate of Arcilia.
Arcilia,f,,"American (South, Archaic)|French (Acadian)|French (Quebec)|Spanish (Canarian)",,,,,,,,Southern variant of Arcelia and Acadian and Québécois variant of Arcélia.
Arçın,f,Арчын(Khakas),Khakas,,,,,,,,"Means ""juniper"" in Khakas."
Arciom,m,Арцём(Belarusian),Belarusian,,,,,,,,Łacinka transcription of Artsiom.
Arciš,m,Арціш(Belarusian),Belarusian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Arsienij.
Arcite,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Arcite is a deceitful knight in ""Anelida and Arcite"", a 357-line English poem by Geoffrey Chaucer. It tells the story of Anelida and her wooing by false Arcite from Thebes, Greece."
Arcjom,m,Арцём(Belarusian),Belarusian,,,,,,,,Belarusian-Łacinka form of Artyom.
Arcois,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A brave knight from Flanders in the service of King Claudas.Arcois spoke up against Claudas when Claudas suggested that he might go to war with Arthur. Claudas respected Arcois’ honesty and sense of justice, and he made Arcois the constable of his court.In the Vulgate, Arcois accompanies Claudas to England and is there knighted by him."
Arcolas,m,,Romani (Archaic),,,,,,,,Corruption of Hercules.
Arcrun,m,Արծրուն(Armenian),Armenian,/ɑɾt͡s.ˈɾun/|/ɑɾd͡z.ˈɾun/,,,,,,,Variant of Artsrun.
Arctino,m,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Arctinus.
Arctinus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Arktinos. This was the name of a Greek epic poet, who was said to have been a pupil of Homer."
Arcuo,m,,Medieval Galician,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ard,m,ארד(Ancient Hebrew),Dutch|Biblical Hebrew,AHRD,,,,,,,"This name is often a short form of Arnoud (and other names containing the Germanic element aran or arn meaning ""eagle"", such as Arend) and Eduard. However, it can also be a short form of Adriaan and Adrianus, as it is in the case of the Dutch politician and minister Ard van der Steur (b. 1969) and the Dutch former speed skater Ard Schenk (b. 1944). Last but not least, in some cases, this name can also be a variant spelling of Art."
Arda,f,,Sicilian|Dutch (Rare),AHR-dah,,,,,,,Variant of Alda 1.
Ardabast,m,,Gothic,,,,,,,,Name of two Visigothic kings.
Ardagast,m,,Medieval Slavic|History,,,,,,,,"The meaning of the first element of this name is unknown to me, but the second element is probably derived from Slavic gost ""guest"". Also note the resemblance to the Germanic name Arbogast. This name was borne by a South Slavic chieftain from the 6th century AD."
Ardakhshir,m,,Middle Persian,,,,,,,,"Variant of Ardashir. This was the name of a dynast (frataraka) of Persis in the late 3rd century BC, ruling sometime after 220 to c. 205 BC."
Ardaleon,m,Ардалеон(Russian),Russian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Extremely rare variant of Ardalion, which was probably influenced by the Greek word λέων (leon) meaning ""lion"" (see Leon)."
Ardalioni,m,არდალიონი(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Form of Ardalion with the nominative suffix, used in Georgian when the name is written stand-alone."
Ardan,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Benedictine monk, also known as Ardanus. 13th abbot of the abbey of Saint Philibert at Tournus, diocese of Autun, France in 1028. Restored monastic buildings there, and cared for the local people during the famine of 1030 to 1033."
Ardanelle,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ardaq,f,Ардақ(Kazakh),Kazakh,,,,,,,,"Means ""honour, esteem"" in Kazakh."
Ardaric,m,,Germanic|History,,,,,,,,"Variant of Hardaric, with reduction of the 'h.' Ardaric was the name of a 5th-century king of the Gepids, a Germanic tribe."
Ardarich,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,German form of Ardaric.
Ardarico,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Ardaric.
Ardaricus,m,,Germanic (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Ardaric.
Ardarik,m,,Dutch|Frisian,,,,,,,,Dutch and Frisian form of Ardaric.
Ardaryk,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Ardaric.
Ardashel,m,არდაშელ(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,Georgian form of Ardashir.
Ardavan,m,اردوان(Persian),Persian,/ʔæɾ.dæ.ˈvɒːn/,,,,,,,Modern Persian form of Artabānu.
Ardavon,m,Ардавон(Tajik),Tajik,,,,,,,,Tajik form of Artabānu. Also compare modern Persian Ardavan.
Arddun,f,,Medieval Welsh|Welsh (Rare),AR-dhin(Welsh),,,,,,,"Means ""beautiful, magnificent, sublime"" in Welsh."
Ardel,m,,English,,,,,,,,Variant of Ardell or Ardal.
Ardelia,f,,"American (South, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Artelia.
Ardell,,,English,ahr-DEHL,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. In some cases it could be a transferred use of the Swedish surname Ardell. It could also possibly be variant of Ardal, or of Odell or Iredell (perhaps influenced by the initial syllable in names such as Arthur and Ardis 3).As a feminine name, it could also be a variant of Ardella or Ardelia. Also compare Ardelle."
Ardella,f,,English,ar-DELL-ah,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ardell.
Ardelle,f,,English,ar-DELL,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ardell.
Ardemis,f,Արտեմիս(Armenian),"Armenian (Western, Rare)",/ɑɾ.de.ˈmis/(Western Armenian),,,,,,,Western Armenian transcription of Artemis.
Ardena,f,,English (American),,,,,,,,Not available.
Ardene,f,,English (Rare),ahr-DEEN,,,,,,,Possibly a feminine form of Arden or a blend of Ardith and Arlene.
Ardent,m,,English,,,,,,,,"Middle English from Old French ardant from Latin ardens, ardent-, from ardere ‘to burn’."
Ardesia,f,,Italian,/ar.ˈdɛzja/,,,,,,,"Means ""slate"" or ""slate grey"", derived from a rock often used for building"
Ardeşir,m,,Turkish,,,,,,,,Turkish form of Ardashir.
Ardeth,f,,American (Rare),AHR-dəth,,,,,,,Variant of Ardath.
Ardevan,m,არდევან(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Georgian form of Artabānu, likely via its modern Persian form Ardavan."
Ardhanarishvara,,अर्धनारीश्वर(Sanskrit),Hinduism,,,,,,,,"Means ""lord who is half female"", from Sanskrit अर्ध (ardha) meaning ""half"", नारी (nārī) meaning ""woman, wife"", and ईश्वर (īśvara) meaning ""lord, ruler, god"". This is the name of the Hindu diety representing the masculine and feminine energies of the universe. The right superior side of the body usually is the male Shiva and the left is the female Parvati."
Ardhendu,m,অর্ধেন্দু(Bengali),Bengali,/ˈɔɾ.d̪ʱen.d̪u/|/ˈɔɹ.d̪ʱen.d̪u/,,,,,,,"From Sanskrit अर्धेन्दु (ardhendu) meaning ""half moon, crescent moon""."
Ardhian,m,,Indonesian,/ar.ˈdi.an/,,,,,,,Variant of Ardian.
Ardhiansyah,m,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Variant of Ardiansyah.
Ardi,m,,Albanian|Estonian (Rare)|Indonesian,/ˈar.di/,,,,,,,Variant of Ardian.
Ardî,m,,Kurdish,/ˈɑːɾ.diː/,,,,,,,Not available.
Ardian,m,,Indonesian,/ar.ˈdi.an/,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic أرض (ardh) meaning ""land, earth""."
Ardiana,f,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ardian.
Ardiansyah,m,,Indonesian,/ar.di.ˈan.ʃah/,,,,,,,"Combination of the name Ardian and Persian شاه (shāh) meaning ""king""."
Ardıç,f,,Turkish (Modern),ahr-dich,,,,,,,"From Turkish ardıç, meaning ""juniper"". This is a modern invention."
Ardice,f,,"English (American, Rare)",AR-dis(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Ardis.
Ardie,,,American (Rare)|Indonesian,AHR-dee|/ˈar.di/,,,,,,,Diminutive of Arden or names with the sound ard or perhaps a variant of Artie.
Ardijan,m,Ардијан(Serbian),Albanian|Serbian,Arr di yaan,,,,,,,Variant of Ardian.
Ardijana,f,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ardijan.
Ardil,m,,Kurdish,ardle,,,,,,,Fire Heart
Ardin,m,,Dutch (Rare),AHR-din,,,,,,,"Short form of Ardinus. In some cases, the name can also be an elaboration of Ard, which itself is a short form of Arnoud (and other names containing the Germanic element aran or arn meaning ""eagle"") as well as of Eduard and Adriaan.With that said, you might also want to compare this name with Arduin, as they are quite similar in appearance.A known bearer of this name is Ardin Mourik (b. 1981), a Dutch theologian who is also active as a politician for the Dutch political party CDA (specifically, for its branch that is located in the province of Utrecht)."
Ardina,f,,Dutch,ahr-DEE-nah,,,,,,,"This name can be a contracted form of Arendina and Arnoldina as well as be a more elaborate form of Arda. Less commonly, the name is also found as a short form of names ending in -ardina, such as Bernardina and Gerardina.Known bearers of this name include the Dutch left-wing politician Ardina ""Arda"" Gerkens (b. 1965) and the Dutch former field hockey player Mijntje Donners (b. 1974), who carries the name as a middle name."
Ardine,f,,Dutch (Rare),ahr-DEE-nə|ahr-DEEN,,,,,,,"Variant form of Ardina, of which the spelling and pronunciation was inspired or influenced by French feminine names. However, there are also (rare) cases where it is a Dutch short form of actual French given names, such as Bernardine and Gérardine."
Ardiñipén,f,,Romani (Caló),ar-dee-nyi-PEN(Caló),,,,,,,"Means ""nativity, birth"" in Caló. This name is used as a Caló form of Natividad (see Molchibé)."
Ardinus,m,,Dutch (Rare),ahr-DEE-nus,,,,,,,"Contracted form of Arendinus. But in other words, you could also say that this given name is a masculinization of Ardina.However, it should be noted that it is possible that there might be cases where this name is a Dutch variant of Arduinus."
Árdís,f,,Icelandic,,,,,,,,Variant of Arndís.
Ardis,f,,Norwegian (Rare)|Swedish (Rare)|Latvian|English (American),/ˈɑɹd.ɪs/(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Arndis.
Ardisia,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,From the name of the genus of flowering plants that is also called coralberry or marlberry.
Ardita,f,,Medieval Italian (Tuscan),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ardito.
Ardjan,m,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Variant of Ardian.
Ardo,m,,Fula,,,,,,,,"Meaning ""chief"" or ""ruler""."
Ardofe,m,,Occitan,ar-DO-fe,,,,,,,Variant of Adofe.
Ardon,m,,Biblical,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ardoth,,,"American (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Ardous,f,,Obscure (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Ardoxsho,f,Αρδοχϸο(Bactrian Script),Near Eastern Mythology,,,,,,,,"Kushan goddess of wealth primarily worshipped in the early first milennium CE. She is known in the Avesta as Ashi, and parallels have also been drawn with the Greek goddess Tyche, the Persian goddess Anahita, and the Buddhist deity Hariti."
Ardra,f,आर्द्रा(Sanskrit)|ആർദ്ര(Malayalam),Astronomy|Malayalam,/ˈɑːɾ.d̪ɾɑː/(Sanskrit)|/ˈaːr.d̪ɾɐ/(Malayalam),,,,,,,"From Sanskrit आर्द्र (ārdra) meaning ""wet, moist, damp"". This is the name of the sixth nakshatra (lunar mansion) in Hindu astronomy, which corresponds to the star Betelgeuse."
