Argyrios,m,Αργύριος(Greek),Greek,Ar-γEE-ree-os,,,,,,,From the word silver.
Argyroula,f,Αργυρούλα(Greek),Greek,/aɾ.ʝi.ˈɾu.la/,,,,,,,Diminutive of Argyro.
Arhaan,m,,Indian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Arham,m,أرحم(Arabic),Arabic,,,,,,,,"Means ""merciful"" in Arabic."
Arhan,m,,Indian (Rare),,,,,,,,Variation of Arhaan.
Arhane,f,,Basque (Modern),,,,,,,,"Taken from the town of Arhane (in French, Arhan) in the French part of the Basque Country. The name of the town ultimately comes from Basque haran (""valley"")."
Arhelaj,m,,History,,,,,,,,Macedonian form of Archelaos (see Archelaus).
Arhild,f,,Anglo-Saxon,"/ɑːr,xild/(Old English)",,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Old Norse Arnhildr, ""eagle battle"". Alternatively, could be from the Old English elements ār ""honour, glory, grace"" (see aizō) and hild ""battle"". This was the name of a saint from Oldbury-on-Severn. See Arilda."
