Aricia,f,Άρικία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Roman Mythology|Literature|Theatre,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of the Greek Άρικία (Arikia), probably derived from Latin aro ""to plough"". This is commonly thought to be the name of a consort of Hippolytos due to a verse from the Aeneid that roughly translates as ""To the war also came the most fair offspring of Hippolytus, Virbius, whom, distinguished, mother Aricia sent...""; however, as Virbius is the name that Hippolytos was given after being resurrected by Athena, and Aricia was the name of the town that he was brought to after his resurrection, it’s likely that this is a misunderstanding of the text.In the literary world, Aricia was one of the main characters in Jean Racine's tragedy Phèdre (1677)."
Aricie,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized)|Theatre|French (Archaic)|Louisiana Creole (Archaic)|Haitian Creole (Rare),,,,,,,,French form of Aricia. This name was used by Jean Racine in his play Phèdre (1677) which inspired Jean-Philippe Rameau to write his first opera Hippolyte et Aricie (1733).
Arick,m,,"English (American, Rare)",EHR-ik(American English),,,,,,,Likely a variant of Erick.
Aricka,f,,African American,,,,,,,,Variant of Erica.
Aricu,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Variant of Arigu.
Aridane,,,Spanish (Canarian),/a.ɾi.ˈda.ne/(Canarian Spanish),,,,,,,"From the name of a valley on the island of La Palma (Canary Islands, Spain). The place name is of Guanche origin, a metathesis of *adiran meaning ""base, foundation; bed (of a valley)"". A similar name, Aridañy, was recorded as belonging to a Guanche man from the island of Gran Canaria (late 15th century). Known bearers of this name include the Canarian-born footballers Aridane Santana (1987-) and Aridane Hernández (1989-)."
Aridani,m,,Guanche|Spanish (Canarian),,,,,,,,Meaning uncertain; possibly a variant of Aridane.
Aridatha,m,,Biblical,,,,,,,,"A son of Haman in the book of Esther. Possibly related the Persian Haridata, meaning ""given by Hari""."
Aridela,f,Ἀριδήλα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Aridelos. On the Greek island of Crete, the mythological Cretan princess Ariadne was also called Aridela. In other words: Aridela was an alternative name for Ariadne, which was primarily or even exclusively used on Crete."
Aridelos,m,Ἀρίδηλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀρίδηλος (aridelos) meaning ""clear, distinct, far seen"". It consists of the Greek strengthening prefix ἀρι (ari) meaning ""most"" combined with the Greek adjective δῆλος (delos) meaning ""visible, conspicuous, manifest"". The latter word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb δηλόω (deloo) meaning ""to make visible""."
Aridelus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aridelos.
Aridian,f,,Spanish (Canarian),a-ṘEE-dyan(Canarian Spanish),,,,,,,Variant of Haridian.
Aridio,m,,Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,"Spanish and Portuguese form of Aridius. However, in these languages, the name can also be a variant form of Arildo.A bearer of this name was Aridio Saldaña, the Dominican father of American actress Zoe Saldana (b. 1978)."
Aridius,m,,Late Roman,,,,,,,,"This name was borne by Quintus Aridius Rufinus, a Roman consul from the early 3rd century. His name may possibly have been derived from Latin aridus meaning ""dry, arid, parched"". But as it is possible that he may have been a Gallo-Roman instead of a pure Roman, Aridius could also be the latinized form of a Gaulish name. Lastly, it might also be interesting to compare this name with Arrhidaeus, a Greek name that is sometimes also spelled as Arideus."
Aridj,f,أريج(Maghrebi Arabic),Arabic (Maghrebi),,,,,,,,"Alternate transcription of أريج (see Arij), chiefly used in Northern Africa."
