Aristaenetus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aristainetos. Bearers of this name include an Achaean general (3rd century BC) and a Byzantine Greek epistolographer (5th or 6th century AD).
Aristaenus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aristainos. A notable bearer of this name was Aristaenus of Megalopolis (2nd century BC), a strategos (military leader) of the Achaean League."
Aristagora,f,Ἀρισταγόρα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek elements ἄριστος (aristos) ""best, noblest"" and ἀγορά (agora) ""assembly, gathering place, marketplace"". Compare the masculine equivalent Aristagoras."
Aristagoras,m,Ἀρισταγόρας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|History,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Greek adjective ἄριστος (aristos) meaning ""best"". The second element is derived from either the Greek verb ἀγορεύω (agoreuo) meaning ""to orate, to speak publicly"" or the Greek noun ἀγορά (agora), which can mean ""assembly"" as well as ""market, marketplace"".This name was borne by several ancient Greek tyrants, such as Aristagoras of Cyme (6th century BC) and Aristagoras of Miletus (5th century BC)."
Aristaichmos,m,Ἀρίσταιχμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/a.rís.tai̯kʰ.mos/(Attic Greek)|/a.ˈris.tɛx.mos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἄριστος (aristos) meaning ""best"" and αἰχμή (aichme) meaning ""point of a spear, point of an arrow"", or figuratively ""war; warlike spirit""."
Aristaineta,f,Ἀρισταινέτα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Aristainetos, derived from Ancient Greek ἄριστος (aristos) meaning ""best"" and αἰνετός (ainetos) meaning ""praiseworthy""."
Aristainetos,m,"Ἀρισταίνετος, Ἀρισταινέτος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,/a.ris.tǎi̯.ne.tos/(Attic Greek)|/a.ris.ˈtɛ.ne.tos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄριστος (aristos) meaning ""best"" combined with αἰνετός (ainetos) meaning ""praiseworthy"". The latter word is ultimately derived from the Greek noun αἴνη (aine) meaning ""praise, fame"" (see Aeneas)."
Aristainos,m,Ἀρίσταινος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἄριστος (aristos) meaning ""best"" combined with the Greek noun αἴνη (aine) meaning ""praise, fame"" (see Aeneas)."
Aristaj,m,Аристај(Serbian),Serbian,,,,,,,,"Serbian name for Aristaeus, a minor Greek god of agriculture, hunting and cattle."
Aristajos,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Aristaeus.
Aristakes,m,"Արիստակես, Արիստակէս(Armenian)",Ancient Armenian|Armenian,[ɑ.ɾis.tɑ.ˈkɛs](Eastern Armenian)|[ɑ.ɾis.dɑ.ˈɡɛs](Western Armenian),,,,,,,"Possibly an Armenian form of Aristarchos, or perhaps from a Middle Persian word meaning ""resurrection of the dead"" (Persian رستاخیز‎ (rastâxiz)). This was borne by a 4th-century Armenian saint who was a son of Gregory the Illuminator (the religious leader traditionally credited with Christianizing Armenia). This name has been used from the 4th century CE until now."
Aristander,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aristandros. Aristander was the name of Alexander the Great's favorite seer.
