Aslamaz,m,ასლამაზ(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. It is probably of Persian or Turkic origin, but it is also possible that it is of Kartvelian origin, in which case it is likely related to the Georgian adjective ლამაზი (lamazi) meaning ""beautiful, handsome"".In Georgia, this name was widespread in the 16th and 17th century, but it has since fallen out of use."
Aslambek,m,Асламбек(Chechen),Chechen,,,,,,,,"The first element is derived from Arabic áslama ""to submit, to surrender"" (and is thus etymologically related to the word islam and the name Eslam). The second element of this name is derived from the Ottoman Turkish military title beg (also bey) meaning ""lord, chief(tain), master"", making it the Turkish equivalent of the Arabic-Persian military title amir, emir. The etymological origin of this military title is uncertain, however. Some experts theorize it is derived from Old Turkic bég, which itself is probably ultimately derived from Proto-Turkic bek meaning ""firm, solid, stable"". Others theorize it is ultimately derived from Old Iranian baga (possibly via Sogdian beĝe) meaning ""God, lord, master"". Also compare Uzbekistan, which is the name of a country in Central Asia."
