Astykrates,m,Ἀστυκράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""city, town"" and κράτος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength""."
Astylochos,m,Ἀστύλοχος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Ἀστύλοχος, derived from Greek ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""city, town"" and λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Astylochus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Astylochos. In Greek mythology, this is the name of one of the suitors of Penelope."
Astylos,m,"Ἀστύλος, Ἄστυλος, Ἀστῠ́λος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἄστυλος (astylos) meaning ""without pillar, without support"". It consists of the Greek negative prefix ἀ (a) combined with the Greek noun στῦλος (stylos) meaning ""pillar, support"".A known bearer of this name was the ancient Olympic athlete Astylos of Croton (5th century BC)."
Astymedon,m,Ἀστυμέδων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""city, town"" and μέδων (medon) meaning ""ruler"", itself from μέδω (medo) meaning ""to protect, to rule over""."
Astymedousa,f,Ἀστυμέδουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Greek form of Astymedusa.
Astymedusa,f,Ἀστυμέδουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Astymedousa, which is the feminine form of Astymedon. In some Greek myths this name belongs to a princess of Mycenae who is a later wife of Oedipus."
Astynome,f,Αστυνόμη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Astynomos. Alternatively, the second element could be derived from the related Greek words νομός (nomós) meaning ""district"", from νέμω (nemo) meaning ""I divide, I distribute"", or from νομή (nome) meaning ""a pasture, a grazing"" or ""spreading, multiplication."" This is the name of several women in Greek mythology, including the mother of Calydon by Ares, a woman commonly called Chryseis in the Iliad of Homer, and one of the children of Niobe (a Niobid)."
Astynomos,m,Αστυνόμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from ἄστυ (ástu) meaning ""city, town"" and νόμος (nomos) meaning ""law, custom""."
Astynoos,m,Ἀστύνοος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""town, city"" and νόος (noos) meaning ""mind, thought"". This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including two defenders of Troy."
Astyochos,m,Ἀστύοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀστυόχος (astyochos) meaning ""protecting the city"". It consists of the Greek noun ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""city, town"" combined with the Greek verb ὀχέω (ocheo) meaning ""to bear, to carry, to hold fast, to sustain"", which is closely related to the Greek verb ἔχω (echo) meaning ""to have, to hold, to possess"". Also compare the Greek noun ὀχή (oche) meaning ""prop, support"" as well as ""nourishment, food"" (see Antiochus)."
Astyochus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Astyochos. This was the name of a Spartan naval commander from the 5th century BC.In Greek mythology, Astyochus is the son of Aeolus and a king of Lipara."
Astypalaea,f,Ἀστυπάλαια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/as.ty.pá.lai̯.a/(Attic Greek)|/as.ty.ˈpa.lɛ.a/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀστυπάλαια (Astypalaia) which meant ""ancient city"", derived from Ancient Greek ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""town, city"" and παλαιός (palaios) meaning ""old (in years), aged, ancient"" (compare the related word πάλαι (palai) meaning ""long ago, in days of yore""). This name was borne by a lover of the god Poseidon in Greek mythology."
Astyphilos,m,Ἀστύφιλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄστυ (asty) meaning ""city, town"" combined with φίλος (philos) meaning ""friend, lover""."
Astyphilus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Astyphilos. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 5th century BC."
Astypylos,m,Ἀστύπυλος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""gate of the city"", derived from Ancient Greek ἄστυ (asty) ""city, town"" combined with πύλη (pyle) ""gate, entrance"". In Greek mythology, this was the name of a Paeonian killed by Achilles."
Astypylus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Astypylos. In Greek mythology, Astypylus was a Paeonian killed by Achilles."
