Ate,f,Ἄτη(Ancient Greek)|安殿(Japanese Kanji),"Greek Mythology|Japanese (Rare, Archaic)|Frisian",ə-TAY(English)|/á.tɛː/(Attic Greek)|/ˈa.ti/(Koine Greek)|AH-TE,,,,,,,"Means ""folly, delusion, error"" as well as ""disaster, ruin, misfortune, doom"" and ""bane, pest"" in Ancient Greek. In Greek mythology she was the goddess (daimona) of mischief, blind folly, and delusion, leading men down the path to ruin while the Litai (prayers) follow and try to mitigate damage. By later accounts, she avenges misdeeds and inflicts punishments on the wicked. In the Iliad she was called the eldest daughter of Zeus, while in Theogeny she is one of the many children of Eris."
Atea,,,Polynesian Mythology|Polynesian|Albanian|Māori,at-tay-uh|AT-TEH-A,,,,,,,"Atea is a creator deity in several Polynesian cultures. Atea means ""clear, white"" in different Polynesian languages. Atea, a shapeless being, was the giver of light to the universe. This gave rise to Rangi and Papa, who's children eventually divided their parents into heaven and earth. In other creator stories Atea is the husband of Papa. Atea, as a Polynesian concept, can be considered equivalent to the Big Bang."
