Atymnius,m,Ἀτύμνιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀτύμνιος (Atymnios), of uncertain etymology. It may be a compound name that consists of the Greek negative prefix ἄ- (a-) combined with Greek τυμνία (tumnia) or (tymnia) meaning ""rod, stick, wand"". The latter might be an Anatolian loanword, as this word also exists in the Carian and Lycian languages, with the same meaning. This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a Trojan warrior, a companion of Sarpedon, and a son of Cassiopeia, either by her husband king Phoenix or by the god Zeus."
