Autochthe,f,Αὐτόχθη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly a feminine form of Autochthon. This was the name of a mythical Mycenaean princess, a daughter of Perseus and Andromeda."
Autochthon,m,Αὐτόχθων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek αὐτόχθων (autochthon) meaning ""sprung from the land itself; indigenous, native"", composed of αὐτός (autos) ""self"" and χθών (chthon) ""earth, soil"". In Plato's myth of Atlantis, Autochthon was one of the ten sons of Poseidon and Cleito, twin brother to Mneseus."
Autocles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Autokles. This name was borne by an Athenian general from the 5th century BC.
Autocrates,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Autokrates. This name was borne by an ancient Athenian comic poet.
Autodice,f,Αὐτοδίκη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek αὐτός (autos) meaning ""self"" combined with δίκη (dike) meaning ""justice, custom, order"". Compare the Greek adjective αὐτόδικος (autodikos) meaning ""with independent jurisdiction, with one's own law-courts""."
Autokles,m,Αὐτοκλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""glory itself, personified glory"", derived from Ancient Greek αὐτός (autos) meaning ""self"" combined with κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Autokrates,m,Αὐτοκράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""ruling by oneself, absolute, independent"", derived from Ancient Greek αὐτός (autos) meaning ""self"" combined with κρατος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength"". Also compare the English word autocrat, which derives from the same roots."
Autolekythos,m,,Ancient Greek|History,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek αὐτολήκυθος (autolekythos) (composed of αὐτός (autos) meaning ""self"" and λήκυθος (lekythos) meaning ""lekythos; oil flask or bottle"") meaning ""one who carries his own oil-flask"", which had connotations of ""shabby person, beggar"" as well as the sexual euphemism of ""well-hung"". This was the name of an Indian slave of Favorinus, a Roman sophist and skeptic philosopher."
Autólico,m,,Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,Spanish and Portuguese form of Autolycus.
Autolico,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Autolycus.
Autolycus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Autolykos. In Greek mythology, Autolycus is a renowned thief and the maternal grandfather of Odysseus."
Autolykos,m,Αὐτόλυκος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Greek αὐτός (autos) meaning ""self"". The second element is a little bit uncertain, in that there are two possibilities available for it. The first possibility is that it is derived from Greek λυκου (lykou) ""of a wolf"", which itself is ultimately derived from Greek λύκος (lykos) ""wolf"". The second possibility is that it is derived from Greek λύκη (lyke), which can mean ""light"" as well as ""morning sunshine""."
Automate,f,Αὐτομάτη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Ancient Greek αὐτόματος (automatos) meaning ""self-willed, unbidden, spontaneous"". This was the name of one of the Danaids in Greek mythology."
Automedon,m,Αὐτομέδων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek αὐτός (autos) meaning ""self"" combined with the Greek noun μέδων (medon) meaning ""ruler"" (see Medon).This name was borne by a Greek tyrant of Eretria (4th century BC) and a Greek poet (lived somewhere between the 1st century BC and the 1st century AD).In Greek mythology, Automedon is the name of the charioteer of Achilles."
Autonoë,f,Αὐτονόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/au̯.to.nó.ɛː/(Attic Greek)|/aɸ.to.ˈno.i/(Koine Greek)|/af.to.ˈno.i/(Byzantine Greek)|/ɔː.ˈtɒn.oʊ.iː/(English),,,,,,,"Feminine form of Autonoos (see Autonous). In Greek mythology this was the name of the daughter of Cadmus, founder of Thebes and Harmonia, one of the Bacchae in Euripides' play of the same name."
Autonom,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Romanian form of Autonomus.
