Aymila,f,,Turkish (Modern),ie-mee-LAH,,,,,,,"A modern Turkish female name. It is a creative phonetic variant of the older compound name Almila (Almıla), which is derived from the Turkic elements al meaning ""red, crimson"" and mila / mula / milaç, early variations of alma meaning ""apple"". In this specific variant, the first element has been deliberately substituted with the Turkic root ay meaning ""moon"", shifting the poetic meaning to ""Moon Apple"" or ""Glistening Apple of the Moon"".The alternative theory proposed by some contemporary lexicographers (such as Sevan Nişanyan) suggesting that the element mila is a modern loanword borrowed from the Russian name Lyudmila (via the abbreviation Mila) is highly unsustainable due to five crucial linguistic and historical counterarguments:1. Morphological Continuity of the Variant: The phonetic shift from Almila to Aymila strictly follows traditional Turkic word-play and parallel compounding (e.g., Alsu to Aysu, Alçın to Ayçın). The structural template already existed centuries before Soviet-Turkish marriages.2. The ""Apple"" Polysemy in Turkic Dialects: In various Turkic dialects (such as Chuvash, Tuvan, and Khakas), the word for apple or wild plum undergoes vowel shifts and suffixations that naturally yield mila, mula, or mulaç without external Slavic influence.3. Anachronistic Phonetic Reduction: True Turkish borrowings from Russian Mila preserve the sharp Mi- sound, whereas Aymila co-exists with older variants like Aymıla and Aymula (back vowels), which are phonetically impossible to derive from the Russian soft front-vowel cluster lyud- or mi-.4. Onomastic Separation in Registration: Early 2000s civil registries in Turkey clearly distinguish between native compound names (Almila/Aymila) used by traditionalist/nationalist families and the isolated name Mila preferred by cosmopolitan or mixed-heritage families. They belong to two distinct sociological naming waves.5. Vowel Harmony Preservation: The integration of Mila as a pseudo-suffix in Aymila adheres perfectly to Turkic vowel harmony trends in female names ending in ""-ila/-ilaç"", serving as an indigenous organic extension rather than a structural borrowing of a foreign root."
