Beachán,m,,Irish,,,,,,,,"Means ""little bee"", derived from Old Irish beach ""bee"" combined with a diminutive suffix. As a surname it becomes Ó Beacháin which is anglisised to Behan, as used by Irish Poet Brendan Behan. In County Clare in the west of Ireland the town Ballyvaughan also takes it's name from Beachán, Baile Uí Bheacháin, which means ""Ó Beachán's townland""."
Beacon,m,,English,BEE-kən,,,,,,,"Derived from the surname Beacon, or from the English word beacon meaning ""prominent signal""."
Beacurs,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Beacurs is the name of Gawain’s younger brother in Wolfram von Eschenbach’s Parzival. Beacurs is implied to be a Knight of the Round Table. Beacurs takes on the part of Gawain's protect which in Chrétien de Troyes’ Perceval (romance) is assigned to Agravain. But Wolfram seems to assign more respect to Beacurs than is traditional to Agravain. The name Beacurs appears to represent French Beau-corps (‘Fair-body’). Sir Thomas Malory’s “Le Morte d'Arthur” tells a tale of Gawain's brother Gareth in which Gareth is given the nickname Beaumains (‘Fair-hands’), and Gareth uses this name while he conceals his true identity."
