Belisa,f,,Galician (Rare),,,,,,,,Diminutive of Isabel.
Bélisaire,m,,"French (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,French form of Belisarius.
Belisama,f,,Celtic Mythology,,,,,,,,"Belisama was a goddess worshipped in ancient Gaul and Britain, associated with lakes and rivers, fire, crafts and light, who was identified with Minerva in the interpretatio romana. Her name is of debated origin and meaning: theories include a derivation from Gaulish bel ""strong"" and the superlative suffix -isama and a derivation from the root bel ""bright"" and the superlative suffix -isama ( based on this she has also been speculatively claimed as companion of Belenus, whose name seems to contain the same root)."
Belisaria,f,,"Portuguese (Rare)|Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)|Italian (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Belisario.
Belisario,m,,Spanish|Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Spanish and Italian form of Belisarius.
Bélise,f,,French (Rare)|Literature|Theatre,,,,,,,,Gallicized form of Belisa. This name was used on one of the characters in Molière's play Les Femmes savantes (1672).
Belise,f,,English (Rare)|Portuguese (Brazilian),,,,,,,,English and Brazilian Portuguese adoption of Bélise.
Belisencia,f,,Medieval English (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Belisent.
Belisent,f,,Medieval English|Carolingian Cycle|Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"From an Old French form of Belissendis. It is borne by a legendary daughter of Emperor Charlemagne in the poems Ami et Amile (c.1200) and Otinel or Otuel a Knight (c.1330). In the late 13th-century Arthurian tale Arthour and Merlin, Belisent is King Arthur's half-sister, the wife of Lot and mother of Gareth. Alfred Lord Tennyson also used the spelling Belicent in his Arthurian epic Idylls of the King (1859)."
Belislav,m,Белислав(Bulgarian),Bulgarian,,,,,,,,"From old Slavic bělъ meaning ""white"", possibly also idiomatically ""good"", and slava meaning ""glory""."
Belisma,f,,Celtic Mythology,,,,,,,,"Belisama (often spelled Belisma) is a Gaulish and British Celtic goddess of light; she is the embodiment of illumination and heat, linking the powers of the sun and moon. Her name translates to ""the brightest one"" or ""the most powerful"".The Romans equated her with Minerva due to shared domains like wisdom and craftsmanship. Like her Roman equivalent, she is a patroness of smiths, artisans, and craftsmanship.She was historically tied to specific bodies of water, most notably the River Ribble (known by the Romans as the Belisama) in northwestern England, and possibly the Mersey."
Belissa,f,,Spanish (Latin American)|Portuguese (Brazilian)|American (Hispanic),,,,,,,,Variant of Belisa.
Belissenda,f,,Medieval Occitan,,,,,,,,Not available.
Belissendis,f,,Medieval German (Latinized),,,,,,,,"A dithematic name formed from the name elements bili ""soft, tender, smooth, peaceful"" and swinth ""strong"". The first name element may also refer to Bil, the girl in the moon in Nordic mythology."
Belita,f,,Medieval Basque|Portuguese|Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Medieval Basque name of uncertain origin and meaning. It was first recorded in Artaxoa (in the Navarre area) in 1330.
Beliyu-work,f,በልዩ ወርቅ(Amharic),Amharic,,,,,,,,"Means ""especially gold"" in Amharic."
