Beothach,m,,Irish Mythology,,,,,,,,"Beothach is a prominent figure in early Irish mythology and the Lebor Gabála Érenn (The Book of Invasions). He was the grandson of Nemed, the leader of the Nemidians who colonized Ireland.Beothach is sometimes referred to as the son of Iarbonel Fáidh (the Prophet), who was the son of Nemed. His bloodline is depicted as a direct link between the early biblical/mythical colonizers of Ireland and the ancient, magical pantheon of gods and heroes who feature prominently in the Ulster and Mythological Cycles.In some mythological accounts, Beothach is cited as one of the three great leaders (alongside his contemporaries Simeon and Briotán) who divided Ireland into three distinct parts following the collapse of the earlier Nemedian civilization.Beothach’s son, Iobáth, is recorded as the ancestor of the Tuatha Dé Danann. After the Nemidians were defeated in Ireland, Iobáth led his clan into the northern reaches of Europe. There, his descendants spent generations studying Druidism, magic, and philosophy before returning to conquer Ireland as the mystical, divine race known as the Tuatha Dé Danann"
Bép,m,,Vietnamese (Rare),/ʔɓɛp̚˧˦/|/ʔɓɛp̚˦˧˥/|/ʔɓɛp̚˦˥/,,,,,,,Meaning unknown.
Bep,,,Dutch|Limburgish,/ˈbɛp/,,,,,,,"Short form of Elisabeth and Elizabeth (strictly feminine). As a unisex name, Bep is usually a short form of names that contain the Germanic element beraht meaning ""bright"", such as Albert and Gijsbert for men and Berta/Bertha and Lamberta for women.In Limburgish, the name is strictly feminine. In addition to the aforementioned women's names, Bep is also used as a short form of Barbara there.A notable Dutch bearer of this name was Bep Voskuijl (1919-1983). She was one of the people who helped conceal Anne Frank (1929-1945) and her family from Nazi persecution."
