Capaneus,m,Καπανεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/kə.ˈpæn.juːs/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Καπανεύς (Kapaneus), which is possibly derived from Ancient Greek καπάνη (kapane) meaning ""chariot"". Alternatively, it could derived from σκαπάνη (skapane) meaning ""digging, spadework"" or ""spade, mattock, digging tool"", or perhaps from καπνός (kapnos) meaning ""smoke"". In Greek mythology, Capaneus was one of the Seven against Thebes, renowned for his great size, strength and arrogance. He was killed by a thunderbolt while attempting to scale the walls of Thebes, after shouting that not even Zeus could stop him from invading."
Capanilli,m,,Nahuatl|Aztec and Toltec Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""one who makes noise"", from Nahuatl capani ""to make a cracking or slapping noise"". This was an epithet of Xolotl, possibly associated with sleep or dreams."
Caparronia,f,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,Caparronia was one of the Vestal Virgins.
Capella,f,,Astronomy,,,,,,,,"This is the brightest star in the constellation Auriga. Its name means ""little she-goat"" from Latin capra ""she-goat"" with a diminutive suffix. In Roman mythology the star represented the goat Amalthea."
