Celusa,f,"Κηλοῦσα, Κήλουση(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek κηλόω (kêloô) meaning ""to have an abnormal delivery"" or perhaps ""to burn""; it could also be a variant form of κηλέω (keleo) meaning ""to charm, enchant, beguile"". This was the name of a naiad or an oread who personified Mount Celusa or Celossa in Phliasia, mother of the river god Asopus by Poseidon in some accounts."
