Ceylon,m,,English (Rare),/sɪ.ˈlɒn/(British English)|/seɪ.ˈlɑn/(American English)|/si.ˈlɑn/(American English),,,,,,,"From the historical name of the British crown colony (present-day Sri Lanka), borrowed from Portuguese Ceilão and ultimately derived from Sanskrit सिंहल (simhala), literally meaning ""lionlike"" and composed of सिंह (simha) meaning ""lion"" and the suffix -ल (-la)."
Ceyran,f,,Azerbaijani,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Azerbaijani noun ceyran meaning ""gazelle, antelope"", which is ultimately of Persian origin (see Jeiran). Also compare the related name Ceylan."
Ceysu,f,,Turkish,jey-SOO|JAY-soo|jā-SOO|tse-soo,,,,,,,"The name Ceysu is a modern Turkish feminine name formed by the combination of two native Turkic elements.The first element, cey, is a phonetic variant and dialectal form of the Old Turkic and Anatolian Turkish word çay, meaning ""stream"" or ""creek"" (cognate with Chagatai çay). This root should not be confused with the homophonous loanword çay (meaning ""tea""), which entered Turkish from Mandarin Chinese via Persian.The second element is the native Turkic word su, meaning ""water"", which is a highly popular root and suffix in Turkish feminine names (such as Aysu, Bensu, or Tansu).Therefore, the combined meaning of Ceysu is ""stream water"" or ""flowing water"". It is important to note that despite the phonetic similarity, Ceysu is etymologically entirely unrelated to names of Arabic, Persian, or Mongolic origins such as Ceyda, Ceylan, or Ceyhun."
Ceyx,m,"Κήυξ, Κήϋξ(Ancient Greek)","Greek Mythology (Rare)|Ancient Greek (Latinized, Rare)",/ˈsiː.ɪks/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κῆϋξ (keyx) referring to an uncertain kind of seabird, possibly meaning ""tern, sea-swallow"", ""gannet"", or ""seagull"". In Greek mythology, Ceyx was the husband of Alcyone. After he was killed in a shipwreck, his wife threw herself into the water, but the gods saved her and turned them both into birds."
