Changqin,m,长琴(Chinese),Chinese Mythology,,,,,,,,"From Chinese 长 (cháng) meaning ""long"" and 琴 (qín) meaning ""zither, lute"". This is the name of a mythological figure found in the Shanhaijing (山海经), or Classic of Mountains and Seas. He is the son of the fire god Zhurong (祝融) who lives on Mount Yao, playing music while accompanied by three five-colored birds. His full name, including his title, is Taizi Changqin (太子长琴) ""Prince Changqin""."
Changqing,,"长卿, 长庆, 长清, 长青, 常青, 常清, etc.(Chinese)",Chinese,/ʈ͡ʂʰɑŋ.t͡ɕʰiŋ/,,,,,,,"From Chinese 长 (cháng) meaning ""long"" or 常 (cháng) meaning ""common, frequent, regular"" combined with 卿 (qīng) meaning ""minister, noble, officer"", 庆 (qìng) meaning ""congratulate, celebrate"", 清 (qīng) meaning ""clear, pure, clean"" or 青 (qīng) meaning ""blue, green, young"". Other character combinations can form this name as well."
