Charybdis,f,Χάρυβδις(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/kə.ˈrɪb.dɪs/(English)|/kʰá.ryb.dis/(Attic Greek)|/ˈxa.ryβ.ðis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology, possibly derived in part from Ancient Greek ῥοῖβδος (rhoibdos) meaning ""rushing noise, rushing motion"". In Greek mythology, this was the name of a sea monster that resided on one side of a narrow strait, opposite to Scylla, sucking in and spitting out a massive whirlpool three times a day and consuming any ships that strayed too close. In some accounts she was a daughter of Gaia and either Pontus or Poseidon; in others she was simply a massive whirlpool."
