Chrodechilde,f,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of the Germanic name Hrothildis (see Rothild). This was the original name of Saint Clotilde (for whom the names Rohilde or Rotilde would be more accurate).
Chrodegang,m,,History (Ecclesiastical)|Frankish,,,,,,,,Form of Rotgang borne by an 8th-century Frankish saint.
Chrodobert,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,Chrodobert was an Aleman dux (leader) in the early 7th century.
Chrodogangus,m,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Latinized)",,,,,,,,Latinized form of Chrodegang.
Chrom,,,Popular Culture (Rare),,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek chroma meaning ""color"". This is the name of one of the protagonists of the video game Fire Emblem: Awakening. Variant of Chrome"
Chromace,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,French form of Chromatius.
Chromatios,m,Χρωματίος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun χρῶμα (chroma) meaning ""colour"", which is a word that originally referred to the colour of one's skin (i.e. complexion)."
Chromatius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Chromatios.A notable bearer of this name was the 5th-century saint Chromatius of Aquileia.
Chromia,f,Χρωμία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˈkroʊ.mi.ə/,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek χρῶμα (chroma) meaning ""skin, surface; complexion, colour, pigment"". In Greek mythology, Chromia was a daughter of king Itonus of Iton. In some traditions, she was married to Endymion."
Chromios,m,"Χρόμιος, Χρομίος(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈxro.mi.os/(Koine Greek)|/xro.ˈmi.os/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Most likely derived from Ancient Greek χρόμος (chromos) meaning ""crashing sound"" or ""neighing, whinnying (of a horse)"". It coincides with χρόμιος (chromios), a kind of sea fish apparently named for the creaking or grunting noise it made. Also compare χρῶμα (chroma) meaning ""colour""."
Chromius,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Chromios. This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of them being a son of king Priam."
Chronicle,m,,Obscure,KRAHN-i-kəl,,,,,,,From the English word chronicle.
Chronicles,,,Obscure,KRAHN-i-kəlz,,,,,,,From the English word chronicles.
Chronis,m,Χρόνης(Greek),Greek,,,,,,,,Short form of Polychronis.
Chrothild,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,A more or less latinized form of Rothild.
Chrothildis,f,,Frankish,,,,,,,,"Chrothildis was a Frankish queen consort by marriage to king Theuderic III. She was formally the nominal regent for her son Clovis IV during his minority from his succession in 691 until her own death in 692, though in reality the de facto regent was Pepin of Herstal."
Chrotilda,f,,Germanic|History,,,,,,,,"Variant of Chrothild. Chrotilda was the daughter of Clovis I (a Frankish king) and wife of Amalaric, a 6th-century king of the Visigoths."
Chrotlind,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,A more or less latinized form of Rodelind.
Chrotrud,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,A more or less latinized form of Rotrud.
