Cleoboea,f,Κλεόβοια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Κλεόβοια (Kleoboia), derived from Ancient Greek κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"" and βοῦς (bous) meaning ""ox, cow, cattle"". Alternatively, the second element could be interpreted as βοάω (boao) meaning ""to shout, to proclaim"" (also ""to roar"" and ""to howl"" when used of the wind). Compare Euboea, Meliboea and Periboea. This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology."
Cléobule,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized)|History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Cleobulus.
Cleobulina,f,Κλεοβουλίνη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Κλεοβουλίνη (Kleobouline), derived from the name Kleoboulos combined with the feminine adjectival suffix -ινη (-ine). This was the name of a 6th-century BC Greek poet from Lindos, a small town on the island of Rhodes. She was the daughter of Cleobulus, one of the Seven Sages of Greece. According to Plutarch, her real name was Eumetis."
