Conaing,m,,Medieval Irish,/ˈkˠʌn.ɪŋ/,,,,,,,"Its meaning is uncertain though it likely relates to con (""hound, wolf""), a common element in Irish names of its time.Though having fallen out of use as a given name, Conaing is still seen in a modern context in the surname Ó Conaing (anglicised as O'Gunning, Gunning, O'Conaing, and Cunning).Historically, the name was notable in the town of Chaisleán Uí Chonaing (Castleconnell) in County Limerick, where the Ó Conaing family held chief seats as lords of Aos Gréine in medieval times. This Limerick branch is linked to Brian Ború, being descendents of Donnchuan (a brother of his).The name is also linked to Lough Reane in Fíonach (Fenagh) in County Carlow through a local tale involving Conaing II Beag Eaglach, a High King of Ireland. He is said to have once resided there, and Conaing's son, Cobhthach, drowned in the nearby lake one day while out with his nurse, Rian. The lake was named after Rian to honour her efforts in trying to save him."
