Conomor,m,,Breton Legend|History|Medieval Breton (?),,,,,,,,"From a Brythonic name, possibly *Cunomāros, derived from Common Celtic *kwon- ""hound"" or *kuno- ""high"" and *māros ‎""great"". This was the name of Conomor the Cursed, a 6th-century king of Domnonée (modern-day northern Brittany) notorious for his cruelty, who was ultimately excommunicated at the behest of Saint Samson of Dol. He survives as a villain in Breton folklore, and is often considered the basis for the legends of Bluebeard and of King Mark of Cornwall in the tale of Tristan & Isolde."
