Conwall,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Variant of Conval.
Conwenna,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain, probably a Latinized form of a Celtic name (like Cuniovenda, Cunovinda or Cunovinna). According to the 12th-century chronicler Geoffrey of Monmouth, Conwenna was the mother of Brennius and Belinus, two early kings of Britain. In Welsh chronicles (either translations of Geoffrey's Latin into Welsh or else copies from a lost earlier manuscript - perhaps Geoffrey's source), she was called Tanwen (see also Tangwen, Tonwen). Conwenna may be a cognate of the Breton Conven."
Conwoion,m,,Breton,,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton kon ""warrior"" and uuoion ""sincere""."
Conxita,f,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Concepció.
Conyers,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Transferred use of the surname Conyers.Known bearers of Conyers as a given name include the English politician and military commander Conyers Clifford (c. 1566-1599), the English clergyman and author Conyers Middleton (1683-1750) and the American physicist Conyers Herring (1914-2009)."
