Coppe,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Cop.
Coppélia,f,,Theatre|French (Rare),,,,,,,,"The name of a life-sized mechanical doll created by the mysterious Doctor Coppélius in Léo Delibes' comic ballet Coppélia (1870), based on two macabre stories by E. T. A. Hoffmann. The inventor's name is possibly a Latinized form of Yiddish Koppel. Alternatively this name may be inspired by Greek κοπελιά (kopelia) meaning ""young woman"", a dialectal variant of κοπέλα (kopela)."
Copper,m,,English,KAWP-UR,,,,,,,"The name comes from the Greek word Kyprios, the name of the island of Cyprus in the Mediterranean Sea where copper occurs. The Latin, cuprum, (Cu) also means “Metal of Cyprus,” as the Romans had large copper mines on the island."
