Cyndy,f,,English (American)|English (Canadian),SIN-dee(English),,,,,,,Diminutive of Cynthia. Also compare Cyndi.
Cyne,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈky.ne/(Old English),,,,,,,"Short form of names containing the Old English element cyne meaning ""royal, kingly"". The surname Kinsley is derived from the name. Cognate of Kuno."
Cynebeorht,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈky.ne.ˌberxt/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements cyne ""royal"" and beorht ""bright""."
Cyneburh,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Alternate spelling of Cyneburg, or Cyneburga."
Cynedyr,m,,Arthurian Cycle|Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,Character that appears in Culhwch and Olwen.
Cynegar,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements cynn ""rank, family, kin"", or possibly the related cyne ""royal"", and gar ""spear""."
Cynegifu,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements cyne ""royal"" and giefu ""gift""."
Cynegils,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈky.ne.ˌjils/(Old English),,,,,,,"From Old English cyne meaning ""royal"" and gisl meaning ""pledge, hostage"".An alternative Celtic origin has been suggested, with the given meaning of ""grey dog""."
Cynegyð,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old English cyne ""royal"" and guð ""battle"", making it a cognate of Cunigund. This name was borne by a wife of King Wihtred of Kent (c. 670-725) as well as a wife of King Coenwulf of Mercia (died 821)."
Cynehelm,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈky.ne.ˌxelm/(Old English)|[ˈky.ne.ˌheɫm](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the elements cyne ""kingly, royal"" and‎ helm ""helmet"". Used an Anglo-Saxon saint who was a king."
Cynehild,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈky.ne.ˌxild/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from Old English cynn meaning ""family"" (or possibly cyne ""kingly, royal"") and hild meaning ""battle""."
Cynelaf,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements cyne meaning ""royal"" and lāf meaning ""legacy"" (from Proto-Germanic *laibō)."
Cynemund,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈky.ne.mund/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements cyne ""royal"" (related to cynn ""rank, family, kin"") and mund ""protector, guardian"", making it a cognate of Cunimund."
Cynesi,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Form of Cynesige found in the Phillimore edition of Domesday Book.
Cynestan,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old English cyne ""royal"" and stan ""stone""."
Cyneþegn,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,An Anglo Saxon name meaning royal servant/ warrior.
