Deichtine,f,,Irish Mythology,/dʲexʲ.tʲɪ.nʲə/|/dʲexʲ.ˈtʲiː.rʲe/,,,,,,,"Possibly from Old Irish deich, meaning ""ten"", and tine meaning ""fire"" or tíre, ""land, country"". This was the name of the mother of Cúchulainn, whom she conceived with Lugh."
Déicole,,,History (Ecclesiastical)|French (Archaic),,,,,,,,"French form of Deicolus. Even though the saint was male, this name was also occasionally used on women."
Deicolus,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Of uncertain origin and meaning; theories include a Latinization of the Irish Dícuil. Deicolus was a Christian saint of Irish origin who lived in the 7th century during the pontificate of Pope Vigilius. He may have been a brother of Saint Gallus and is said to have accompanied Columbanus to Luxeuil, France. When Columbanus was expelled in 610, Deicolus was too ill to accompany him and remained behind in the Vosges where he built a hermitage in the vale of Orignon. His cult survived in the areas of Lure, Alsace, Bourgogne and Franche-Comté into the twentieth century."
Deicuil,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Deicolus.
Deidad,f,,"Spanish (Latin American, Rare)",[d̪ei̯.ˈð̞að̞](Latin American Spanish),,,,,,,"Means ""deity"" in Spanish, a word derived from Latin deitās (which in turn was coined by Augustine of Hippo, who derived it from Latin deus meaning ""god"")."
Deidamia,f,Δηιδάμεια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Theatre,,,,,,,,"From Greek Δηιδάμεια (Deidameia), possibly derived from δηιόω (dêioô) ""to destroy"" (cf. Deianira) and δαμάζειν (damazein) ""to tame"". This was the name of several historical women, including the daughter of King Pyrrhus II of Epirus, the last surviving representative of the royal Aeacid dynasty who was assassinated in the Temple of Artemis (c.239/229 BCE). Another was a daughter of Aeacides, king of Epirus and his wife, Queen Phthia, and sister of King Pyrrhus, who was at one time betrothed by her father to Alexander IV, the son of Roxana and Alexander the Great.In Greek mythology, this is another name of Hippodamia, Laodamia; and possibly the mother of Iphicles. Deidamia is also the name of one of King Lycomedes's daughters who gave sanctuary to Achilles, and with whom she had a son Neoptolemus. She is mentioned by Dante in his 'Inferno' (14th century). This name is also borne by the main character in the opera of the same name by Georg Friedrich Händel (1740), which is about the Deidamia who married Achilles."
Deidara,m,デイダラ(Japanese Katakana),Popular Culture,Day-dah-rah|Day-dara|Day-dar-ah,,,,,,,"Deidara means clay bender in japanese-------------------------------------The origin of the name is unknown, however, it is the name of a fictional antagonist from the animated series ""Naruto"".The meaning of ""Deidara"" is mud."
