Deijii,f,"庭司, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,DE:-JEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 庭 (dei) meaning ""garden"" combined with 司 (jii) meaning ""to take charge of; to control; to manage"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Usage of this name is, most likely, influenced by the name and word Daisy."
Deile,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,Local form of Deicolus found in the Bourgogne-Franche-Comté region up until the 1800s.
Deileon,m,Δηϊλέων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δήϊος (deios) meaning ""burning, blazing, destroying, hostile"" and λέων (leon) meaning ""lion"". This was the name of a son of King Deimachus of Thessaly in Greek mythology, also called Demoleon by Plutarch. It was also the name of a Greek soldier in the Trojan war."
Deilochos,m,"Δηΐλοχος, Δηϊλοχος, Δηίλοχος(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Epic Greek adjective δήϊος (deios) meaning ""hostile, destructive"" as well as ""unhappy, wretched"". It is related to the Greek verb δηϊόω (deioo) meaning ""to slay, to cut down"" (see Deianeira).The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Deilochus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Deilochos. In Greek mythology, this is the name of two separate characters who were both slain by Tydeus during the War of the Seven against Thebes."
Deilotte,f,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Deile found in the Bourgogne-Franche-Comté region up until the 1800s.
Deilwen,f,,Welsh,DAYL-wen,,,,,,,"Means ""white leaves"" from Welsh dail ""leaves"" (singulative deilen) combined with gwen ""white, fair, blessed""."
Deimachus,m,"Δηΐμαχος, Δαΐμαχος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),/di.ˈɪm.ək.əs/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of the Greek name Δηΐμαχος (Deimachos), derived from δηιόω (deioo) meaning ""to slay"" (compare Deilochos) and μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"". A noted historical bearer was Deimachus, a 3rd-century BC Greek of the Seleucid Empire who became an ambassador to the court of Bindusara 'Amitragata' (son of Chandragupta Maurya) in Pataliputra in India. He apparently wrote extensively on ancient India and is quoted as a reference in geographical matters, but his works are now lost. This was also the name of several men in Greek mythology."
Deimantas,m,,Lithuanian,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Deimantė. Known bearers of this name include the Lithuanian soccer players Deimantas Bička (b. 1972) and Deimantas Petravičius (b. 1995).
Deina,f,,"Basque (Hispanicized, ?)|Spanish (?)",,,,,,,,Allegedly a Hispanicized form of Deiñe.
Deinarchos,m,Δείναρχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/děː.nar.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/ˈði.nar.xos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek δεινός (deinos) meaning ""terrible, fearsome; mighty, powerful"" and ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""ruler, leader, master""."
Deincora,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Deiñe,f,,Basque,,,,,,,,"Derived from either Basque dei ""call; announcement"" or deikunde ""announcement; Annunciation"", this name is considered a Basque equivalent of Anunciación."
Deineira,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Variant of Deianira.
Deinna,f,,American (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Deino,f,Δεινώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek δεινός (deinos) meaning ""fearful, terrible, dread, dire"" or δίνη (dine) meaning ""whirlpool, eddy"". This was the name of several characters in Greek myth."
Deinokrates,m,Δεινοκράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""terrible power"", derived from the Greek elements δεινός (deinos) ""fearful, terrible"" and κρατος (kratos) ""power""."
Deinolochos,m,Δεινόλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/deː.nó.lo.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/ði.ˈno.lo.xos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek δεινός (deinos) meaning ""terrible, fearsome; mighty, powerful"" and λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush, trap""."
Deinomache,f,Δεινομάχη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Deinomachos. This was the name of Alcibiades' mother.
Deinomachos,m,Δεινόμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δεινός (deinos) meaning ""fearful, terrible"" as well as ""mighty, skilful, wondrous"" combined with μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle""."
Deinomenes,m,Δεινομένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""terrible strength"", derived from the Greek elements δεινός (deinos) ""fearful, terrible, marvellous, skilful"" and μένος (menos) ""mind, courage, strength, spirit""."
Deinophilos,m,Δεινόφιλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δεινός (deinos) meaning ""fearful, terrible"" as well as ""mighty, powerful"" combined with φίλος (philos) meaning ""friend, lover; beloved, dear""."
Deinosthenes,m,Δεινοσθένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/deː.nos.tʰé.nɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/ði.nos.ˈθe.nis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek δεινός (deinos) meaning ""terrible, fearsome; mighty, powerful"" and σθένος (sthenos) meaning ""vigour, strength""."
