Delbáeth,m,,Irish Mythology,,,,,,,,"Among the Tuatha Dé Danann, multiple Delbáeths appear across generations in the Lebor Gabála Érenn (Book of Invasions), a 11th-century pseudo-historical compilation.One of the most notable Delbáeths is a Fomorian king, father of Elatha (also Elotha or Ealadha), who himself sired Bres, the half-Fomorian king of the Tuatha Dé Danann during the Second Battle of Mag Tuired. This Delbáeth is depicted as a ruler whose lineage bridges the rival supernatural factions through intermarriages, such as Elatha's union with Ériu of the Tuatha Dé Danann.Another Delbáeth, son of Elada (a Tuatha Dé Danann figure with Fomorian ties), married Ernmas (daughter of Etarlam and granddaughter of Nuadu Airgetlám) and fathered Fiacha, whose daughters—Ériu, Banba, and Fódla—personify Ireland and feature prominently in the Milesian invasion narrative, where they name the island.Yet another is Delbáeth mac Ogma, son of Ogma (the god of learning) and Étan (daughter of Dian Cecht, the healer), who succeeded the Dagda as High King of Ireland and ruled for ten years before dying in the Second Battle of Mag Tuired against the Fomorians. He is the father of the triplets Brian, Iuchar, and Iucharba (also known as sons of Tuirenn in some traditions), as well as Fiacha and Ollam, and is sometimes linked to the mother-goddess Danand; his descendants include the war-furies such as Badb, Macha, and the Morrígan in certain genealogies."
