Devadatta,m,देवदत्त(Sanskrit),Buddhism|Sanskrit,/ˈd̪eː.ʋɐ.d̪ɐt̪.t̪ɐ/(Sanskrit),,,,,,,"Means ""god-given"" from Sanskrit देव (deva) meaning ""god"" and दत्त (datta) meaning ""given, granted"". In Buddhist tradition this is the name of a cousin and brother-in-law of Siddhartha Gautama (the Buddha). By some accounts he was originally a disciple of the Buddha but is cast out after growing jealous of his cousin. He attempts to murder the Buddha numerous times and eventually forms a group of breakaway monks to try to divide the Buddhist community (which ultimately fails). Before dying of illness, he realises the error of his ways and attempts to apologise to the Buddha, only to be sucked into the ground and sent to naraka (hell)."
