Devayani,f,देवयानी(Sanskrit)|ദേവയാനി(Malayalam)|దేవయాని(Telugu)|ದೇವಯಾನಿ(Kannada)|தேவயானி(Tamil),Hinduism|Malayalam|Telugu|Kannada|Tamil,/d̪eː.ʋɐ.ˈjɑː.n̪iː/(Sanskrit)|/d̪eː.ʋɐ.ˈjaː.n̪i/(Malayalam),,,,,,,"Means ""vehicle of the gods"" or ""path of the gods"" from Sanskrit देव (deva) meaning ""god"" and यान (yāna) meaning ""carriage, vehicle, leading, conducting"". In the Hindu epic the Mahabharata Devayani is the daughter of the god Shukra and a wife of Yayati, a king of Chandravamsha (the Lunar dynasty)."
