Dictynna,f,Δικτυννα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Δίκτυννα (Diktynna) which was associated with Greek δίκτυα (diktya) ""hunting nets"". This was an epithet of the Cretan goddess Britomartis, allegedly given for the fishermen's nets into which she leaped from Mount Dikte on Crete. According to legend she was originally a mortal woman beloved by King Minos; fleeing his attentions, she leaped into the sea and was transformed into the goddess in the moment of her death."
Dictys,m,Δικτυς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"From Greek Δίκτυς (Diktys) meaning ""of the nets"", from δίκτυον (diktyon) ""fishing-net"". This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, notably the fisherman of the island Seriphos who ""with his net drew to land the chest in which were enclosed Perseus and his mother Danaë""."
