Diopatre,f,Διοπάτρη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δῖον (dion) meaning ""divine, noble"" and πατήρ (pater) meaning ""father"". This was the name of a nymph of Mount Othrys in Greek mythology; when the youth Cerambus spread rumours that she and her sisters were actually daughters of the river Spercheus and Deino, as opposed to Zeus, and that the god Poseidon transformed the nymphs into poplar trees in order to seduce Diopatre without interference, they turned him into a beetle in outrage."
Diope,f,Διόπη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology. Perhaps derived from Ancient Greek διόπη (diope) meaning ""ear ring"" (from διά ""through, between"" and ὀπή ""opening, hole""), from Διός (Dios) ""of Zeus"" and ὄψ (ops) ""face, eye"", or from δίοπος (diopos) ""ruler, commander, captain of a ship"". In Greek mythology, Diope was a daughter of Triptolemos and mother of Eumolpos."
Diopeithes,m,Διοπείθης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek Διός (Dios) meaning ""of Zeus"" combined with the Greek verb πείθω (peitho) meaning ""to persuade, to convince"" as well as ""to obey, to yield to"" and ""to believe, to trust (in)"".This name was borne by an Athenian general from the 4th century BC, who is thought to have been the father of the poet Menander."
Diophanes,m,Διοφάνης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/di.o.pʰá.nɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/ði.o.ˈɸa.nis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/daɪ.ˈɒ.fən.iːz/(English)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek Διός (Dios) meaning ""of Zeus"" and φανης (phanes) meaning ""appearing""."
