Dofri,m,,Old Norse|Icelandic|Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from the Old Norse toponym Dofrar, which itself is probably related to Proto-Slavic *dьbrь (dubri) ""valley, ravine"" and Proto-Celtic dubros ""water; dark"" (see dwfr), both deriving from Proto-Indo-European *dʰubʰrós ""deep"". Alternatively, it could be from Old Norse dofi ""numbness, torpor"", perhaps with the meaning ""lazy one"", or from daufr ""deaf"". In Norse mythology this is the name of a giant who lived in the Dofrafjall (Dovrefjell) mountain range."
