Dolgþrasi,m,,Old Norse|Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from dolg (""hostility, battle"") and þrasa (""to snort, to boast""). This is the name of a dwarf (also called Dolgþvari) in Norse mythology."
Dólgþvari,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Variant of Dolgþvari.
Dolgþvari,m,,Old Norse|Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from dolg (""battle"") and þvari (""staff, sword, spear""). This is the name of a dwarf (also called Dolgþrasi) in Norse mythology."
Dolguuna,f,Долгууна(Yakut),Yakut,,,,,,,,"From долгун (dolgun) meaning ""wave""."
Doli,f,דוֹלִי(Hebrew),"Croatian|Navajo|Hebrew (Modern, Rare)",DAW-lee,,,,,,,Croatian form of Dolly.
Dolina,f,,Scottish,,,,,,,,"Contracted form of Donaldina as well as a Latinate formation based on Gaelic Dolag, itself a feminine diminutive of Donald."
Dolinde,f,,French (Archaic)|Dutch (Rare)|Afrikaans (Rare),,,,,,,,"Archaic French vernacular form of Adelinde found in the Poitou-Charentes region. Today, the name is occasionally found in the Netherlands and in Afrikaans-speaking South Africa. In this case, however, the origin of the name is uncertain; it might be an adoption of the French name, but it is equally possible that it is a modern coinage, perhaps based on Dolly and names that end in -linde."
Dolion,m,Δολίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δόλιος (dolios) meaning ""crafty, deceitful, treacherous"" (see Dolios). In Greek mythology, Dolion was the son of the god Silenus and nymph Melia, and was the eponymous founder of the Doliones tribe."
Dolios,m,Δόλιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δόλιος (dolios) meaning ""crafty, deceitful, treacherous"", which is ultimately derived from δόλος (dolos), meaning ""bait, trick; deceit, guile, treachery, trickery"". This was the name of a gardener slave of Penelope in the Odyssey."
Dolius,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Dolios. In Greek mythology, Dolius is one of Penelope's slaves."
Dolkar,f,སྒྲོལ་དཀར(Tibetan),Tibetan|Bhutanese|Buddhism,,,,,,,,"From Tibetan སྒྲོལ་དཀར (sgrol-dkar) derived from སྒྲོལ (sgrol) meaning ""to liberate, to save"" (referring to the bodhisattva Tara 2) and དཀར (dkar) meaning ""white"". This is the Tibetan name for the White Tara, one of the manifestations of Tara."
Dolker,f,སྒྲོལ་དཀར(Ladakhi),Ladakhi,,,,,,,,Ladakhi form of Dolkar.
