Domicelė,f,,Lithuanian,,,,,,,,"Derived from medieval Latin domicella, which is the feminine form of domicellus, which literally means ""little master"" and was a term used to denote a young nobleman and/or junker. The term is a contraction of dominicellus, which is a diminutive of the Latin noun dominus meaning ""master (of the house), lord"".With that said, you might also want to take a look at the name Domitilla for comparison, as not only is it etymologically related, but it has occasionally also been found spelled as Domicilla and even Domicella.Lastly, a known Lithuanian bearer of this name was the graphic artist and book illustrator Domicelė Tarabildienė (1912-1985)."
