Draupnir,m,,Norse Mythology|Icelandic,[ˈdrɔup.nez̠](Norse Mythology),,,,,,,"The name of a gold ring that was able to multiply itself in Norse mythology, with eight new rings appearing every ninth night. It was created by the dwarf Brokkr and gifted to Odin, who eventually laid it on his son Baldr's funeral pyre. The name probably means ""the dripper"", related to Old Norse drjúpa ""to drip, rain, fall in drops"", referring to the new rings ""dripping"" from the original one. As such, it was also used as a metaphor for ""goldsmith"", and a kenning for ""gold"" in skaldic verse. It was also the name of a dwarf mentioned in the Völuspá."
