Duccia,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Duccio.
Duccio,m,,Medieval Italian (Tuscan)|Italian (Tuscan),"DOOCH-choh(Medieval Tuscan Italian, Tuscan Italian)",,,,,,,"Medieval masculine given name recently fashionable in Tuscany. It is a short form of Andreuccio, Leonarduccio (diminutive of Leonardo), Bernarduccio (diminutive of Bernardo), Armanduccio (diminutive of Armando) and other given names ending in -duccio.A famous bearer of this name was the Sienese painter Duccio di Buoninsegna (1255-1318)."
Duce,f,,Medieval English,DOOS(Middle English),,,,,,,Medieval form of Dulcie.
Duchelle,f,,Obscure,/du.ˈʃɛl/,,,,,,,"Perhaps inspired by French duchesse meaning ""duchess""."
Duchess,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Old French from medieval Latin ducissa, from Latin dux, duc- (see Duke)."
Duchomysł,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Polish duch ""spirit, ghost, soul"", which is ultimately derived from Proto-Slavic duxъ ""spirit"". The second element is derived from Polish myśl ""thought"", which is ultimately derived from Proto-Slavic mysliti ""to think""."
Duchosław,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Polish duch ""spirit, ghost, soul"", which is ultimately derived from Proto-Slavic duxъ ""spirit"". The second element is derived from Slavic slav ""glory""."
