Dueyna,f,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Variant of Dueynna.
Dueynia,f,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Variant of Dueynna.
Dueynna,f,"דואינה, דואיניא(Hebrew)",Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,"Means ""lady, mistress"" in Judeo-Spanish, ultimately from Latin domina, meaning ""mistress""."
Dúfa,f,,Norse Mythology|Icelandic (Rare),/ˈtuː.va/(Icelandic),,,,,,,"A poetic word meaning ""pitching wave"" in Old Norse, from Old Germanic dūbaną ""to dive, sink"". It can also derive from the Icelandic and Old Norse word dúfa ""dove, pigeon"". In Norse mythology, Dúfa was one of nine daughters of the jötunn Ægir and goddess Rán, both personifications of the sea."
Dufe,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈduː.fe/(Old English),,,,,,,"Old English name deriving from dūfe ""dove"". This name is used as a female equivalent of Dufenal."
Dufenal,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈdu.fe.nɑl/(Old English),,,,,,,"Old English form of Donald, derived from Old Irish Domnall. This was the name of Scottish kings and an early saint."
Duff,m,,Romansh (Archaic),,,,,,,,Short form of Rudolf via the form Dulf. The name was early on misunderstood as a Romansh form of David.
Duffus,m,,Scottish Gaelic (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Dub.
Duffy,m,,American,DUF-ee,,,,,,,Transferred from the surname Duffy 1 or Duffy 2.
Dúfr,m,,Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Either derived from Old Norse dúfa ""to drive"" or means ""sleepy one"", related to Norwegian duva. This is the name of a dwarf in Norse mythology."
