Elbika,f,Эльбика(Chechen),Chechen,,,,,,,,"Either from Proto-Semitic *ʾil- meaning ""deity, god"" (see El or Allah) or Turkic el meaning ""people, country, nation"" (see Elnur or Eldar) combined with Turkic bika meaning ""lady, girl, mistress""."
Elbilge,f,,Medieval Turkic,el-beel-geh|/el.bil.ɡe/,,,,,,,"Elbilge (Old Turkic: 𐰃𐰠𐰉𐰃𐰠𐰏𐰀) is an ancient Turkic title and personal name composed of two distinct elements: el (or il), representing ""the state,"" ""the realm,"" or ""the organized political community,"" and bilge, meaning ""wise,"" ""learned,"" or ""possessed of deep knowledge.""Historically, it was the title of Elbilge Khatun, the consort of Ilterish Khagan, the founder of the Second Turkic Khaganate in the 7th century. She was the mother of the prominent rulers Bilge Khagan and Kül Tigin. Her significance in Turkic statecraft and ideology is immortalized in the Orkhon inscriptions (specifically the Bilge Khagan and Kül Tigin stelae). The inscriptions state that ""Teŋri yarlığadok üçün, özüm kutum bar üçün kagan olortom. Babam İlteriş Kaganıg, özüm anam İlbilge Katunug Teŋri töpüsinde tutup yüze kötürmüş erinç."" (""Heaven, which had granted the State to my father, Ilterish Khagan, and my mother, Elbilge Khatun, raised them up."") This phrasing underscores the belief that her authority and wisdom were divinely sanctioned, viewing the Khatun not merely as a consort but as a co-architect of the state's revival."
Elbira,f,,Medieval Basque|Basque,,,,,,,,Basque form of Elvira. Elbira Zipitria Irastorza (1906–1982) was an innovative Spanish-Basque educator who pioneered home schools as a means of reviving use of the Basque language at a time when it was prohibited.
Elbire,f,,Basque (Rare),,,,,,,,Coined by Sabino Arana Goiri and Koldo Elizalde as a Basque equivalent of Elvira and Elvire.
