Elik,f,,Turkish,[e-LEEK],,,,,,,"In Turkish, elik primarily refers to the ""mountain goat"" or ""ibex"" (specifically the Capra ibex or Rupicapra rupicapra), often described as ""hooked-horned"" (Tureng). It is a name rooted in nature, symbolizing agility and resilience, predominantly found in the Anatolian provinces of Tokat and Çorum.A secondary linguistic layer comes from the dialectal verb eliklemek, meaning ""to adopt a child"" or ""to raise a child,"" suggesting a name associated with caregiving and chosen family.The name has deep roots across the Turkic world, appearing in various historical and regional forms such as elig (Old Uyghur), elika (Modern Turkish), ilek or ilək (Bashkir/Khakas), yeläg (Old Turkic), and the forms eli, heli, or helig as documented in historical Chinese records regarding Turkic titles.The name is deeply embedded in Turkmen folklore through ""The Legend of the Elik Goat"" (Elik Keçi Efsanesi). In this legend from the Ordu/Sivas region, a Türkmen mother fleeing hardship is forced to leave one of her twin infants in a cave; the child is discovered and nursed by an Elik goat, who acts as a foster mother until the child is eventually reunited with the biological mother (Kültür Portalı, 2013). It may also be related to the Old Turkic title Elig meaning ""king"" or ""ruler.""For parents, the name symbolizes a child who carries a soul as nimble and resilient as the ibex on the Anatolian peaks. It suggests a personality that is both independent, like the creature that navigates the highest crags, and deeply connected to the spirit of ""chosen family"" through its folklore of foster-care and nurturing. To name a child Elik is to bestow upon them a legacy of ""the survivor’s grace""; a reminder that even in the harshest landscapes, one can find a path with agility, strength, and a heart that heeds the call of the wild steppes."
