Enarete,f,Ἐναρέτη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ɪ.ˈnæ.rɪ.tiː/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἐνάρετος (enaretos) meaning ""virtuous, productive"", literally ""in virtue"" from the preposition ἐν (en) ""in"" and‎ ἀρετή (arete) ""virtue"". In Greek mythology Enarete was a daughter of Deimachus who became the wife of Aeolus and ancestress of the Aeolians. She was also called Αἰναρέτη (Ainarete), which may be a variant spelling influenced by the word αἰναρέτης (ainaretes) meaning ""terribly brave"" (from αἰνός ""terribly, exceedingly"" and arete), or perhaps by αἴνη (aine) ""praise""."
