Eoppa,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Member of the house of Wessex and a Christian missionary.
Eopsin,f,업신(Korean Hangul),Korean Mythology,,,,,,,,"The name of the goddess of storage and wealth in traditional Korean religion, who traditionally takes the form of a snake or weasel and protects the home. Her name is derived from 業 (eop) meaning ""profession, work, job"" and 神 (sin) meaning ""god, goddess, spirit"". However, according to other theories her name is derived from the Mongolian word овоо (ovoo) referring to a kind of sacred shrine, or the Japanese 氏神 (ujigama), refering to protective gods, both of which may have been adopted into Korean as eop."
Eorcenbeald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.ken.bæ͜ɑld/(Old English),,,,,,,"Old English form of Archibald, cognate to Germanic Ercanbald. Derived from the Old English elements eorcnan ""precious, genuine"" (from erknaz) and weald ""powerful, night"" and ""authority, leader""."
Eorcenberht,m,,Anglo-Saxon,[ˈe͜.orˠ.ken.ˌberˠxt](Old English),,,,,,,Old English cognate of Erkanberht. This was the name of a 7th-centuryAnglo-Saxon king of the kingdom of Kent
Eorcengota,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"It perhaps means ""Genuine Geat"", it likely comes from the Ancient Germanic elements erkan meaning ""Genuine"" and gaut meaning ""Geat, Goth"", referring to the tribes.Eorcengota was a Kentish princess and nun. She was the daughter of Eorcenberht of Kent and Saint Sexburga."
Eorcenweald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.ken.wæ͜ɑld/(Old English),,,,,,,"From Old English eorcnan ""precious, genuine"" (from erknaz) and weald ""powerful, mighty"" or ""authority, leader""."
Eorin,f,어린(Korean Hangul),Korean,/ʌ.ɾin/,,,,,,,"Derived from Korean 어리다(eorida) meaning ""young""."
Eormenburg,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.men.ˌburɡ/(Old English),,,,,,,"From Old English eormen meaning ""whole, great"" and burg ""fortress"" (making it a cognate of Erminburg)."
Eormengeard,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.men.ˌjæ͜ɑrd/(Old English),,,,,,,"From Old English eormen meaning ""whole, great"" and geard ""enclosure"" from Germanic gardaz (making it a cognate of Ermengard)."
Eormengyð,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.men.ɡyːθ/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from Old English eormen ""whole, great"" and guð ""battle"". This name was borne by a daughter of Eormenred of Kent, son of Eadbald, who ruled as King of Kent from 616 until his death in 640."
Eormenræd,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.men.ræːd/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements eormen ""whole, great"" and ræd ""advice, counsel, wisdom"". This was the name of an Anglo-Saxon King of Kent."
Eormenþryð,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈe͜or.men.ˌθryːθ/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from Old English eormen ""whole, great"" and þryþ ""strength"", making it a cognate of Ermendrud."
Eorpweald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/e͜orp.wæ͜ɑld/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements eorp ""dark, dusky; brown"" and weald ""powerful, mighty"" or ""authority, leader"". This name was borne by Eorpwald of East Anglia, who succeeded his father Rædwald as King of the East Angles."
Eorsebet,f,,Hungarian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Archaic variant of Erzsébet.
Éos,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),/e.os/,,,,,,,French form of Eos.
