Eos,f,,Welsh,/ˈeː.ɔs/,,,,,,,"Means ""nightingale"" in Welsh."
Eòsaph,m,,Scottish Gaelic,/ˈjɔː.s̪əf/,,,,,,,Scottish Gaelic form of Joseph.
Eosphoros,m,Ἑωσφόρος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/he.ɔːs.pʰó.ros/(Attic Greek)|/e.os.ˈɸo.ros/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""bringer of dawn"" or ""morning star, Venus"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἠώς (eos) ""dawn"" (see also Eos) and φερω (phero) ""to carry, to bear"". This was another name of Phosphoros in Greek mythology, personification of the morning star and son of the goddess Eos."
Eosphorus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eosphoros.
Eostre,f,,Anglo-Saxon Mythology,E-ostra|Oostra|Esther|Yestr,,,,,,,"Eostre, or Ostara; Anglo-Saxon goddess of fertility. Foundation of the name Easter."
