Etyah,f,עֶתְּיָה(Hebrew),Hebrew,/ʔet.ˈja/,,,,,,,"Derived from the Hebrew name עֶתְּיָה, composed of the elements ʿet (עֵת) meaning ""time"" or ""moment"" and the theophoric suffix -yah (יָה), referencing the divine name of God. The name can be interpreted to mean ""God's time"" or ""moment of God."" It has roots in traditional Hebrew naming patterns and has been used among Jewish communities, particularly those with Middle Eastern heritage. Related to other theophoric names ending in -yah."
Étyinne,m,,Picard,,,,,,,,Picard form of Étienne.
Etzli,,,Aztec,Eht-Zlee(Classical Nahuatl),,,,,,,Aztec name meaning “Blood”
Euá,f,,New World Mythology,,,,,,,,"Euá is an Orixá (a goddess) of the Brazilian Candomblé. She is a water goddess who manifests as river, rain or mist."
Euaemon,m,Εὐαίμων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/eu̯.ǎi̯.mɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/e.ˈβɛ.mon/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Εὐαίμων (Euaimon), possibly derived from Ancient Greek εὔαιμος (euaimos) meaning ""full-blooded, well-blooded"", from εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" and αἷμα (haima) ""blood"". This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty sons of King Lycaon, a son of Poseidon and younger twin of Ampheres in Plato’s myth of Atlantis, and the son of Ormenus and father of Eurypylus in the Iliad."
Euaeon,m,Εὐαίων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of the Greek name Εὐαίων (Euaion) which meant ""happy in life, fortunate, lucky, blessed"" in Ancient Greek, composed of (eu) ""good, well"" and αἰών (aion) ""life, lifetime, time"". This name was borne by one of the students of Plato."
Euaggelia,f,Ευαγγελία(Greek),Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Ευαγγελία (see Evangelia).
Euagoras,m,Εὐαγόρας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/eu̯.a.ɡó.raːs/(Classical Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek verb εὐαγορέω (euagoreo) meaning ""to praise formally"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek verb ἀγορεύω (agoreuo) meaning ""to orate, to speak publicly"".Alternatively, this name can mean ""good assembly"", in which case it is derived from the aforementioned Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek noun ἀγορά (agora), which can mean ""assembly"" as well as ""market, marketplace"".Euagoras was the name of two kings of Salamis in Cyprus, the earliest of which lived in the 5th century BC."
Euagrios,m,Εὐάγριος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὔαγρος (euagros) meaning ""lucky in the chase, blessed with success (in hunting)"", itself from εὖ (eu) meaning ""good"" and the noun ἄγρα (agra) meaning ""a hunt, a chase"" or ""prey, quarry, catch"" (compare agrios ""wild"")."
Euain,m,,Scottish (?),Yoo-in,,,,,,,Alternate spelling of Euan.
Euainetos,m,Εὐαίνετος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐαίνετος (euainetos) meaning ""much-extolled"", itself from the elements εὖ (eu) ""good"" and αἰνετός (ainetos) ""praiseworthy""."
Eual,m,,"English (American, Rare)",YOO-əl(American English),,,,,,,Likely a variant of Ewell.
Eualdo,m,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Euvaldo.
Euangelion,m,εὐαγγέλιον(Ancient Greek),Biblical Greek (Rare),YOO-ahn-GEHL-ee-on,,,,,,,Greek word for gospel or good news.
Euangelios,m,"Εὐαγγελίος, Εὐαγγέλιος(Ancient Greek)",Late Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Can derive from either the Ancient Greek word εὐαγγέλιος (euangelios) meaning ""giver of glad tidings; that which concerns good news"", or a patronymic form of the given name Ευάγγελος (see Evangelus). This was an epithet of Zeus in Greek mythology. It was also the name of the 8th month of the ancient Asianian calendar, which was used in the Roman province of Asia (also known as Asiana) until the 7th century AD. The territory had first belonged to the Greeks before being annexed by Rome in the 2nd century BC."
Euanor,m,Εὐάνωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant of Euenor.
Euanthes,m,Εὐάνθης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,yoo-AN-theez(Greek Mythology),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek εὐανθής (euanthês) meaning ""blooming, flowery"". It is the masculine equivalent of Euanthe. This name occurs in the 'Odyssey' belonging to the father of Maron, a priest of Apollo at Ismarus in Thrace."
Eubert,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old High German êwa ""time, age, law"" combined with Old High German beraht ""bright."""
Eubha,f,,Scottish Gaelic,,,,,,,,Scottish Gaelic form of Eva.
Eubie,m,,English (American),,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Hubert. Stage name of jazz singer Eubie Blake (Born James Hubert Blake, 1877-1983)."
Eubod,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old High German êwa ""time, age, law"" combined with Gothic biutan ""to offer"" or Old High German boto ""bid, offer."""
Eubonia,f,,Manx (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Of unknown origin and meaning, Eubonia was one of the names for the Isle of Man used by early Irish writers. In the 18th century, the name was used as a feminine given name."
Euboulides,m,Εὐβουλίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Euboulos"" in Greek, derived from the name Euboulos combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Euboulos,m,Εὔβουλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/ěu̯.buː.los/(Attic Greek)|/ˈeβ.βu.los/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὔβουλος (euboulos) meaning ""well-advised, prudent, wise"", which consists of εὖ (eu) meaning""good, well"" combined with βουλή (boule) meaning ""counsel, advice"" as well as ""will, determination""."
Eubrand,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old High German êwa ""time, age, law"" combined with Old Norse brand ""sword."""
