Eumachius,m,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eumachos,m,Εὔμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle""."
Eumachus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eumachos. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 2nd century BC."
Eumaeus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eumaios. In Greek mythology, this is the name of the swineherd of Odysseus."
Eumaios,m,"Εὔμαιος, Εὐμαῖος(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"". The second element is uncertain; it might possibly be derived from the Greek verb μαίομαι (maiomai) meaning ""to seek after, to seek for"" or from the (hitherto unknown) masculine equivalent of the Greek noun μαῖα (maia) meaning ""good mother, foster mother"" (see Maia 1)."
Eumann,m,,Scottish Gaelic,,,,,,,,Scottish Gaelic cognate of Éamonn.
Eumarrah,m,,Indigenous Tasmanian,,,,,,,,Derivative of Murray.
Èume,m,,Medieval Occitan|Occitan (Archaic),EW-me,,,,,,,Occitan form of Elmo.
Eume,m,,"Galician (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"From the name of a river in the north of Galicia, itself from a Celtic word cognate of Latin umeo (""to be wet""), both ultimately from the Proto-Indo-European root *wegʷ (""wet"")."
Eumea,f,,Louisiana Creole,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eumedes,m,Εὐμήδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and μήδομαι (medomai) meaning ""to plan, plot, intend"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty (or fifty-one) grandsons of Thespius fathered by Heracles in Greek mythology, his mother being Lyse."
Eumelos,m,Εὔμηλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ěu̯.mɛː.los/(Attic Greek)|/ˈeβ.mi.los/(Koine Greek)|/ˈev.mi.los/(Byzantine Greek)|/juː.ˈmiː.ləs/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""with good flocks, rich in sheep"" in Greek, derived from εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and μῆλον (melon) meaning ""sheep, goat, beast""."
Eumen,m,,Croatian (Rare)|Bosnian (Rare),,,,,,,,Croatian and Bosnian form of Eumenes.
Eumène,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Eumenes.
Eumene,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Eumenes.
Eumeneia,f,Εὐμένεια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek εὐμένεια (eumeneia) meaning ""goodwill, favour, grace"", a derivative of εὐμενής (eumenes) (see Eumenes)."
Èumenes,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Eumenes.
Eumenés,m,,Czech,,,,,,,,Czech form of Eumenes.
Eumenes,m,Εὐμένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐμενής (eumenes) meaning ""well-disposed, gracious, kindly"", itself derived from Greek εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" combined with Greek μένος (menos) ""power, strength, spirit"". This name was borne by Eumenes of Cardia, a 4th-century BC Greek general and satrap, as well as by Saint Eumenes, a bishop of Gortyna in Crete during the 7th century (some sources say 3rd century)."
Eumenie,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Romanian form of Eumenius.
Eumênio,m,,Portuguese,,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Eumenius via Eumenes.
Eumenio,m,,Italian|Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Eumenios.
Eumenios,m,Εὐμένιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Extended form of Eumenes.
Eumenis,f,Εὐμενίς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Eumenes.
Eumenius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eumenios (see Eumenes).
Eumeniusz,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Eumenius (see Eumenes).
Eumetis,f,"Εὔμητις, Εὐμητίς(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek εὔμητις (eumetis) meaning ""wise, prudent, of good counsel"", a word composed of εὖ (eu) ""good"" and μῆτις (metis) ""wisdom, advice, counsel, skill"". This name was borne by a daughter of Pindar. It was also another name of the poet Cleobulina."
Eumiliò,m,,Medieval Occitan|Occitan (Rare),ew-mi-LYO,,,,,,,Occitan form of Emil.
Eümill,m,,Gallo,,,,,,,,Gallo form of Émile.
Eumir,m,,Spanish (Latin American)|Portuguese (Brazilian),,,,,,,,"Combination of a name that starts with Eu- (such as Eugênio and Eustaquio) with a name that either starts with Mir- (such as Miriã and Mirta) or ends in -mir (such as Ademir).A known bearer of this name is the Brazilian musician and composer Eumir Deodato (b. 1942), who is the father-in-law of the American actor Stephen Baldwin (b. 1966) and grandfather of the American model Hailey Baldwin (b. 1996)."
Eum-jee,f,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eumolpos,m,Εὔμολπος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὔμολπος (eumolpos) meaning ""sweetly singing"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and the Greek noun μολπή (molpe) meaning ""song, dance""."
Eumolpus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eumolpos. In Greek mythology, this was the name of a legendary Thracian king."
Eumon,m,Εὔμων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Probably derived in part from Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good"". This was the name of one of the fifty sons of King Lycaon in Greek mythology."
Eumorphia,f,Εὐμορφία(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""beautiful in form, shapely"", derived from Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good"" and μορφή (morphe) meaning ""shape, form, appearance""."
Eumorphos,m,Εὔμορφος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""fair of form; beautiful, pretty, handsome"", derived from Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good"" and μορφή (morphe) meaning ""shape, form, appearance""."
Eumund,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old High German êwa ""time, age, law"" combined with Old High German mund ""protection."""
Euna,f,,"Irish (Anglicized, Rare)|Scottish Gaelic (Anglicized, Rare)|English (Rare)|English (American)|American (South)",YOO-nah|OO-nah|/ˈju.nə/(English),,,,,,,Anglicized form of Úna and Ùna.
Eunan,m,,Irish|History (Ecclesiastical),/ˈjuː.nən/(Irish),,,,,,,"Anglicized form of Adomnán, an Old Irish diminutive of Ádhamh, used to refer to Saint Eunan or Adomnán of Iona (died 704)."
Eunápio,m,,Ancient Greek (Portuguese-style),/ew.ˈna.pi.u/(Brazilian Portuguese)|/ew.ˈna.pju/(European Portuguese),,,,,,,Portuguese form of Eunapius. Eunápio Peltier de Queirós (1905-1988) was a Brazilian politician.
Eunapio,m,,Italian (Rare)|Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Form of Eunapius.
Eunapios,m,Εὐνάπιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""person from the good glen"" in Greek, from Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good"" combined with νάπη (nape) meaning ""woodland vale, dell, glen"" and the suffix -ιος (ios)."
Eunapius,m,Εὐνάπιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eunapios. This was the name of a 4th-century Greek sophist, rhetorician, and historian from Sardis in the region of Lydia in Asia Minor."
Eunate,f,,Basque,,,,,,,,"From the name of a town in Navarre, Spain where there is a Romanesque church dedicated to the Virgin Mary (the Church of Santa María de Eunate), located on the Way of St. James, a Catholic pilgrimage route."
Eun-bi,f,은비(Korean Hangul),Korean,/ɯn.bi/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" or 慇 (eun) meaning ""careful, anxious, attentive"" and Korean 비 (bi) meaning ""rain""."
Eun-bin,,"은빈(Korean Hangul)|恩斌, 恩彬, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.bin/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" combined with 斌/彬 (bin) meaning ""refined"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Eun-byeol,f,은별(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-BYUL,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" combined with Korean 별 (Byeol) meaning ""star""."
Eun-Byul,f,"은별(Korean Hangul)|銀별, 恩별, etc.(Hanja/Hangul)",Korean,/ɯn.bjʌl/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" or 恩 (eun) meaning ""grace, favour, gratitude"" combined with the native Korean word 별 (byeol) meaning ""star"". This name can be formed by other hanja character combinations as well."
Eun-chae,f,은채(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-CHAY,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 ""kindness, mercy, charity"" and 彩 ""variegated colors""."
Eun-chan,m,"은찬(Korean Hangul)|殷撰, 殷纂, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.t͡ɕʰa̠n/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 殷 (eun) meaning “abundant, flourishing, numerous” combined with 撰 (chan) meaning ""write, compose"" or 纂 (chan) meaning ""edit, compile"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Eunchang,m,은창(Korean Hangul),Korean,,,,,,,,"From 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 ""silver"" and Sino-Korean 昌 ""light of sun; good, proper""."
Euneice,f,,"English (American, Rare)",yoo-NEES(American English),,,,,,,American English variant of Eunice.
Euneike,f,Εὐνείκη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Most likely a variant form of Eunike (see Eunice). However, it is also possible that this name is an independent name on its own, in which case it is derived from Greek ευ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with Greek νείκη (neike) meaning ""quarrel, wrangle, strife"", which is ultimately derived from the Greek verb νεικέω (neikeo) meaning ""to quarrel, to wrangle with"".This name was borne by a Bosporan queen from the 1st century AD."
Eunel,m,,Filipino (Rare),,,,,,,,Possibly a combination of given names containing the sounds eu and nel.
Eunetta,f,,"American (South, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Euna mimicking diminutive forms ending in -etta.
Eunez,f,,"English (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Eunice with the spelling being influenced by Inez.
Eunghyeon,m,응현(Korean Hangul),Korean,,,,,,,,Meaning unknown.
Eun-gwang,m,은광(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-GWANG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 ""kindness, mercy, charity"" and 光 ""light, brilliant, shine; only""."
Eun-ha,f,"은하(Korean Hangul)|銀河, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,UWN-HAH,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" combined with 河 (ha) meaning ""river, stream, creek"" or 銀河 (eunha) meaning ""galaxy"", as well as other hanja combinations."
Eunhae,f,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eun-ho,,은호(Korean Hangul),Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eun-hui,f,은희(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-HEE,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 銀 ""silver"", 恩 ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 殷 ""many, great; abundant, flourishing"" (eun), and 姬 ""beauty"", 熹 ""dim light, glimmer; warm, bright"" or 僖 ""joy, gladness, delight"" (hui)."
Eunhwa,f,은화(Korean Hangul),Korean,,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) ""kindness, mercy, charity"", Sino-Korean 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" or 恩 (eun) meaning ""grace, favour, gratitude"" and 華 (hwa) ""flowery; illustrious"" or 花 (hwa) ""flower; blossoms"". Means silver coin in korean."
Eun-hye,f,은혜(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-HYE,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩惠, meaning ""grace""."
Eunica,f,,English (African)|South African,,,,,,,,Latinate variant of Eunice.
Eunícia,f,,Portuguese (African),,,,,,,,Cognate of Eunicia.
Eunicia,f,,American (Hispanic)|English (Rare)|Portuguese (Brazilian)|Filipino (Rare),yoo-NEE-see-ə(Hispanic American)|e-oo-NEE-syah(Hispanic American),,,,,,,Latiniate form of Eunice.
Eunician,m,,Late Greek (Anglicized),,,,,,,,"Anglicized form of Eunikianos via its latinized form Eunicianus.This name was borne by one of the ten martyrs of the Greek island of Crete, who were tortured and killed during the reign of the Roman emperor Decius (3rd century AD)."
Euniciano,m,,"Spanish (Rare)|Spanish (Latin American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Spanish form of Eunikianos via its latinized form Eunicianus.
Eunicianus,m,,Late Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eunikianos.
Eunicien,m,,"French (Archaic)|French (African, Rare)",,,,,,,,French form of Eunikianos via its latinized form Eunicianus.
Eunicjan,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Eunikianos via its latinized form Eunicianus.
Eunico,m,Ευνοϊκός(Greek),"Italian (Rare, Archaic)",YOO-nee-koh,,,,,,,"Italian variant of the Greek diminutive variant of the Greek adjective εὐνοικός (eunoikos) meaning ""well-disposed, kindly, favorable"". Also compare the very similar-looking Greek adjective ἔνοικος (enoikos) meaning ""inhabitant"", which is derived from the Greek verb ἐνοικέω (enoikeo) meaning ""to dwell in"", itself ultimately derived from the Greek noun οἶκος (oikos) meaning ""house""."
Eunicus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eunikos. This name was borne by an Athenian comic poet from the 4th century BC.
Eunie,f,,English|American (South),YOO-nee,,,,,,,Diminutive of Eunice. This was the birth name of American screenwriter and actress Ouida Bergère (1886-1974).
Euniké,f,,Hungarian|Czech (Rare),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Eunice.
Eunikianos,m,Ευνικιανός(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Late Greek extended form of Eunikos, which was made in the style of Roman cognomina."
Eunikos,m,Εὔνικος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Eunike (see Eunice).
Eunique,f,,African American (Modern),yoo-NEEK,,,,,,,"Variant of Unique, possibly influenced by the spelling of Eunice."
Eun-jae,,"은재(Korean Hangul)|恩在, 恩載, 銀在, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.d͡ʑɛ/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver, money"" combined with 在 (jae) meaning ""located at, exist"" or 載 (jae) meaning ""carry, transport"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Eun-jin,f,은진(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-JEEN,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 銀 (eun) ""silver; cash, money, wealth"" and 珍 (jin) ""precious, valuable, rare""."
Eun-joo,f,은주(Korean Hangul),Korean,OO-N-JU,,,,,,,"From “Eun” meaning kindness, mercy, etc. “Ju” meaning jewel, pearl. Can have different meanings (Eun & Ju) depending what Korean Hanja is being used."
Eun-joon,m,"은준(Korean Hangul)|恩俊, 銀俊, 慇俊, 恩准, 銀准, 慇准, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.t͡ɕun/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangeul 은준 (see Eun-jun).
Eun-jun,m,"은준(Korean Hangul)|恩俊, 銀俊, 慇俊, 恩准, 銀准, 慇准, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.t͡ɕun/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver, money"" or 慇 (eun) meaning ""careful, anxious, attentive"" combined with 俊 (jun) meaning ""talented, handsome"" or 准 (jun) meaning ""approve, permit"""
Eun-mi,f,은미(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-MEE,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 ""silver"" (eun), and 美 ""beautiful, pretty; pleasing"" (mi)."
Eunoe,f,Εὐνοη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"From Greek εὔνους (eunous) meaning ""well-minded, friendly"", which is composed of ευ (eu) ""good, well"" and νοῦς (nous) ""mind"" (see also Eunous, Eunoia). In Greek mythology this was the name of one of the rivers in Hades. It occurs in Dante's epic poem the 'Divine Comedy' (1321); after purging their sins, the penitent souls of Purgatory drink from the river Lethe to forget their misdeeds, after which they drink from the river Eunoe so that they remember their good deeds in life, and are thus able to enter Paradise.Eunoë was also the name of a nymph in Greek mythology, the daughter of Persephone, wife of the Phrygian king Dymas, and mother of Hecuba."
Eunoeus,m,,Late Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eunoios. This name was borne by a 5th-century Greek sophist from Emesa (which is nowadays Homs in Syria), who was a tutor of the Cynic philosopher Sallustius of Emesa."
Eunoia,f,"Εὔνοια, Εὐνόια(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek εὔνοια (eunoia) meaning ""goodwill, favour, kindness"", literally ""well-mindedness"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""good"" and νόος (noos) ""mind, thought""."
Eunóico,m,,Portuguese,,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Eunoikos via its latinized form Eunoicus.
Eunoico,m,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Eunoikos via its latinized form Eunoicus.
Eunoicus,m,,Late Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eunoikos. This name is best known for being the name of one of the forty martyrs of Sebaste (which is nowadays Sivas in Turkey), who all died in 320 AD."
Eunoikos,m,Ευνοϊκός(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐνοικός (eunoikos) meaning ""well-disposed, kindly, favorable"". Also compare the very similar-looking Greek adjective ἔνοικος (enoikos) meaning ""inhabitant"", which is derived from the Greek verb ἐνοικέω (enoikeo) meaning ""to dwell in"", itself ultimately derived from the Greek noun οἶκος (oikos) meaning ""house"".This name is best known for being the name of one of the forty martyrs of Sebaste (which is nowadays Sivas in Turkey), even though he is even better known under the latinized form of his name, namely Eunoicus."
Eunoios,m,Εὐνόιος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὔνοιος (eunoios) meaning ""good-minded"", which is closely related to the Greek noun εὔνοια (eunoia) meaning ""goodwill, favour"". Both words are ultimately derived from Greek ευ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with Greek νοῦς (nous) meaning ""mind"". As such, this given name is basically a variant of Eunous.This name was borne by a 5th-century Greek sophist from Emesa (which is nowadays Homs in Syria), who was a tutor of the Cynic philosopher Sallustius of Emesa."
Eunoïque,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,French form of Eunoikos via its latinized form Eunoicus.
Eunomie,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,"French form of Eunomia. It appears in this form in Book 5, Canto 9 of Edmund Spenser's epic poem The Faerie Queene (1590), in which the Hora or Hour Eunomia is mentioned briefly as 'wise Eunomie', an assistant to Queen Mercilla."
Eunomos,m,Εὔνομος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Ancient Greek adjective εὔνομος (eunomos) meaning ""under good laws, well-ordered"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" and νόμος (nomos) ""usage, custom, law, ordinance"" (see Eunomia)."
Eunomus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eunomos. Notable bearers of this name include a Spartan king (8th century BC) and an Athenian admiral who participated in the Corinthian War (4th century BC).
Eunon,m,Εὔνων(Ancient Greek),Late Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. It might possibly be derived from Greek εὐνῶν (eunon), which is the genitive plural of the Greek noun εὐνή (eune) meaning ""bed"". In the plural, that word can also refer to stones that are thrown from a ship and used as anchors. As such, the meaning of this name could more or less be ""anchor"".Also compare Doric Greek εὐνά (euna) meaning ""marriage"", which is related to the aforementioned εὐνή (eune) meaning ""bed""."
Eunonina,f,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,"From Eunoia, which is derived from Roman eúnoia through Ancient Greek εὔνοιᾰ, meaning ""well mind, beautiful thinking""."
Eunous,m,Εὔνους(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""well-disposed, kindly, friendly"" in Ancient Greek, derived from εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with νόος (noos) meaning ""mind, thought""."
Eunsae,f,은새(Korean Hangul),Korean,/ən.se/,,,,,,,"From 은(銀, eun) meaning ""silver"" or 은(恩, eun) meaning ""grace, charity"" combined with 새(sae) meaning ""bird"" or 새(sae) meaning ""new"" or 새(璽, sae) meaning ""imperial seal"". Other combinations can also form this name."
Eun-saem,f,은샘(Korean Hangul),Korean,,,,,,,,"From the Sino-korean 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" or 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy"", and the native Korean 샘 (saem) meaning ""spring, fountain""."
Eunsan,,은산(Korean Hangul),Korean,UN-SAN,,,,,,,"Eun meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" and San meaning “mountain”"
Eun-sang,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Famous bearer-Lee-eun sang.
Eunseok,m,은석(Korean Hangul),Korean,/ʊn.sʌk/,,,,,,,"From 恩(eun) meaning ""grace"" or 銀(eun) meaning ""silver"" combined with 石(seok) meaning ""stone"" or 錫(seok) meaning ""tin"" or 昔(seok) meaning ""past"" or 奭(seok) meaning ""prosper"". Other hanja combinations can also form this name."
Eun-seong,,은성(Korean Hangul),Korean,UWN-SUNG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 殷 (eun) meaning ""many, great; abundant, flourishing"" combined with 誠 (seong) meaning ""sincere, honest; true, real""."
Eun-so,f,"은서(Korean Hangul)|恩瑞, 恩敍, 恩書, 銀瑞, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.sʌ/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 은서 (see Eun-seo).
Eun-sol,f,"은솔(Korean Hangul)|恩率, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.sʰo̞ɭ/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity” combined with 率 (sol) meaning ""lead, command"" or native Korean 솔 (sol) meaning ""pine (tree)"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Eun-soo,,"은수(Korean Hangul)|恩秀, 銀秀, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.su/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 은수 (see Eun-su).
Eun-sook,f,"은숙(Korean Hangul)|銀淑, 恩淑, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.suk̚/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 은숙 (see Eun-suk).
Eun-su,,"은수(Korean Hangul)|恩秀, 銀秀, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.su/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver, money"" combined with 秀 (su) meaning ""luxuriant, beautiful, elegant, outstanding"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Eun-suh,f,"은서(Korean Hangul)|恩瑞, 恩敍, 恩書, 銀瑞, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.sʌ/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 은서 (see Eun-seo).
Eun-suk,f,"은숙(Korean Hangul)|銀淑, 恩淑, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.suk̚/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver, money"" or 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" combined with 淑 (suk) meaning ""good, pure, virtuous, charming""."
Eunus,m,ইউনুস(Bengali),Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Bengali,/ˈi̯u.nus/,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eunous. This name was borne by a Syrian slave from the 2nd century BC, who was the leader of the slave uprising in the First Servile War."
Eun-won,f,"은원(Korean Hangul)|恩園, 銀援, 恩媛(Korean Hanja)",Korean (Rare),UHN-WON,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) ""kindness, mercy, charity"" 銀 (eun) meaning ""silver"" or 恩 (eun) meaning ""grace, favour, gratitude"" and 園 meaning ""yard, garden; field,"" (won) 援 meaning ""help, assist, aid"" (won) or 媛 meaning ""(beautiful) woman."" (won)"
Euny,m,,Medieval Cornish|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,The name of a 6th-century Cornish saint.
Eunyce,f,,"English (American, Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Eunice.
Eun-ye,f,은예(Korean Hangul)|恩譽(Korean Hanja),Korean (Rare),UN-YEH,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" combined with 譽 (ye) ""fame, reputation; praise""."
Eunys,f,,"Manx|English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Modern English variant and traditional Manx form of Eunice. It coincides with the Manx word eunys ""pleasure; joy; bliss; delight""."
Eun-yu,f,은유(Korean Hangul),Korean,Un-Yu,,,,,,,"""Eun/은""kindness, mercy, charity""Yu/유"" , the name ""Yoo"" can have various meanings depending on the hanja (Chinese characters) used to write it. Some common meanings associated with different hanja for ""Yoo"" include: 1.""abundant"" or ""rich."" , 2. ""deep"" or ""mysterious."" , 3. ""to flow"" or ""to circulate"
Eun-yul,,"은율(Korean Hangul)|恩聿, 銀栗, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɯn.ɲuɭ/,,,,,,,"Combination of an eun hanja, like 恩 meaning ""favour, grace"" or 銀 meaning ""silver,"" and a yul hanja, such as 聿 meaning ""(writing) brush"" or 栗 meaning ""chestnut (tree)."""
Euodios,m,Εὐόδιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Masculine form of Euodia, a derivative of εὖ (eu) meaning ""good"" and ὁδός (hodos) meaning ""road, way, journey""."
Euodos,m,Εὔοδος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""easy to pass, easy to travel"" as well as ""freedom from difficulty, simple"" and ""favourable"" in Ancient Greek, derived from εὖ (eu) ""good"" and ὁδός (hodos) ""way, road, journey, path"". See also Euodia."
Eupeithes,m,Εὐπείθης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐπειθής (eupeithes) meaning ""ready to obey, obedient"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek verb πείθω (peitho) meaning ""to persuade, to convince"" as well as ""to obey, to yield to"" and ""to believe, to trust (in)"".In Greek mythology, Eupeithes is the father of Antinous, the leader of the suitors of Penelope."
Eupha,f,,"English (American, Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Short form of Euphemia.
Eupham,f,,Scots (Archaic),,,,,,,,Reduced form of Euphemia.
Euphame,f,,Scottish,,,,,,,,Scottish form of Euphemia
Euphan,f,,Scottish (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Possibly a variant of Euphemia, which was also written as Euphame or Eupheme, a name that became common in Scotland because it was used as an Anglicization of Oighrig."
Euphane,f,,Scottish,,,,,,,,Form of Euphame
Euphantos,m,Εὔφαντος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ευ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with φαντός (phantos) meaning ""visible"". The latter element is ultimately derived from φαίνω (phaino) meaning ""to appear, reveal, make visible; to shine, give light""."
Euphantus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Euphantos.
Eupheba,f,,American (South),,,,,,,,Not available.
Euphegenia,f,,Popular Culture,/juː.fɪ.d͡ʒɪ.ˈnaɪ.ə/,,,,,,,"Probably a blend of Euphemia and Iphigenia. This was the name of the title character, played by Robin Williams, in the movie 'Mrs. Doubtfire' (1993)."
Euphelia,f,,English (Rare)|Literature,,,,,,,,Possibly a blend of Euphemia and Ophelia. Euphelia is the title of a poem by Helen Maria Williams (1759-1827).
Euphelma,f,,Bhutanese,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euphémé,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Eupheme.
Euphème,m,,French (Archaic),/ø.fɛm/,,,,,,,French form of Euphemios.
Eupheme,f,,Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Alternate form of Euphemia. The name of an Ancient Greek personification of praise, acclamation, and shouts of triumph. She was likely one of the younger Charites. A moon of Jupiter was named after her."
Euphemianus,m,,Late Roman|Late Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Roman cognomen which was derived from the Greek personal name Euphemios via its latinized form Euphemius. This name was borne by a female saint from the 9th century AD, who used this name as a pseudonym, in order to disguise herself as a male."
Euphémie,f,,"French (Rare)|French (Belgian, Rare)","/ø.fe.mi/(French, Belgian French)",,,,,,,French form of Euphemia.
Euphemie,f,,"English (Anglicized, Modern)",uy-FEE-mee,,,,,,,Anglicized form of Euphémie.
Euphemius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Euphemios.
Euphemon,m,,"Louisiana Creole|French (Rare, Archaic, ?)",,,,,,,,Masculine form of Euphemia.
Euphemos,m,Εὔφημος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/ěu̯.pʰɛː.mos/(Attic Greek)|/ˈeɸ.ɸi.mos/(Koine Greek)|/juː.ˈfiː.məs/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""reputable"" or ""uttering sounds of good omen"" in Greek, ultimately derived from εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and φήμη (pheme) meaning ""rumour, fame, reputation"" or ""prophetic voice, oracle""."
Euphemus,m,Εὔφημος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/juː.ˈfiː.məs/(English),,,,,,,Latinized form of Euphemos.
Euphemy,f,,English (Archaic)|Scottish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Archaic variant of Euphemia.
Euphenia,f,,Greek,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euphia,f,,American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euphie,f,,"English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Diminutive of Euphemia.
Euphonia,f,,English,,,,,,,,"From the name of the Euphonia bird, a tanager of the genus Tanagra."
Euphorbus,m,Εὔφορβος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/juː.ˈfoːɹ.bəs/(Classical Greek),,,,,,,"Euphorbus is a masculine name of ancient Greek origin. It derives from the Greek name Εὔφορβος (Euphorbos), formed from the elements εὐ-meaning ""good, well"" and φορβή meaning ""forage"" or ""food"". In Greek mythology, Euphorbus was a Trojan warrior who stabbed Patroclus in the back with a spear right before Hector killed Patroclus. Then Euphorbus was killed by Menelaus during the Trojan War shortly after, and his story is recounted in Homer’s Iliad. The name was later borne by the Greek physician Euphorbus, personal doctor to King Juba II of Mauretania, after whom the plant genus Euphorbia was named."
Euphoria,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)|Obscure",/juː.ˈfɔːɹ.i.ə/(English),,,,,,,"From the English word meaning ""feeling of intense happiness, state of joy"", originally a medical Latin term meaning ""condition of feeling healthy and comfortable (especially when sick)"". It comes from Greek εὐφορία (euphoria) ""power of enduring easily"", from εὔφορος (euphoros) ""bearing well, able to endure, patient"", ultimately from εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" and φέρω (phero) ""to bear"".This name debuted in the United States baby name data in 2007, when it was given to 6 girls born in the US. Use of the name has probably been influenced by the brand of perfume called Euphoria, which was introduced by Calvin Klein in 2005, and more recently by the American television show Euphoria which premiered in 2019 (19 girls born in the United States in 2021 were named Euphoria, and 16 in 2022)."
Euphorion,m,Εὐφορίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὔφορος (euphoros) meaning ""well borne, patiently borne; easy to bear, manageable"" as well as ""active, healthy, fertile, productive"" and ""patient, able to endure"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" and φέρω (phero) ""to bring, to carry, to bear"". In Greek mythology, Euphorion was a son of Achilles and Helen of Troy born in the Fields of the Blessed."
Euphra,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,Short form of Euphrasia used by the Scottish author George MacDonald in his novel David Elginbrod (1863).
Euphraïlde,f,,"French (Rare, Archaic)",/ø.fʁa.ild/,,,,,,,Not available.
Euphrase,m,,French (Rare),,,,,,,,French form of Euphrasius.
Euphrasien,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Euphrasine,f,,"French (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Diminutive of Euphrasie.
Euphrasios,m,"Εὐφράσιος, Εὐφρασίος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Euphrasia.
Euphrasiu,m,,Corsican,,,,,,,,Corsican form of Euphrasius.
Euphrasius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Euphrasios.
Euphratas,m,Εὐφράτας(Ancient Greek),Old Persian (Hellenized),,,,,,,,"Greek form of the Old Persian name *ʰUfrata meaning ""good and excellent""."
Euphrates,m,Εὐφράτης(Ancient Greek),Old Persian (Hellenized),,,,,,,,"Possibly taken from the name of the river Euphrates, the Greek form of Old Persian 𐎢𐎳𐎼𐎠𐎬𐎢 (*ʰUfrātuš⁠), which possibly derived from proto-Sumerian *burudu meaning ""copper""."
Euphroisine,f,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Euphrosine.
Euphron,m,Εὔφρων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ěu̯.pʰrɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/ˈeɸ.ɸron/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""cheerful, friendly, gracious"" or ""of sound mind"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ευ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and φρήν (phren) meaning ""mind, intellect, emotions"".This name was borne by a Greek tyrant from the 4th century BC, who ruled over the city-state of Sicyon."
Euphrône,m,,"French (Archaic)|French (Quebec, Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical)",,,,,,,,French form of Euphronios via its latinized form Euphronius.
Euphronios,m,Εὐφρόνιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and either φρόνις (phronis) meaning ""prudence, wisdom"" or φρονέω (phroneo) meaning ""to think"" as well as ""to be minded"". Both words are ultimately derived from the φρήν (phren) meaning ""mind, intellect, emotions"". Compare Euphron.This name was borne by an ancient Greek vase painter and potter who was active in Athens in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BC. Euphronios was one of the most important artists of the red-figure technique. His works place him at the transition from Late Archaic to Early Classical art."
Euphronius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Euphronios. A known bearer of this name is saint Euphronius, who was bishop of Tours (in France) from 555 AD to 573 AD."
Euphronous,m,,English (American),,,,,,,,Variant of Euphronios.
Euphrosène,f,,Norman,,,,,,,,Norman form of Euphrosyne.
Euphrosina,f,,German (East Prussian)|German (Archaic),"oy-froh-SEE-nah(East Prussian German, German)",,,,,,,German variant of Euphrosine and East Prussian German form of Euphrosyne.
Euphrosine,f,,French (Archaic)|German (Archaic),,,,,,,,French and German form of Euphrosyne.
Euphrosinia,f,,German (Bessarabian),,,,,,,,Variant of Euphrosina.
Euphy,f,,"English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Euphie.
Eupleios,m,Ευπλειος(Ancient Greek),"Late Greek (Hypothetical, ?)",,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἐύπλειος (eupleios) meaning ""well-filled"", which is derived from Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and Greek πλεῖος (pleios) or πλέως (pleos), which both mean ""full, filled"". As such, one could say that the name has a (more) symbolic meaning of ""abundant"" or ""complete"".This name is not to be confused with the names Euploos, Euplous and Euplos, which look similar but have an entirely different meaning."
Euplia,f,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Italian (Archaic)|English (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Euplius, which is the latinized form of the ancient Greek name Eupleios.Some sources state that Euplia comes from the ancient Greek name Euploia. If this is the case (which is likely in some instances), then the name is either a Roman corruption or contraction of Euploia, since the proper latinized form of the name is actually Euploea.Lastly, Euplia was the name of a Roman slave and prostitute, whose name was found in the records of the city of Pompeii. In ancient Rome, slaves usually had names of Greek origin."
Euplio,m,,"Italian|Spanish (Latin American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Euplius.
Euplius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Euplus, a Latinized form of Euplos. A known bearer of this name was Euplius of Catania, a martyr and saint from the 4th century AD."
Eupliusz,m,,Polish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Polish form of Euplius.
Euploia,f,"Εὔπλοια, Εὐπλοῖα(Ancient Greek)",Late Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὔπλοια (euploia) meaning ""good sailing, a fair voyage"", composed of εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and πλόος (ploos) meaning ""sailing, voyage"" (see Euploos). However, it should be noted that it can also mean ""good ships"", since πλοῖᾰ (ploia) is the plural form of Greek πλοῖον (ploion) meaning ""floating vessel, ship, boat"" (see Euploion).This was one of the epithets of the Greek goddess Aphrodite."
Euploion,m,Εὐπλοίων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Essentially means ""good ship"", derived from Greek ευ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with Greek πλοῖον (ploion) meaning ""floating vessel, ship, boat""."
Euploos,,Εὐπλόος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὔπλοος (euploos) meaning ""good sailing"" or ""a fair voyage"", composed of εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with πλόος (ploos) meaning ""sailing, voyage"". Also compare the names Euploia and Euploion."
Euplos,m,Εὖπλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant form of Euploos. This name is sometimes mistaken for a variant form of Eupleios.
Euplous,m,Εὔπλους(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Attic Greek form of Euploos.
Eupolemos,m,Εὐπόλεμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Biblical Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐπόλεμος (eupolemos) meaning ""good at war, successful in war"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek noun πόλεμος (polemos) meaning ""war, battle"". This name was borne by an Olympic victor from 396 BC."
Eupolemus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Biblical|Biblical Latin,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eupolemos. This name was borne by a Greek athlete and a Greek general, both of whom lived in the 4th century BC."
Eupolis,m,Εὔπολις(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun εὔπολις (eupolis) meaning ""abounding in cities"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek noun πόλις (polis) meaning ""city"".This name was borne by an Athenian comic poet from the 5th century BC."
Eupompos,m,Εὐπόμπος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὔπομπος (eupompos) meaning ""well-conducting"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek verb πομπεύω (pompeuo) meaning ""to conduct, to escort"". Also compare the Greek noun πομπός (pompos) meaning ""guide, escort, conductor""."
Eupompus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eupompos. This was the name of a Greek painter from the 4th century BC.
Euporia,f,Εὐπορία(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""prosperity, plenty, abundance"" in Ancient Greek, derived from εὔπορος (euporos) meaning ""easy to pass or travel through, easily done"", ""ready, resourceful, inventive"" or ""well-provided with"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""good"" and πόρος (poros) ""passageway, opening, means to an end, journey""."
Euporie,f,,Greek Mythology (?)|Astronomy,yoo-PAWR-ee(Astronomy),,,,,,,"A form of Euporia. A moon of Jupiter bears this name in honour of Euporia or Euporie, the Greek goddess of abundance and one of the third generation of Horai."
Eupraksiya,f,Еўпраксія(Belarusian),Belarusian,,,,,,,,Belarusian form of Eupraxia
Eupraxis,,"Εὔπραξις, Εὐπραξίς, Εὐπραξις(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant of Eupraxia.
Euprepio,m,,Italian (Rare)|Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,"Italian and Spanish form of Euprepius, the Latinized form of Greek Euprepios."
Euprepios,m,Εὐπρέπιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Late Greek|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek εὐπρεπής (euprepes) meaning ""good-looking, comely, distinguished"" as well as ""decent, fitting, propriety"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""well, good"" and πρέπω (prepo) ""to be clearly seen, visible; to be conspicuously fitting, to resemble""."
Euprepius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Euprepios
Eupsichio,m,,Italian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Italian form of Eupsychius.
Eupsihie,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Romanian form of Eupsychius.
Eupsiqui,m,,Catalan (Archaic),,,,,,,,Catalan form of Eupsychius.
Eupsíquio,m,,Portuguese (Archaic),,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Eupsychius.
Eupsiquio,m,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Spanish form of Eupsychius.
Eupsychia,f,Εὐψυχία(Ancient Greek),Late Greek|Polish (Rare),ehw-PSI-khya(Polish),,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun εὐψυχία (eupsychia) meaning ""good courage, high spirit"". It ultimately consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek noun ψυχή (psyche) meaning ""spirit, soul, mind"" (also see Psyche)."
Eupsychios,m,Εὐψύχιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Derived from either the Greek noun εὐψυχία (eupsychia) meaning ""good courage, high spirit"" (see Eupsychia) or the Greek adjective εὔψυχος (eupsychos) meaning ""of good courage, stout of heart"" (see Eupsychos).This was the name of a 4th-century saint from Asia Minor, who is better known as Eupsychius."
Eupsychius,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eupsychios. This name was notably borne by Eupsychius of Caesaria, a 4th-century Christian martyr and saint from Asia Minor. He is venerated in both the Catholic Church and the Eastern Orthodox Church."
Eupsychiusz,m,,Polish (Rare),ehw-PSI-khyoosh,,,,,,,Polish form of Eupsychius.
Eupsychos,m,Εὔψυχος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὔψυχος (eupsychos) meaning ""of good courage, stout of heart"". It consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek noun ψυχή (psyche) meaning ""spirit, soul, mind"" (also see Psyche)."
Eupsyque,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,French form of Eupsychius.
Euquerio,m,,Spanish (Archaic),eh-oo-KEH-ṙyo,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek eucherios, meaning ""skilled""."
Eur,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eura,f,,"American (South, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Possibly a short form of names beginning with the element Eur-.
Eural,m,,African American,,,,,,,,Possible variant of Earl.
Eurania,f,,Romani (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Urania.
Eurasia,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",yuw-RAY-zhə,,,,,,,From the name of the continent Eurasia.
Eurddolen,f,,Welsh,ayr-DHO-len|ier-DHO-len,,,,,,,"Means ""golden ring"", derived from the Welsh elements aur ""gold"" and dolen ""ring"". It is sometimes interpreted as the Welsh form of Goldilocks (""golden ringlets, curls"")."
Eureka,f,,"English (American, Rare)|African American (Rare)|English (African, Rare)",[jʊ.ˈɹiː.kə](American English),,,,,,,"From the Ancient Greek word εὕρηκα (heureka) meaning ""I have found (it)"". In modern English, eureka is an interjection used to celebrate a discovery or invention, and it is originally attributed to Ancient Greek mathematician Archimedes."
Eurélia,f,,"Portuguese (Brazilian)|French (Quebec, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Cognate of Eurelia.
Eurelia,f,,"Norwegian (Archaic)|Estonian (Rare, Archaic)|French (Quebec, Archaic)|American (South, Archaic)|Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Aurelia.
Eurem,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Means ""golden jewel"" in Welsh."
Euremar,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euréné,m,,Louisiana Creole (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Irénée.
Eurfon,,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Possibly from Welsh aur ""gold"" combined with Môn, the name of an island in Wales."
Eurfron,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Means ""golden-breasted"", derived from Welsh aur ""gold"" and bron ""breast""."
Eurfryn,m,,Welsh,[ˈeir.vrɪn],,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh eur, the penult form of aur, ""gold"" and bryn ""hill""."
Eurfyl,m,,Welsh,[ˈeir.vɪl],,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh aur meaning ""gold""."
Eurgain,f,,Welsh|Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh aur ""gold"" (penult form eur) and cain ""fair; fine; elegant"". In Welsh mythology, Eurgain is noted as the first female saint and daughter of Caratacus (see Caradog) in the History of Dunraven Manuscript, a manuscript giving the genealogy of Taliesin."
Euri,m,,Romani (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Uriah.
Euria,f,,"Basque (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Derived from Basque euri ""rain""."
Euriah,,,African American|Filipino,yuw-RIE-ə(African American),,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Uriah.
Eurialo,m,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Euryalus.
Euriamis,m,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"Uknown meaning, possibly a combination of two other names. Euriamis Losada is a Cuban-American actor."
Eurich,m,,German,,,,,,,,German form of Euric.
Eurícide,f,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Spanish form of Eurycyda.
Eurides,,,Portuguese (Brazilian),,,,,,,,Variant of Eurídice.
Eurídice,f,,Catalan|Spanish|Portuguese,,,,,,,,"Catalan, Spanish and Portuguese form of Eurydice."
Euridice,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Eurydice.
Euridici,f,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Eurydice.
Euridicio,m,,Spanish|Italian,,,,,,,,Spanish and Italian masculine form of Eurydice.
Euridiké,f,,Hungarian,,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Eurydice.
Eurìel,m,,Emilian-Romagnol,,,,,,,,Emilian form of Euryalus.
Euriel,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Uriell.
Eurielle,f,,"History (Ecclesiastical)|French (Modern)|French (Belgian, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Urielle.
Eurik,m,,Croatian|Dutch|Frisian|Norwegian (Archaic)|Swedish (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Croatian, Dutch, Frisian, Norwegian and Swedish form of Euric. In Swedish and Norwegian, the name is not used outside of translations of historical documents about the 5th century king of the Visigoths."
Eurilla,f,,"American (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Eurion,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"From the Welsh aur meaning, ""gold."""
Euripid,m,,Bosnian|Croatian,,,,,,,,Bosnian and Croatian form of Euripides.
Euripide,m,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Euripides.
Euripidese,m,,Estonian,,,,,,,,Estonian form of Euripides.
Euripīds,m,,Latvian,,,,,,,,Variant of Eiripīds.
Euristeo,m,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Eurystheus.
Euristeu,m,,Catalan|Portuguese,,,,,,,,Catalan and Portuguese form of Eurystheus.
Eurliw,,,Welsh,Eir-liu,,,,,,,"Welsh name, meaning ""golden colored""."
Eurlyne,f,,"English (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Earline or Arline.
Euro,m,,Italian|Spanish (Latin American)|Portuguese,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euron,m,,Medieval Welsh (Anglicized),UU-ṘON,,,,,,,"Form Welsh Eur meaning ""gold"", from Latin Aur meaning ""gold""."
Europs,m,Εὔρωψ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Masculine form of Europe, derived from Greek εὐρύς (eurys) ""wide"" and ὄψ (ops) ""face, eye"". This was the name of two characters in Greek mythology."
Europus,m,Εὐρωπός(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Εὐρωπός (Europos), a masculine form of Europa. In Greek mythology, Europus was a son of Makedon and Oreithyia."
Euros,m,Εὖρος(Ancient Greek),Welsh|Greek Mythology,[ˈɛɨ̯.rɔs]|[ˈəi.rɔs],,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh aur meaning ""gold"", perhaps originally a short form of Euroswydd (itself probably derived from aur and oswydd ""enemy"")."
Eurosia,f,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,"A famous bearer was Eurosia Fabris, also known as Mamma Rosa, who was beatified in 2005."
Euroswydd,m,,Welsh Mythology,ayr-OS-widh,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh aur ""gold"" and oswydd ""enemies, adversaries; spears"". This is the name of a figure from Welsh mythology, the father of Nisien and Efnysien."
Eurotas,m,Εὐρώτας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/eu̯.rɔ̌ː.taːs/(Attic Greek)|/e.ˈβro.tas/(Koine Greek)|/e.ˈvro.tas/(Byzantine Greek)|/jʊə.ˈroʊ.təs/(English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek εὐρώς (euros) meaning ""mould, mildew, decay"", or from εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"". This was the name of a king of Laconia in Greek mythology, son of Myles and father of Sparta and Tiasa, as well as of a (possibly eponymous) Laconian river."
Eursula,f,,"English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Ursula.
Eurus,m,Εὖρος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˈjʊɹ.əs/,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Euros. Eurus was the god of the east wind, one of the four directional Anemoi (Wind-Gods). He was associated with the season of autumn and dwelt near the palace of the sun-god Helios in the far east."
Eurwyn,m,,Welsh,[ˈeir.wɪn],,,,,,,Masculine form of Eurwen.
Euryanassa,f,Εὐρυάνασσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Euryanax. This is the name of two characters from Greek mythology.
Euryanax,m,Εὐρυάναξ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" and ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king"". This was the name of a Spartan general from the 5th century BC, who was the son of the Spartan prince Dorieus."
Eurybates,m,Εὐρυβάτης(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύβατος (eurybatos) meaning ""wide-ranging, wide-roaming, far-stepping"", composed of εὐρύς (eurys) ""wide, broad"" and βαίνω (baino) ""to go, step, walk""."
Eurybatos,m,Εὐρύβατος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/eu̯.rý.ba.tos/(Attic Greek)|/e.ˈβry.βa.tos/(Koine Greek)|/e.ˈvry.va.tos/(Byzantine Greek)|/e.ˈvri.va.tos/(Constantinopolitan Greek)|/jə.ˈrɪ.bət.əs/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""wide-stepping, expansive"" in Greek, ultimately derived from εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide"" and βατός (batos) meaning ""speeding"" or ""passable, accessible""."
Eurybia,f,Εὐρυβία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""of wide power, with far-reaching might"" from the Greek elements εὐρύς (eurys) ""wide"" and βία (bia) ""force"", with all of its connotations. This was the name of the ancient Greek goddess of the mastery of the sea; she did not so much preside over the whole, but rather the external influences such as seasonal weather, the power of the winds and the rise of constellations which were believed to influence it. Hesiod described Eurybia as 'having a heart of flint within her'."
Eurybiades,m,Εὐρυβιάδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/eu̯.ry.bi.á.dɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/e.βry.βi.ˈa.ðis/(Koine Greek)|/e.vry.vi.ˈa.ðis/(Byzantine Greek)|/e.vri.vi.ˈa.ðis/(Constantinopolitan Greek)|/ˌjʊə.rɪ.ˈbaɪ.ə.diːz/(English),,,,,,,Patronymic form of the Greek name Εὐρύβιος (see Eurybius).
Eurybie,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Eurybia.
Eurybius,m,Εὐρύβιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" and βίος (bios) meaning ""life""."
Euryclea,f,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurykleia. In Greek mythology, Euryclea was the wet nurse of Odysseus' son Telemachus. She was the first to recognize Odysseus when he returned to Ithaca after the Trojan War."
Eurycleia,f,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Variant latinization of Eurykleia, because its proper (and most common) latinized form is Euryclea.In Greek mythology, Eurycleia was the wet nurse of Odysseus' son Telemachus. She was the first to recognize Odysseus when he returned to Ithaca after the Trojan War."
Eurycles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eurykles. Known bearers of this name include the Olympic victor Eurycles of Laconia (6th century BC) and the Spartan ruler Gaius Julius Eurycles (1st century BC).
Eurycrates,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eurykrates. This name was borne by a Spartan king from the 7th century BC.
Eurycratidas,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eurykratidas.
Eurycratides,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurykratides.This is the name by which a Spartan king from the 7th century BC is usually known. His actual name was Eurykratidas, of which the proper latinized form is Eurycratidas."
Eurycyda,f,Εὐρυκύδα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""broad"" and κῦδος (kydos) meaning ""glory, renown"". This was the name of an Elean princess in Greek mythology, the daughter of King Endymion of Elis."
Eurydikos,m,Εὐρύδικος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Eurydike (see Eurydice).
Eurydome,f,Ευρυδόμη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/jʊ.ˈrɪ.də.miː/(English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" and either δόμος (domos) meaning ""house, home, household"" or δόμα (doma) meaning ""gift"" as well as ""house"". Eurydome is sometimes named as a mother of the Charites or Graces in Greek mythology. A moon of Jupiter is named after her."
Euryganeia,f,Εὐρυγάνεια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide"" and an uncertain second element, possibly Greek γάνος (ganos) meaning ""brightness"". In some Greek myths this name belongs to the second wife of Oedipus, after Jocasta commits suicide upon discovery of her accidental incest."
Euryk,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Euric.
Eurykleia,f,Εὐρύκλεια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Eurykles. In Greek mythology, Eurykleia was the wet nurse of Odysseus' son Telemachus. She was the first to recognize Odysseus when he returned to Ithaca after the Trojan War."
Eurykles,m,Εὐρυκλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Eurykrates,m,Εὐρυκράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/eu̯.ry.krá.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/e.βry.ˈkra.tis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with κράτος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength""."
Eurykratidas,m,Εὐρυκρατίδας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Aeolic and Doric Greek form of Eurykratides, because it contains ίδας (idas), which is the Aeolic and Doric Greek form of the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This name was notably borne by a Spartan king from the 7th century BC. Contrary to most other Spartan kings, he is not typically known under the latinized form of his native name, which in his case would be Eurycratidas. Instead he is usually known as Eurycratides, which is a latinization of the aforementioned Eurykratides, an Attic Greek name by origin."
Eurykratides,m,Εὐρυκρατίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Eurykrates"" in Greek, derived from the name Eurykrates combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Euryleon,m,Εὐρυλέων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun λέων (leon) meaning ""lion"". This name was borne by a Spartan tyrant of Selinus, who lived in the 6th century BC.In Greek mythology, Euryleon was the original name of king Ascanius of Alba Longa."
Euryleonis,f,Εὐρυλεωνίς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Euryleon. This was the name of a celebrated Olympic charioteer from Sparta, who lived in the 4th century BC."
Eurylochos,m,Εὐρύλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Eurylochus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurylochos. This name was borne by a Thessalian general from the 6th century BC and by a Spartan general from the 5th century BC.In Greek mythology, Eurylochus was the second-in-command of Odysseus' ship during the return to Ithaca after the Trojan War."
Eurymachos,m,Εὐρύμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle""."
Eurymachus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurymachos. In Greek mythology, this is the name of an Ithacan nobleman, who is one of the suitors of Penelope."
Eurymede,f,Εὐρυμήδη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Eurymedes. In Greek mythology, this is the name of one of the daughters of king Oeneus of Calydon."
Eurymedes,m,Εὐρυμήδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek verb μήδομαι (medomai) meaning ""to think, to plan, to plot""."
Eurymedon,m,Εὐρυμέδων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Late Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""widely ruling"" in Ancient Greek, derived from εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with μέδων (medon) meaning ""ruler"" (see medo). This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including the king of the Gigantes, and a son of Dionysus and Ariadne who joined the Argonauts. It was also an epithet of Poseidon, Hermes, and Perseus.A known bearer of this name is Eurymedon of Myrrhinus, the brother-in-law of the Athenian philosopher Plato (5th century BC)."
Eurymedusa,f,Εὐρυμέδουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Most likely a feminine form of Eurymedon meaning ""wide-ruling"", derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) ""wide, broad"" and μέδω (medo) ""to protect, to rule over"". This was the name of several women in Greek mythology."
Eurymenes,m,Εὐρυμένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρυμενής (eurymenes) meaning ""broad and strong"", which consists of the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun μένος (menos) meaning ""mind"" as well as ""spirit"" and ""power, strength, force"".A known bearer of this name is Eurymenes of Samos, an Olympic victor from the 6th century BC. In Greek mythology, Eurymenes is the son of king Neleus of Pylos."
Eurymine,f,,Theatre,,,,,,,,"The heroine of 'The Maid's Metamorphosis' a late Elizabethan stage play, a pastoral first published in 1600. The play was published anonymously, and its authorship has been a long-standing point of dispute among scholars."
Euryn,m,,Cornish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Eurynomos,m,Εὐρύνομος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with either the Greek noun νομός (nomos) meaning ""pasture, field"" or the Greek noun νόμος (nomos) meaning ""usage, custom, law, ordinance"" (see Eunomia).In Greek mythology, Eurynomos is a daemon (spirit) of the underworld, who eats off all the flesh of the corpses, leaving only their bones."
Eurynomus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurynomos. In Greek mythology, this is the name of one of the suitors of Penelope."
Euryphaessa,f,Εὐρυφάεσσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""far-shining"", derived from Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide"" and φάος (phaos) ""light"". This was an epithet of the Titan goddess Theia, occurring in one of the Homeric Hymns (namely Hymn 31, ""To Helios"", where Helios' mother Theia is called ""mild-eyed Euryphaessa, the far-shining one"")."
Euryphon,m,Εὐρυφῶν(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun φωνή (phone) meaning ""voice"" as well as ""sound, tone"".This was the name of a celebrated ancient Greek physician from Cnidos, who is thought to have lived in the 5th century BC."
Eurypon,m,Εὐρυπῶν(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"This name was most notably borne by a king of Sparta, after whom the Eurypontid dynasty was named. It is not quite clear whether he is a historical person, or whether he is fully mythological. If the former applies, then he is thought to have lived in the 9th century BC.His name should be a compound name, of which the first element is derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"". The second element of the name is uncertain. It might possibly be derived from the Greek verb πονέω (poneo) meaning ""to toil, to labor, to work hard"". An other possibility might be πώνω (pono), which is the Aeolic and Doric Greek form of the verb πίνω (pino) meaning ""to drink"" as well as ""to carouse"".Lastly, this name is not to be confused with the very similar name Euryphon."
Euryptolemos,m,Εὐρυπτόλεμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Epic Greek noun πτόλεμος (ptolemos) meaning ""war""."
Euryptolemus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Euryptolemos. This was the name of one of the Alcmaeonidae, a powerful noble family of ancient Athens."
Eurypyle,f,Εὐρυπύλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with πύλη (pyle) meaning ""gate, entrance"". This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including an Amazon queen, a maenad, and one of the fifty daughters of King Thespius, mother of Archedikos by Heracles."
Eurypylos,m,Εὐρύπυλος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun πύλη (pyle) meaning ""gate, entrance""."
Eurypylus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurypylos. This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of which is a Thessalian king."
Eurysaces,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eurysakes. As a Roman name it's a cognomen.
Eurysakes,m,Εὐρυσάκης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/eu̯.ry.sá.kɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/e.βry.ˈsa.cis/(Koine Greek)|/e.vry.ˈsa.cis/(Byzantine Greek)|/e.vri.ˈsa.cis/(Constantinopolitan Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""broad shield"", derived from Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide"" and σάκος (sakos) meaning ""shield"". This was the name of a son of Ajax the Great, named after his father’s shield."
Eurysthée,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Eurystheus.
Eurysthenes,m,Εὐρυσθένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""widely powerful, of far-reaching might"", derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" and σθένος (sthenos) meaning ""vigour, strength"". This name was borne by a Spartan king from the 10th century BC. In Greek mythology, Eurysthenes was one of the sons of King Aegyptus of Egypt listed by Hyginus."
Eurystratos,m,Εὐρύστρατος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with the Greek noun στρατός (stratos) meaning ""army""."
Eurystratus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Eurystratos. In Greek mythology, this is the name of one of the suitors of Penelope."
Euryte,f,Εὐρύτη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈjʊər.ɪ.tiː/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύτης (eurytês) meaning ""width, breadth"" (see eurys). This was the name of multiple minor characters in Greek mythology."
Eurythemis,f,Εὐρύθεμις(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" combined with θέμις (themis) meaning ""law of nature, divinely ordained justice, that which is laid down"" (see Themis). This is the name of several characters from Greek mythology, one of whom is the wife of King Thestius of Pleuron according to Apollodorus."
Eurythemiste,f,Εὐρυθεμίστη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/jʊə.rɪ.θɪ.ˈmɪs.tiː/(English),,,,,,,"Probably a variant form of Eurythemis. In Greek mythology, Eurythemiste was one of two noble maidens being considered for marriage by Boeotus, and was chosen when a star fell and landed on her shoulder. It was also the name of a naiad-nymph daughter of the river god Xanthus, and an alternative name for Laophonte."
Eurythion,m,Εὐρυθίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/jʊə.ˈrɪθ.i.ən/(English),,,,,,,Variant of Eurytion.
Eurytimos,m,"Εὐρυτίμος, Εὐρύτιμος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,/eu̯.rý.tiː.mos/(Attic Greek)|/e.ˈβry.ti.mos/(Koine Greek)|/e.ˈvry.ti.mos/(Byzantine Greek)|/e.ˈvri.ti.mos/(Constantinopolitan Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""widely honoured"" in Greek, derived from εὐρύς (eurys) meaning ""wide, broad"" and τιμάω (timao) meaning ""to honour, esteem, revere""."
Eurytimus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eurytimos.
Eurytion,m,Εὐρυτίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/jʊə.ˈrɪ.ʃən/(English),,,,,,,"From the Ancient Greek name Εὔρυτος (see Eurytus). This name was borne by at least four characters in Greek mythology, one of them being a son of Ares and two others being centaurs."
Eurytus,m,Εὔρυτος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/ˈjʊə.rɪ.təs/(English)|/ěu̯.ry.tos/(Attic Greek)|/ˈe.βry.tos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Εὔρυτος (Eurytos) which may have been derived from Ancient Greek εὔρυτος (eurytos) meaning ""well-flowing, abundant water"", from εὖ (eu) ""well"" and ῥέω (rheo) ""to flow, stream, pour"" (see rhoe) or the related ῥυτός (rhytos) meaning ""flowing, liquid"" or ""drinking horn"". Alternatively, the second element could instead derive from ἐρύω (eryo) ""to draw, drag, pull"", perhaps with the implied meaning ""drawer of the bow"". This was the name of numerous characters in Greek mythology, including a king of Oechalia who was the father of Iole and Iphitus. He was a celebrated archer, and in some versions of the legends he challenged Apollo to a contest and was slain by the god for his hubris. The bow of Eurytus passed to his son, who later gave it to Odysseus.It was also the name of a centaur, a son of Hermes, one of the Gigantes, and a Greek leader in the Trojan war."
Eus,m,,Dutch (Rare),,,,,,,,"Contracted form of Eugenius as well as a short form of latinized Greek names that start with Eus-, such as Eusebius and Eustachius."
Eusamar,f,,"Brazilian (Rare, ?)",êu za mar,,,,,,,Not available.
