Flordespina,f,,Carolingian Cycle|Literature,,,,,,,,"This name is borne by a character in Francisco de Barahona's Flor de caballerías (1599). The name is thought to be derived from Spanish flor de espina ""thorn flower; hawthorn flower"". It is also used in English translations of Ludovico Ariosto's Orlando furioso (1516), while the name appears as Fiordispina in the Italian original version."
Flordiana,f,,Medieval Italian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Flordiane,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"The sister of Duke Eskilabon of Belamunt.All knights who desired her had to contend with Eskilabon and were invariably defeated and imprisoned – until Arthur’s Sir Garel conquered Eskilabon and ended the custom.Garel seemed to engage himself to Flordiane, but he later married Queen Laudamie of Averre."
Flordibel,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Heroine of Der Pleier’s Tandareis and Flordibel. The daughter of the King of India, she was sent to Arthur’s court as a child to serve Guenevere."
Floréal,m,,French,[flɔ.ʁe.al],,,,,,,"Derived from the name of the eighth month in the French Republican calendar. The month was named after the Latin word floreus, meaning ""flowery""."
Floreal,m,,Spanish (Rare),/flo.ɾe.ˈal/,,,,,,,"Spanish form of Floréal. This name was brought to public attention by the novel 'Sembrando Flores' (1906) by Catalan anarchist Juan Montseny Carret, whose main characters are named Floreal and Armonía, and thus it came to be used by anarchist parents who were eager to reject traditional names during the Second Spanish Republic (1931-1939)."
Florean,m,,Dutch (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Floreana,f,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Originally the name of one of the Galapagos Islands. The island was named Floreana because of the ecuadorian president Juan Jose Flores.
Floreanita,f,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Floreana.
Floreat,f,,"English (Rare, Archaic)",/ˈflɔɹ.i.ət/,,,,,,,"Means ""let (it) flourish, may (it) prosper, long live"" in Latin. This is often used as a motto, or as part of a motto, which may help explain its use as a personal name; for example, a common scholastic motto is floreat nostra schola meaning ""may our school flourish"". Also compare the Latin phrases vivat crescat floreat ""may it live, grow, (and) flourish"" and semper floreat ""may it always flourish""."
Florecia,f,,Spanish (Latin American),,,,,,,,Not available.
Florecita,f,,Spanish (Philippines)|Spanish (Mexican),flo-reh-SEE-ta(Spanish),,,,,,,"Spanish diminutive of Flor, formed using the diminutive suffix‎ -cita."
Florecota,f,,Judeo-Anglo-Norman,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Flora and Floria.
Floreczka,f,,Polish,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Flora.
Florée,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A cousin and friend of Princess Hermondine of Scotland.She organized five tournaments to find Hermondine a husband, which produced Arthur’s Sir Meliador. Agravain wooed and won her during a tournament at Camelot."
Floree,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A lady kidnapped by Sir Caradoc of the Dolorous Tower. Caradoc also kidnapped Guinevere.When Gawaine came to rescue Guenevere, Floree gave him Caradoc’s sword – the only weapon which could kill the giant knight. Caradoc died cursing her name."
Floreen,f,,English (Rare),flawr-EEN,,,,,,,"This name can be a variant form of Florine (compare names like Pauleen and Rosaleen) as well as a combination of Flora with any name ending in -een, such as Kathleen and Maureen."
Floreine,f,,English (Rare),FLOR-een,,,,,,,Variant of Florine.
